      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 11 and 21. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 11 by 21. Let me think about how to do that step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 21 is the same as 20 plus 1. So if I multiply 11 by 20 and then add 11 times 1, that should work. Let me write that out:\n\n11 \u00d7 21 = 11 \u00d7 (20 + 1) = (11 \u00d7 20) + (11 \u00d7 1)\n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. 11 times 20. Well, 10 times 20 is 200, and 1 times 20 is 20, so adding those together gives 220. Then 11 times 1 is just 11. So adding those two results: 220 + 11. Let me check that. 220 plus 10 is 230, and then plus 1 more is 231. So does that mean the product is 231?\n\nWait, let me verify another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  21\n\u00d7 11\n-----\nFirst, multiply 21 by 1, which is 21. Then multiply 21 by the tens place, which is 20. So 21 times 20. Hmm, 20 times 20 is 400, and 20 times 1 is 20, so 400 + 20 = 420. Then add that to the previous result. Wait, no, hold on. Maybe I confused the places here.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying 21 by 11, it's like 21 multiplied by (10 + 1). So the first step is 21 \u00d7 1 = 21, then 21 \u00d7 10 = 210. Then add those two together: 21 + 210. That's 231. Oh, okay, that matches what I did before. So that's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use addition. 11 added 21 times. Let's see, 11 + 21 is 32. If I add another 21, that's 32 + 21 = 53. Then another 21: 53 + 21 = 74. Then 74 + 21 = 95. Wait, that's 11 added 21 times? Wait, no. Wait, 11 multiplied by 21 is 11 added 21 times? Wait, no, that's not right. Multiplication isn't repeated addition unless it's area. Wait, actually, no. If you have 11 \u00d7 21, it's 11 multiplied by 21, not adding 11 twenty-one times. So that approach was wrong. I need to stop confusing multiplication with repeated addition.\n\nSo maybe better to stick with the multiplication methods. Let's try another way. Let's do 11 \u00d7 21. Let's think of 11 as 10 + 1 again. So 10 \u00d7 21 = 210, and 1 \u00d7 21 = 21. Then 210 + 21 = 231. Yep, same result. So that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property in another way. For example, 21 can be written as 20 + 1, which we already did. Or 21 can be written as 10 + 10 + 1, but that might complicate things. Let's see: 11 \u00d7 (10 + 10 + 1) = 11\u00d710 + 11\u00d710 + 11\u00d71 = 110 + 110 + 11 = 231. Still the same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 21 is 3 times 7. So 11 \u00d7 21 = 11 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 7. Let's compute 11 \u00d7 3 first. 11 \u00d7 3 is 33. Then 33 \u00d7 7. Hmm, 30 \u00d7 7 is 210, and 3 \u00d7 7 is 21, so 210 + 21 = 231. Again, same result. So that works too.\n\nWait, so all different methods are leading me to 231. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. Let me just check with a calculator to be absolutely sure. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can do another check. For example, 231 divided by 11 should be 21. Let's see: 11 \u00d7 20 = 220, 231 - 220 = 11. So 20 + 1 = 21. Yep, that works. So 11 \u00d7 21 = 231, and 231 \u00f7 11 = 21. Perfect.\n\nAlternatively, 21 \u00d7 11. Let's reverse the numbers. 21 is the same as 11, so it's commutative. So 21 \u00d7 11 is the same as 11 \u00d7 21. So that's 231. Alright.\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods confirm that the product is 231. So I can be confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 11 and 21 is \\boxed{231}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 11 and 21, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n11 \\times 21 = 11 \\times (20 + 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute each part separately:\n\\[\n11 \\times 20 = 220\n\\]\n\\[\n11 \\times 1 = 11\n\\]\n\nThen, add the results together:\n\\[\n220 + 11 = 231\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another 