anding (a + b)(a - b) is a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. So it's 65\u00b2 - 2\u00b2. But when I do the standard multiplication, I get 4355. So 65\u00b2 is 4225, minus 4 is 4221. But 4355 is different. So clearly, that method isn't working here. Maybe I confused the formula. Wait, no, the formula is correct. Wait, but 67*65 is 4355, but according to the formula, it's 65\u00b2 - 2\u00b2 = 4225 - 4 = 4221. That's a problem. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the expansion. Let me check again. (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. So if a is 65 and b is 2, then (65 + 2)(65 - 2) = 65\u00b2 - 2\u00b2 = 4225 - 4 = 4221. But when I multiplied 67*65 directly, I got 4355. So there's a conflict here. That means either my calculation is wrong, or the formula is not applicable here. Wait, but the formula is a general algebraic identity. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 67*65 is not (65 + 2)(65 - 2). Wait, 67 is 65 + 2, but 65 - 2 is 63. So (63)(67) would be different. Wait, no. Wait, 67 is 65 + 2, so (65 + 2)(65 - 2) is 65\u00b2 - 2\u00b2. But 65*65 is 4225, minus 4 is 4221. But 67*65 is 4355. So that's not matching. Therefore, my application of the formula is wrong. Wait, but why?\n\nWait, no, actually, (a + b)(a - b) is a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. So if a is 65 and b is 2, then (65 + 2)(65 - 2) = 65\u00b2 - 2\u00b2. But 65 + 2 is 67, and 65 - 2 is 63. So (63)(67) would be 63*67. Wait, so I think I messed up here. The formula is (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. So if I have (65 + 2)(65 - 2) = 65\u00b2 - 2\u00b2. But 65\u00b2 is 4225, minus 4 is 4221. But 63*67 is 4355. So that's not the same. Therefore, the formula is not applicable here. Wait, but 67*65 is 67*65, which is 4355, but (65 + 2)(65 - 2) is 63*67, which is different. So my mistake was thinking that (65 + 2)(65 - 2) is 67*65, which is incorrect. So that formula is not applicable here. Therefore, the first method where I expanded (60 + 7)(60 + 5) is correct, giving 4355, and the second method using the difference of squares was incorrect because I misapplied the formula. So the correct answer is 4355.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check with another method. Let's do the standard multiplication:\n\n     67\n   x 65\n   ----\n     335  (67 x 5)\n   4020   (67 x 60)\n   ----\n   4355\n\nYes, adding 335 and 4020 gives 4355. So that's correct. Therefore, the product of 67 and 65 is 4355. So the first method I tried was correct, but I messed up the second method by misapplying the difference of squares formula. So the key takeaway is that sometimes when you see something like (a + b)(a - b), you have to be careful about what a and b are. In this case, if you take a = 65 and b = 2, then (a + b)(a - b) is 67*63, not 67*65. So that was my mistake. Therefore, the correct answer is 4355.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 65 is \\boxed{4355}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 65, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 65 = (60 + 7)(60 + 5)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(60 + 7)(60 + 5) = 60 \\times 60 + 60 \\times 5 + 7 \\times 60 + 7 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term step by step:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 60 = 3600\n\\]\n\\[\n60 \\times 5 = 300\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 60 = 420\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 5 = 35\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n3600 + 300 + 420 + 35 = 3600 + 300 = 3900\n\\]\n\\[\n3900 + 420 = 4320\n\\]\n\\[\n4320 + 35 = 4355\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can also use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 67 \\\\\n\\times 65 \\\\\n\\hline\n 335 \\quad \\text{(67 multiplied by 5)} \\\\\n4020 \\quad \\text{(67 multiplied by 60)} \\\\\n\\hline\n4355 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the results of the partial products:\n\n\\[\n335 + 4020 = 4355\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 67 and 65 is \\(\\boxed{4355}\\).",
