      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 77 and 93. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think... 77 times 93. Hmm, 77 is close to 70, and 93 is close to 90. Maybe I can adjust for that.\n\nWait, another way is to think of 77 as 70 + 7 and 93 as 90 + 3. Then use the distributive property: (70 + 7)(90 + 3). Let me expand that. So, 70 times 90 is 6300. Then 70 times 3 is 210. Next, 7 times 90 is 630. And 7 times 3 is 21. Now add all those up: 6300 + 210 + 630 + 21. Let me add them step by step. 6300 + 210 is 6510. Then 6510 + 630 is 7140. Then 7140 + 21 is 7161. So, is the product 7161? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. Multiply 77 by 93. Starting from the right, 77 times 3 is 231. Then 77 times 90 is 6930. Wait, but that's not right. Wait, no, when multiplying by 93, it's 93 times 77. So, the way standard multiplication works is:\n\nFirst, multiply 77 by 3 (units place of 93), which is 231. Then multiply 77 by 90 (tens place of 93), which is 6930. Then add them together: 231 + 6930. Let's add those. 231 + 6930. 200 + 6900 is 7100. 31 + 30 is 61. So total is 7161. Okay, same result. So that seems to confirm it.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method. Maybe using prime factors. Let's see. 77 is 7 times 11. 93 is 3 times 31. So, multiplying them together: 7 * 11 * 3 * 31. Let's rearrange the factors to make it easier: 7 * 3 = 21, 11 * 31 = 341. Then 21 * 341. Hmm, 21 times 300 is 6300, 21 times 40 is 840, 21 times 1 is 21. Adding those: 6300 + 840 = 7140, plus 21 is 7161. Again, same answer. So that's three different methods, all leading to 7161. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use estimation to check. 77 is approximately 80, and 93 is approximately 90. 80 times 90 is 7200. Since 77 is a bit less than 80 and 93 is a bit more than 90, the actual product should be a bit less than 7200. 7161 is indeed 39 less than 7200, which makes sense because 80 is 10 more than 77, and 90 is 3 less than 93. So the differences would multiply to (10)(-3) = -30, but since 77 is 10 less and 93 is 3 more, the total difference is (10)(90) - (3)(77) = 900 - 231 = 669. Wait, maybe that's not the right way. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the estimation method: when you have two numbers, one increased by 10 and the other decreased by 3, the total change is approximately (10)(90) - (3)(77) = 900 - 231 = 669. So the original estimate was 7200, subtract 669 gives 7200 - 669 = 6531? Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe I confused the formula. Let me recall: if you have two numbers, a and b, and you approximate them as (a + x) and (b - y), then the product is approximately ab - xy. So in this case, a = 77, b = 93. So x = -3 (since 77 is 10 less than 80, which is 77 = 80 - 3) and y = 3 (since 93 is 3 more than 90). So the approximate product is 77*93 \u2248 80*90 - (-3)(3) = 7200 + 9 = 7209. Wait, that's not matching. Hmm, maybe my estimation is off.\n\nWait, perhaps the formula is ab - (x)(y). So if a = 80 - 3 and b = 90 + 3, then the product is (80 - 3)(90 + 3) = 80*90 + 80*3 - 3*90 - 3*3 = 7200 + 240 - 270 - 9 = 7200 + 240 = 7440; 7440 - 270 = 7170; 7170 - 9 = 7161. Oh, right! So that works. So using the approximation, we get 7161, which matches the exact calculation. So that's another way to see it.\n\nTherefore, all methods point to the product being 7161. I think that's solid. Let me just do a quick check with a calculator to be 100% sure. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can do another multiplication step manually.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n      77\n    x 93\n    ----\n      231  (77 x 3)\n     6930   (77 x 90, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n     7161\n\nYes, adding 231 and 6930 gives 7161. So that's correct. Therefore, the product of 77 and 93 is 7161.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 77 and 93 is \\boxed{7161}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 77 and 93, we can use the distributive property:\n\n1. Break down the numbers into smaller components:\n   - \\(77 = 70 + 7\\)\n   - \\(93 = 90 + 3\\)\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   (70 + 7)(90 + 3) = 70 \\cdot 90 + 70 \\cdot 3 + 7 \\cdot 90 + 7 \\cdot 3\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - \\(70 \\cdot 90 = 6300\\)\n   - \\(70 \\cdot 3 = 210\\)\n   - \\(7 \\cdot 90 = 630\\)\n   - \\(7 \\cdot 3 = 21\\)\n\n4. Add the results together:\n   \\[\n   6300 + 210 + 630 + 21 = 6300 + 210 = 6510\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6510 + 630 = 7140\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7140 + 21 = 7161\n   \\]\n\nAnother method using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n1. Multiply 77 by 3 (units place of 93):\n   \\[\n   77 \\cdot 3 = 231\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 77 by 90 (tens place of 93):\n   \\[\n   77 \\cdot 90 = 6930\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   231 + 6930 = 7161\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 77 and 93 is \\(\\boxed{7161}\\).",
