      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 96 and 19. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done step by step, but maybe there's a quicker way to do it mentally. Let me think.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 96 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 19. I know that 96 is 90 plus 6. So, if I split 96 into 90 and 6, then I can use the distributive property. That means 96 times 19 is the same as (90 + 6) times 19. Which would be 90 times 19 plus 6 times 19. Let me write that down:\n\n90 \u00d7 19 + 6 \u00d7 19\n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. Starting with 90 times 19. Hmm, 90 times 20 would be 1800, right? But since it's 19, which is one less than 20, I need to subtract 90 from 1800. So, 1800 minus 90 is 1710. So, 90 \u00d7 19 = 1710.\n\nNow, the other part is 6 times 19. Let me calculate that. 6 times 20 is 120, so subtracting 6 gives me 120 - 6 = 114. So, 6 \u00d7 19 = 114.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 1710 + 114. Let's see, 1710 + 100 is 1810, and then adding 14 more would make it 1824. So, 1710 + 114 = 1824.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 90 \u00d7 19: 90 \u00d7 10 is 900, 90 \u00d7 9 is 810, so 900 + 810 = 1710. That's correct. Then 6 \u00d7 19: 6 \u00d7 10 is 60, 6 \u00d7 9 is 54, so 60 + 54 = 114. Adding them together: 1710 + 114. Let's do 1710 + 100 = 1810, then 1810 + 14 = 1824. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication directly without breaking it down. Let's try that method to cross-verify. Multiplying 96 by 19. Let's use the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, multiply 96 by 9 (the units digit of 19). 96 \u00d7 9. Let's compute that. 90 \u00d7 9 = 810, and 6 \u00d7 9 = 54. So, 810 + 54 = 864. That's the units digit. Then, since the 9 in 19 is in the tens place, we need to multiply 96 by 10 (which is the tens digit) and add a zero at the end. So, 96 \u00d7 10 = 960. Now, add 864 and 960 together.\n\n864 + 960. Let's add the hundreds: 800 + 900 = 1700. Then the tens: 60 + 60 = 120. Then the units: 4 + 0 = 4. So, 1700 + 120 = 1820, plus 4 is 1824. So, same result. That's reassuring.\n\nAnother way to check could be using a calculator, but since I don't have one, I'll just do another verification. Let's see, 96 \u00d7 20 is 1920. Since 96 \u00d7 19 is 96 \u00d7 20 minus 96. So, 1920 - 96. Let's subtract: 1920 - 100 = 1820, but we subtracted 4 more than needed, so add back 4: 1820 + 4 = 1824. Yep, same answer.\n\nSo, all methods point to the product being 1824. I think that's solid. Let me just recap the steps to ensure I didn't skip anything. Breaking down 96 into 90 and 6, multiplied by 19, then adding the results. Alternatively, standard multiplication, breaking down 19 into 9 and 10, doing the multiplications, and adding. Both methods worked. Also, cross-checking by subtracting from 96\u00d720. All methods lead to 1824. So, I'm confident that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 96 and 19 is \\boxed{1824}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 96 and 19, we can break down 96 into 90 and 6 and use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n96 \\times 19 = (90 + 6) \\times 19 = 90 \\times 19 + 6 \\times 19\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(90 \\times 19\\):\n\\[\n90 \\times 19 = 90 \\times (20 - 1) = 90 \\times 20 - 90 \\times 1 = 1800 - 90 = 1710\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(6 \\times 19\\):\n\\[\n6 \\times 19 = 6 \\times (20 - 1) = 6 \\times 20 - 6 \\times 1 = 120 - 6 = 114\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n1710 + 114 = 1824\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  96 \\times 19 \\\\\n  \\hline\n  864 \\quad (\\text{96} \\times 9) \\\\\n 960 \\quad (\\text{96} \\times 10) \\\\\n\\hline\n1824\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method involves subtracting from \\(96 \\times 20\\):\n\\[\n96 \\times 20 = 1920 \\\\\n1920 - 96 = 1824\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 96 and 19 is \\(\\boxed{1824}\\).",
