      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 21 and 80. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 21 by 80. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe breaking down the numbers would help. I know that 21 is 20 plus 1, and 80 is 80. So if I split 21 into 20 and 1, then the multiplication becomes 20 times 80 plus 1 times 80. Let me write that out:\n\n21 \u00d7 80 = (20 + 1) \u00d7 80 = 20\u00d780 + 1\u00d780.\n\nOkay, that seems manageable. Let's compute each part separately. 20 times 80. Well, 20 is 2 times 10, so 20\u00d780 is the same as 2\u00d710\u00d780. Multiplying 10 and 80 first gives 800, then times 2 is 1600. So 20\u00d780 is 1600.\n\nThen the other part is 1\u00d780, which is just 80. So adding those two results together: 1600 + 80. Let me add those. 1600 plus 80 is 1680. So does that mean the product of 21 and 80 is 1680?\n\nWait, let me check another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  21\n\u00d780\n-----\n...\n\nMultiplying 21 by 0 (the units place of 80) gives 0. Then multiplying 21 by 8 (the tens place of 80) gives 168. But since the 8 is in the tens place, we have to add a zero at the end. So 168 becomes 1680. Yep, that matches the previous result. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of 80 as 8 times 10. So 21 times 80 is 21 times 8 times 10. Let's compute 21\u00d78 first. 20\u00d78 is 160, and 1\u00d78 is 8, so 160+8=168. Then multiply by 10 gives 1680. Again, same answer. So three different methods all leading to 1680. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can check with addition. If I add 21 eighty times. Hmm, that's a lot, but let's see. 21 added eight times is 21+21+21+21+21+21+21+21. Let's compute that step by step:\n\n21+21 = 42\n\n42+21 = 63\n\n63+21 = 84\n\n84+21 = 105\n\n105+21 = 126\n\n126+21 = 147\n\n147+21 = 168\n\nWait, that's only eight times. But 21\u00d780 is 80 times 21. So if adding 21 eight times gives 168, then adding it another 72 times (since 80 is 8+72) would give 168\u00d78. Let's see:\n\n168\u00d78: 100\u00d78=800, 60\u00d78=480, 8\u00d78=64. So 800+480=1280, plus 64=1344. Then 1680-1344=336. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Maybe this method isn't the best. Maybe addition isn't the best approach here because it's tedious and error-prone. Let me try another way.\n\nAlternatively, 21\u00d780 is the same as 21\u00d78\u00d710, which we already did as 1680. So that's straightforward. The addition method was confusing me because I thought it should match, but maybe I made a mistake in the steps. Let me check again:\n\n21 added 80 times. If I group them into 8 groups of 21 and 72 groups of 21. Wait, no, 80 is 8+72. So 21\u00d78=168, then 21\u00d772. Let's compute 21\u00d770=1470 and 21\u00d72=42, so 1470+42=1512. Then 168+1512=1680. There we go. So that works. So 21\u00d780=1680. So that's consistent.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using distributive property in another form. For example, 21\u00d780 can be thought of as (20+1)\u00d780, which we already did. But maybe also, 21 is 20+1, but 80 is 8\u00d710. So combining them, (20+1)\u00d7(8\u00d710) = 20\u00d78\u00d710 + 1\u00d78\u00d710 = 1600 + 80 = 1680. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, using the fact that 80 is 100 - 20. So 21\u00d7(100 - 20) = 21\u00d7100 - 21\u00d720 = 2100 - 420 = 1680. That works too. So subtracting 420 from 2100 gives 1680. That's another valid method.\n\nSo all these different methods confirm that the product is 1680. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. Each step checks out with multiple methods, so I'm confident the answer is correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 21 and 80 is \\boxed{1680}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 21 and 80, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers:**\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 80 = (20 + 1) \\times 80 = 20 \\times 80 + 1 \\times 80\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(20 \\times 80\\):\n     \\[\n     20 \\times 80 = 1600\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(1 \\times 80\\):\n     \\[\n     1 \\times 80 = 80\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     1600 + 80 = 1680\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    21 \\\\\n   \\times 80 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    1680 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   - Multiply 21 by 8 (tens place):\n     \\[\n     21 \\times 8 = 168\n     \\]\n   - Add a zero at the end (since 80 is in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     168 \\times 10 = 1680\n     \\]\n\n3. **Using distributive property with different grouping:**\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 80 = 21 \\times (8 \\times 10) = (20 + 1) \\times 8 \\times 10 = 21 \\times 8 \\times 10\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(21 \\times 8\\):\n     \\[\n     21 \\times 8 = 168\n     \\]\n   - Multiply by 10:\n     \\[\n     168 \\times 10 = 1680\n     \\]\n\n4. **Subtracting from a larger multiple:**\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 80 = 21 \\times (100 - 20) = 21 \\times 100 - 21 \\times 20\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(21 \\times 100\\):\n     \\[\n     21 \\times 100 = 2100\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(21 \\times 20\\):\n     \\[\n     21 \\times 20 = 420\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the results:\n     \\[\n     2100 - 420 = 1680\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 21 and 80 is \\(\\boxed{1680}\\).",
