      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 26 and 11. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 26 by 11. Alright, let me think about how to do this. \n\nFirst, I know that multiplying by 11 can sometimes be done using a trick, especially if the number is a two-digit number. Wait, isn't there a trick where you take the original number, multiply it by 10, then add the original number again? Let me check if that works here. \n\nSo, if I take 26 and multiply it by 10, that would be 260. Then, adding the original number, which is 26, to that result. So 260 + 26. Let me add those together. 260 plus 20 is 280, and then plus 6 more would be 286. So does that mean 26 times 11 is 286? Let me verify that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can do the multiplication the standard way. Let's break down 26 into 20 and 6. So 20 times 11 is 220, and 6 times 11 is 66. Then add those two results together: 220 + 66. Let's see, 220 + 60 is 280, and then +6 is 286. Okay, that matches the previous answer. So that seems right.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can do it the long multiplication way. Let's line them up:\n\n   26\nx  11\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 26 by 1, which is 26. Then multiply 26 by 10, which is 260. Now add those two together: 26 + 260. That's 286. Yep, same result. \n\nAnother way to check might be to use addition repeatedly. 11 added 26 times. Let's see, 11 + 11 is 22. Then 22 + 11 is 33. 33 + 11 is 44. 44 + 11 is 55. 55 + 11 is 66. 66 + 11 is 77. 77 + 11 is 88. 88 + 11 is 99. 99 + 11 is 110. Wait, that's 10 times. So 11 times 26 is 286. Because 11 times 10 is 110, so 11 times 20 is 220, plus 11 times 6 is 66, which is 220 + 66 = 286. Yep, that works too.\n\nSo all methods point to the same answer. I think that's solid. Let me just do a quick check with another multiplication technique. Maybe the distributive property again. 26 is 20 + 6, so 20*11 + 6*11 = 220 + 66 = 286. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using fractions. 11 can be written as 11/1. So 26*(11/1) = (26*11)/1 = 286/1 = 286. That works too. \n\nI guess another way is to use the fact that 26*11 is the same as 26*(10+1) which we already did. So all these different methods confirm that the product is 286. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. Each step checks out. So I'm confident that the answer is 286.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 26 and 11 can be calculated using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n26 \\times 11 = 26 \\times (10 + 1) = (26 \\times 10) + (26 \\times 1) = 260 + 26 = 286\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{286}",
