tep by step:\n\nFirst, multiply 25 by 6 (units place): 25 \u00d7 6 = 150. So write 0 and carry over 15.\n\nThen multiply 25 by 3 (tens place): 25 \u00d7 3 = 75. Add the carried over 15: 75 + 15 = 90. So write 90. So the total is 900. Yes, that's correct. So the multiplication by breaking down the numbers step by step also gives 900. \n\nTherefore, all methods confirm that the product of 25 and 36 is 900. I think that's solid. No mistakes in my calculations as far as I can tell. All different methods lead to the same answer, so I'm confident that's the right one.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 25 and 36 can be calculated using multiple methods:\n\n1. **Breaking down 36 into 30 and 6:**\n   - \\(25 \\times 30 = 750\\)\n   - \\(25 \\times 6 = 150\\)\n   - Adding the results: \\(750 + 150 = 900\\)\n\n2. **Using the distributive property:**\n   - \\(25 \\times 36 = (20 + 5) \\times 36 = 20 \\times 36 + 5 \\times 36\\)\n   - \\(20 \\times 36 = 720\\) and \\(5 \\times 36 = 180\\)\n   - Adding the results: \\(720 + 180 = 900\\)\n\n3. **Breaking down 36 into 4 and 9:**\n   - \\(25 \\times 36 = 25 \\times (4 \\times 9) = (25 \\times 4) \\times 9\\)\n   - \\(25 \\times 4 = 100\\) and \\(100 \\times 9 = 900\\)\n\n4. **Standard multiplication:**\n   - \\(25 \\times 36 = 900\\) (verified by multiplying each digit place and summing)\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{900}\\).",
