is 20 + 6, and 76 is 70 + 6. Wait, that's the same as before. Alternatively, maybe use the fact that 76 is 70 + 6, but maybe there's a different way. Hmm, not sure. Alternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm again but write it out.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n      26\n    x 76\n    ----\n      156  (26 times 6)\n     1820   (26 times 70, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n     1976\n\nYes, adding 156 and 1820 gives 1976. So that's the same as before. So, seems like 1976 is the correct product.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, maybe do it another way. Let's use the original numbers. 26 times 76. Let's compute 26*70 and 26*6, then add them. 26*70: 26*7=182, so 182*10=1820. Then 26*6=156. 1820+156=1976. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the difference of squares formula? Wait, not sure if that applies here. The difference of squares is (a+b)(a-b)=a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. But here, 26 and 76 don't seem to fit that. Unless we can express them in terms of something else. Let me check. 76 - 26 = 50. Hmm, not sure. Maybe not the right approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe break down 76 into 50 + 26. Then, 26*50 + 26*26. Let's see. 26*50 is 1300, and 26*26 is 676. Then, 1300 + 676 = 1976. Oh, that's another way. So, 26*(50 + 26) = 26*50 + 26*26 = 1300 + 676 = 1976. That works too. So, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 1976. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 26 and 76 is 1976.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 76 is \\boxed{1976}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 76, we can use the distributive property:\n\n1. Decompose 26 into 20 + 6 and 76 into 70 + 6.\n2. Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number:\n   \\[\n   (20 + 6) \\times (70 + 6) = 20 \\times 70 + 20 \\times 6 + 6 \\times 70 + 6 \\times 6\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each product:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 70 = 1400, \\quad 20 \\times 6 = 120, \\quad 6 \\times 70 = 420, \\quad 6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n4. Add the results together:\n   \\[\n   1400 + 120 + 420 + 36 = 1976\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n1. Multiply 26 by 6: \\(26 \\times 6 = 156\\)\n2. Multiply 26 by 70: \\(26 \\times 70 = 1820\\)\n3. Add the results: \\(156 + 1820 = 1976\\)\n\nAnother verification by decomposing 76 into 50 + 26:\n1. Multiply 26 by 50: \\(26 \\times 50 = 1300\\)\n2. Multiply 26 by 26: \\(26 \\times 26 = 676\\)\n3. Add the results: \\(1300 + 676 = 1976\\)\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 26 and 76 is \\(\\boxed{1976}\\).",
