fy. But since I don't have a calculator here, but I know all the methods give the same result, so I can be confident. So, the answer is 5664.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 96 and 59 can be calculated using multiple methods for verification:\n\n1. **Adjusting for proximity to 100**:\n   - \\( 100 \\times 59 = 5900 \\)\n   - Subtract \\( 4 \\times 59 = 236 \\):  \n     \\( 5900 - 236 = 5664 \\)\n\n2. **Breaking down 96 into 90 + 6**:\n   - \\( 90 \\times 59 = 5310 \\)\n   - \\( 6 \\times 59 = 354 \\)\n   - Adding results: \\( 5310 + 354 = 5664 \\)\n\n3. **Using \\( 59 = 60 - 1 \\)**:\n   - \\( 96 \\times 60 = 5760 \\)\n   - Subtract \\( 96 \\times 1 = 96 \\):  \n     \\( 5760 - 96 = 5664 \\)\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{5664}\\).",
