tual product should be a bit less than 1750. 1625 is indeed less than 1750, so that makes sense. The difference between 25 and 25 is 0, and between 65 and 70 is -5. So the adjustment is 25 times 5, which is 125. So 1750 minus 125 is 1625. Perfect, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, using mental math. 25 times 60 is 1500, and 25 times 5 is 125. So 1500 + 125 is 1625. Yep, same result. I think I'm confident that the product is 1625. No mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm it. So the answer should be 1625.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 25 and 65 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n25 \\times 65 = 25 \\times (60 + 5) = (25 \\times 60) + (25 \\times 5)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(25 \\times 60 = 1500\\)\n- \\(25 \\times 5 = 125\\)\n\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n1500 + 125 = 1625\n\\]\n\nVerification using prime factors:\n- \\(25 = 5^2\\)\n- \\(65 = 5 \\times 13\\)\n- \\(25 \\times 65 = 5^3 \\times 13 = 125 \\times 13 = 1625\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{1625}\\).",
