= 496. But if I think of 31 as 30 + 1, then 16*30 + 16*1. So that's 480 + 16 = 496. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nAnother way: 16 times 31. Let's do 16*31. Let's do 10*31 = 310, 6*31 = 186. Then 310 + 186. 310 + 100 is 410, 410 + 86 is 496. Yep, same result. So all these different methods are confirming that the product is 496. I think that's solid.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can check with another multiplication table. Let's see, 16*31. Let's do 16*30=480, 16*1=16, so 480+16=496. Yep. Alternatively, 16*31. Let's do 16*31. 16*3 is 48, so 16*30 is 480. Then 16*1 is 16. So 480+16=496. All right, that's consistent.\n\nI think that's thorough enough. All different methods lead to the same answer, so I can be confident that the product of 16 and 31 is 496.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 16 and 31 is \\boxed{496}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 16 and 31, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\(16 \\times 30\\):\n   \\[\n   16 \\times 30 = 16 \\times (3 \\times 10) = (16 \\times 3) \\times 10 = 48 \\times 10 = 480\n   \\]\n\n2. Add the remaining 16 to the result:\n   \\[\n   480 + 16 = 496\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using different methods:\n- Using the distributive property: \\(16 \\times 31 = (30 + 1) \\times 16 = 30 \\times 16 + 1 \\times 16 = 480 + 16 = 496\\)\n- Breaking down 31 into 25 and 6: \\(16 \\times 25 = 400\\) and \\(16 \\times 6 = 96\\), then adding the results: \\(400 + 96 = 496\\)\n- Using the standard multiplication algorithm: \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     16 \\\\\n   \\times 31 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     16 \\quad (\\text{16} \\times 1) \\\\\n   480 \\quad (\\text{16} \\times 30) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     496 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 16 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{496}\\).",
