      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 93 and 27. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think. \n\nFirst, 93 can be broken down into 90 and 3, right? And 27 is 20 and 7. So if I distribute the multiplication over addition, it would be (90 + 3) multiplied by (20 + 7). Wait, but actually, since multiplication distributes over addition, maybe I can do it step by step. Let me try that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a quicker way. I recall that 27 is 3 cubed, but I'm not sure if that helps here. Let me think of another approach. Maybe I can multiply 93 by 20 first and then subtract 93 times 3, since 27 is 20 + 7. Wait, 27 is 20 + 7, but 93 times 20 is 1860, and then 93 times 7 is... Let me calculate that. 93 times 7. 90 times 7 is 630, and 3 times 7 is 21, so 630 + 21 = 651. Then, adding 1860 and 651. Let's see, 1860 + 600 is 2460, and then +51 is 2511. So is the answer 2511? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, another way to do it might be to break down 27 into 9 times 3. So 93 times 27 is the same as 93 times 9 times 3. Let's try that. 93 times 9 first. 90 times 9 is 810, and 3 times 9 is 27, so 810 + 27 = 837. Then multiply that by 3. 837 times 3. 800 times 3 is 2400, 30 times 3 is 90, and 7 times 3 is 21. So 2400 + 90 is 2490, plus 21 is 2511. Okay, same result. So that seems to check out.\n\nBut let me try another method just to be thorough. Maybe the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  93\nx 27\n----\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, 7 times 93. 7 times 90 is 630, and 7 times 3 is 21. So 630 + 21 = 651. Then the next digit is 2, but since it's in the tens place, we need to multiply by 10. So 651 times 10 is 6510. Then add 651 and 6510. 6510 + 651. Let's see, 6510 + 600 is 7110, then +51 is 7161. Wait, that's different. Wait, hold on, that can't be right. Wait, no, maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nWait, no. Let me try again. When multiplying 93 by 27, the standard multiplication would be:\n\nFirst, multiply 93 by 7 (units place):\n\n93\nx  7\n----\n651\n\nThen multiply 93 by 2 (tens place), which is 186, but since it's in the tens place, we need to multiply by 10, so 1860. Then add 651 and 1860. 651 + 1860. Let's do that step by step. 600 + 1800 = 2400, 50 + 60 = 110, 1 + 0 = 1. So 2400 + 110 is 2510, plus 1 is 2511. There we go. So that matches the previous results. So 2511 is the answer.\n\nWait, but when I first tried adding 1860 and 651, I thought I got 2511, but when I broke down the second method, I thought I had 7161. Wait, no, that was a mistake. Wait, no, actually, when I first thought of adding 651 and 1860, I thought 651 + 1860. Let me check that again. 651 + 1860. 600 + 1800 = 2400, 50 + 60 = 110, 1 + 0 = 1. So 2400 + 110 = 2510, plus 1 is 2511. So that's correct. So all methods lead to 2511. So that must be the answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 27 is 3^3, but I don't know if that helps here. Alternatively, maybe use the difference of squares formula? Hmm, not sure. Let me think. 93 and 27. 93 is 90 + 3, 27 is 20 + 7. Maybe another way. Let's see, 93 * 27. Let's write 27 as 30 - 3. Then 93*(30 - 3) = 93*30 - 93*3. 93*30 is 2790, and 93*3 is 279. So 2790 - 279. 2790 - 200 is 2590, minus 79 is 2511. Again, same answer. So that's another method. So all different methods give me 2511. So I think that's correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can check with a calculator. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can do a quick check. Let's see, 90*27 is 2430, and 3*27 is 81. So 2430 + 81 = 2511. Yes, that works. So 90*27 is 2430, plus 3*27 which is 81, gives 2511. Perfect. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nSo all methods lead to 2511. Therefore, the product of 93 and 27 is 2511.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 93 and 27 is \\boxed{2511}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 93 and 27, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers:**\n   - \\(93 = 90 + 3\\)\n   - \\(27 = 20 + 7\\)\n   - Calculate \\((90 + 3) \\times (20 + 7)\\) using the distributive property:\n     \\[\n     (90 + 3) \\times (20 + 7) = 90 \\times 20 + 90 \\times 7 + 3 \\times 20 + 3 \\times 7\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 1800 + 630 + 60 + 21 = 1800 + 630 = 2430; \\quad 2430 + 60 = 2490; \\quad 2490 + 21 = 2511\n     \\]\n\n2. **Multiplying by parts:**\n   - \\(93 \\times 27 = 93 \\times (9 \\times 3) = (93 \\times 9) \\times 3\\)\n     - Calculate \\(93 \\times 9\\):\n       \\[\n       93 \\times 9 = 837\n       \\]\n     - Then multiply by 3:\n       \\[\n       837 \\times 3 = 2511\n       \\]\n\n3. **Standard multiplication:**\n   - Multiply 93 by 7 and 2:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{r}\n       93 \\\\\n     \\times 27 \\\\\n     \\hline\n       651 \\quad \\text{(93 \\times 7)} \\\\\n     1860 \\quad \\text{(93 \\times 20)} \\\\\n     \\hline\n       2511 \\\\\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Alternative method using distributive property:**\n   - \\(93 \\times 27 = 93 \\times (30 - 3) = 93 \\times 30 - 93 \\times 3\\)\n     \\[\n     93 \\times 30 = 2790; \\quad 93 \\times 3 = 279\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2790 - 279 = 2511\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 93 and 27 is \\(\\boxed{2511}\\).",
