ve messed up the steps when I first did it. So both methods confirm that the product is 520. Therefore, the answer should be 520.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's think of 26 as 25 + 1. Then 25*20 = 500, and 1*20 = 20. Then 500 + 20 = 520. Yep, same result. So that's another way to check. So all methods lead to 520. So I think that's the correct answer. \n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can do a quick check. If 26*20=520, then 26*10=260, so 260*2=520. Correct. Alternatively, 20*26=20*(20+6)=400 + 120=520. All right, so all different methods confirm that the product is 520. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 26 and 20 can be calculated by recognizing that multiplying by 20 is equivalent to multiplying by 2 and then by 10. \n\nFirst, multiply 26 by 2:\n\\[\n26 \\times 2 = 52\n\\]\nNext, multiply the result by 10:\n\\[\n52 \\times 10 = 520\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, breaking down 20 into 20 + 10:\n\\[\n26 \\times 20 = 26 \\times (20 + 10) = (26 \\times 20) + (26 \\times 10) = 520 + 260 = 520\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{520}\\).",
