      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 30 and 27. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 30 by 27. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 30 times 27. Well, 30 is 3 times 10, and 27 is 27. So maybe I can write it as 3 times 10 times 27. That might be easier. Let me try that. So 3 times 10 is 30, and then 30 times 27. Wait, that's the same as the original problem. Hmm, maybe breaking it down differently.\n\nAlternatively, I know that 27 times 30 is the same as 27 times 3 times 10. Because 30 is 3 times 10. So 27 times 3 is... let's see, 27 times 3. 20 times 3 is 60, and 7 times 3 is 21, so 60 plus 21 is 81. Then 81 times 10 is 810. So that would make the product 810. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, another way to do it might be to do the multiplication directly. Let's try that. 30 multiplied by 27. Using the standard multiplication method. Let's see, 30 times 27. So 30 is 3 tens, and 27 is 27 ones. So multiplying 3 tens by 27 ones. That would be 3 times 27 times 10. 3 times 27 is 81, then times 10 is 810. Yeah, same result. So that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use distributive property differently. Let's see, 30 times 27. 30 is 27 plus 3. So 27 times 30 is 27 times 30. Wait, that's the same as the original. Hmm, maybe not helpful. Alternatively, 30 is 10 times 3, so 10 times 27 is 270, then times 3 is 810. Yeah, that works too. So 10 times 27 is 270, then multiply by 3 gives 810. That's another way to think about it.\n\nLet me verify with another method. Maybe using addition. 27 added to itself 30 times. But that would be tedious. 27 added 30 times. Well, 27 times 10 is 270, so 270 times 3 is 810. Yeah, same answer. So all these different methods are leading me to 810. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can check with a calculator. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me do another check. Let's see, 27 times 30. 27 times 10 is 270, so 270 times 3 is 810. Yep, that's right. Alternatively, 27 times 30. 27 times 3 is 81, so 81 times 10 is 810. All right, that's consistent.\n\nAnother way: 30 times 27. Let's write it out vertically:\n\n   30\nx 27\n-----\n?\n\nMultiplying 30 by 27. Starting from the right, 0 times 27 is 0. Then 0 times 7 is 0. Then 3 times 27. Wait, no, actually, when multiplying by the units digit first, which is 7. So 30 times 7. 30 times 7 is 210. Then bring down the 2 (from 30). Wait, maybe I need to do it step by step.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying 30 by 27, it's like multiplying 30 by 20 and 30 by 7, then adding the results. So 30 times 20 is 600, and 30 times 7 is 210. Then 600 plus 210 is 810. That works too. So that's another way to split the multiplication into two parts and add them.\n\nSo all these different methods confirm that the product is 810. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. Each step checks out, and multiple methods lead to the same answer. So I'm pretty confident the product of 30 and 27 is 810.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 30 and 27 is \\boxed{810}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 30 and 27, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property. \n\nFirst, recognize that 30 can be written as \\(3 \\times 10\\). Therefore, the multiplication \\(30 \\times 27\\) can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n(3 \\times 10) \\times 27\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply 3 by 27:\n\\[\n3 \\times 27 = 81\n\\]\n\nThen, multiply the result by 10:\n\\[\n81 \\times 10 = 810\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by breaking down 27 into 20 and 7, then multiplying:\n\\[\n30 \\times 27 = 30 \\times (20 + 7) = (30 \\times 20) + (30 \\times 7)\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 20 = 600\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 7 = 210\n\\]\n\\[\n600 + 210 = 810\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 30 and 27 is \\(\\boxed{810}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
