carry over 4. 8*3=24, plus 4 is 28. So that's 288. Then, add 252 and 2880. Wait, that's the same as before. 252 + 2880. 252 + 2000=2252, 2252 + 880=3132. Yep, same result.\n\nSo all methods lead to 3132. I think that's solid. Maybe check with a calculator? Well, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can think of another way. Let's see, 36*87. If I know that 36*80=2880 and 36*7=252, so 2880+252=3132. Yep, same thing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use distributive property in another way. For example, 36*87 = (40 - 4)*87 = 40*87 - 4*87. Let's compute that. 40*87. 40*80=3200, 40*7=280, so 3200+280=3480. Then, 4*87=348. So, 3480 - 348. 3480 - 300=3180, then -48=3132. Again, same answer.\n\nAlright, so all different methods confirm that the product is 3132. I think that's correct. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Each step checks out when I verify with another method. So, I'm confident the answer is 3132.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 36 and 87 is \\boxed{3132}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 36 and 87, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 87 = (30 + 6) \\times (80 + 7)\n   \\]\n   Distribute and calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 80 + 30 \\times 7 + 6 \\times 80 + 6 \\times 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2400 + 210 + 480 + 42\n   \\]\n   Adding these together:\n   \\[\n   2400 + 210 = 2610\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   480 + 42 = 522\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2610 + 522 = 3132\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    36 \\\\\n  \\times 87 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    252 \\quad (\\text{36} \\times 7) \\\\\n   2880 \\quad (\\text{36} \\times 80) \\\\\n   \\hline\n   3132 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Using the fact that \\(87 = 90 - 3\\):**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 87 = 36 \\times (90 - 3) = 36 \\times 90 - 36 \\times 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3240 - 108 = 3132\n   \\]\n\n4. **Direct multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    36 \\\\\n   \\times 87 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    252 \\quad (\\text{7 times 36}) \\\\\n   2880 \\quad (\\text{80 times 36}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n   3132 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 36 and 87 is \\(\\boxed{3132}\\).",
