      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 36 and 22. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers together gives their product. Alright, so 36 times 22. Let me think about how to approach this. Maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I know that 36 can be split into 30 and 6. So, if I multiply 30 by 22 and then multiply 6 by 22 separately, I can add those two results together. Let me try that. \n\nStarting with 30 times 22. Well, 30 times 20 is 600, and 30 times 2 is 60. So adding those together, 600 + 60 = 660. Okay, that part seems right. Now, 6 times 22. Hmm, 6 times 20 is 120, and 6 times 2 is 12. So 120 + 12 = 132. \n\nNow, adding the two products together: 660 + 132. Let me do that step by step. 660 + 100 is 760, and then adding 32 more gives 792. So, 36 times 22 is 792. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see, 22 is 20 + 2, so 36 times 20 is 720, and 36 times 2 is 72. Adding those together: 720 + 72. That's 792. Oh, same answer. That's reassuring. \n\nAnother way to check might be to use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n   36\nx 22\n-----\nFirst, multiply 36 by 2 (the units digit of 22). 36 x 2 = 72. Write down 2 and carry over 7.\nThen, multiply 36 by 20 (the tens digit of 22). 36 x 20 = 720. Write down 720. Now, add the two results: 72 and 720. 720 + 72 = 792. Yep, same answer again. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can do it the standard long multiplication way. \n\nMultiplying 36 by 22:\n\nStart with the rightmost digit of 22, which is 2. Multiply 2 by 36: 2 x 36 = 72. Write down 2 (units digit) and carry over 7.\n\nNext digit is 2. Multiply 2 by 36 again: 2 x 36 = 72, plus the carried over 7 makes 79. Write down 9 and carry over 7.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, write down the carried over 7. So the result is 792. \n\nAll methods lead to the same answer, 792. I think that's solid. Let me just verify with a calculator in my mind. 36 times 20 is 720, 36 times 2 is 72. 720 + 72 is 792. Definitely correct. \n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Each step checks out. So, the product of 36 and 22 is 792.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 22 can be calculated by breaking down the multiplication into simpler parts. \n\nFirst, multiply 36 by 20:  \n\\( 36 \\times 20 = 720 \\)\n\nNext, multiply 36 by 2:  \n\\( 36 \\times 2 = 72 \\)\n\nAdd the results:  \n\\( 720 + 72 = 792 \\)\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm confirms the result:  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n36 \\\\\n\\times\\,22 \\\\\n\\hline\n72\\,\\, \\text{(36 \u00d7 2)} \\\\\n720\\,\\text{(36 \u00d7 20, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n792 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{792}",
      "logprobs": null,
