      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 32 and 22. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers together gives their product. Alright, let me start by recalling how to multiply a two-digit number by another two-digit number. Maybe I can break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, I can think of 32 as 30 + 2. That might make it easier to multiply each part by 22 separately and then add the results together. So, if I do that, it's like (30 + 2) \u00d7 22. Using the distributive property, that would be 30\u00d722 + 2\u00d722. Let me calculate each part.\n\nStarting with 30\u00d722. Well, 30 times 20 is 600, and 30 times 2 is 60. So adding those together, 600 + 60 = 660. Okay, that part seems straightforward.\n\nNow, the other part is 2\u00d722. That's simpler. 2 times 20 is 40, and 2 times 2 is 4. So 40 + 4 = 44. Got that.\n\nNow, add the two results together: 660 + 44. Let me do that addition. 660 + 40 is 700, and then adding the remaining 4 gives 704. So, 30\u00d722 + 2\u00d722 = 660 + 44 = 704. Therefore, the product of 32 and 22 should be 704.\n\nWait, let me check if there's another way to multiply them directly to confirm. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  32\n\u00d722\n----\nFirst, multiply 32 by 2. That's 64. Then, multiply 32 by 20. That's 640. Now, add 64 and 640 together. 64 + 640 is 704. Yep, same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the fact that 32 is 8 times 4 and 22 is 11 times 2. So, 8\u00d74=32 and 11\u00d72=22. Then, multiplying those results: 8\u00d711=88 and 4\u00d72=8. Then, 88\u00d78. Hmm, 80\u00d78=640 and 8\u00d78=64, so 640+64=704. Again, same answer. So that's three different methods giving me 704. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can use another approach. Let's see, 32\u00d722. Breaking down 22 into 20 + 2, so 32\u00d720 + 32\u00d72. 32\u00d720 is 640, and 32\u00d72 is 64. Adding them gives 640 + 64 = 704. Yep, same as before.\n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to 704. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Each step makes sense. So, the product of 32 and 22 is definitely 704.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 32 and 22 is \\boxed{704}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 32 and 22, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n32 \\times 22 = (30 + 2) \\times 22 = 30 \\times 22 + 2 \\times 22\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(30 \\times 22\\):\n\\[\n30 \\times 22 = 660\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(2 \\times 22\\):\n\\[\n2 \\times 22 = 44\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n660 + 44 = 704\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 32 \\\\\n\\times 22 \\\\\n\\hline\n 64 \\quad (\\text{32} \\times 2) \\\\\n640 \\quad (\\text{32} \\times 20) \\\\\n\\hline\n 704 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 32 and 22 is \\(\\boxed{704}\\).",
