      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 68 and 47. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can be done by breaking them down, maybe using the distributive property. Let me think. 68 times 47... Wait, 68 is 60 plus 8, and 47 is 40 plus 7. So if I distribute, it would be (60 + 8) multiplied by (40 + 7). \n\nFirst, I can multiply 60 by 40. That's 60*40. Let me calculate that. 60 times 40 is 2400. Then, 60 times 7. That's 60*7=420. Next, 8 times 40. 8*40=320. Finally, 8 times 7. That's 56. Now, add all those up: 2400 + 420 + 320 + 56. Let me add them step by step. 2400 + 420 is 2820. Then 2820 + 320 is 3140. Finally, 3140 + 56 equals 3196. So, is the product 3196? Let me check if that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. Multiply 68 by 47. Starting from the rightmost digit: 7 times 68. Wait, no, actually, in standard multiplication, you multiply the entire number. Let me write it out:\n\n      68\n    x 47\n    ----\n      68*7 (which is 476)\n     68*40 (which is 2720)\n    ----\n  476\n 2720\n  ----\n 3196\n\nYes, that gives the same result. So 68*47 is 3196. Let me try another way to confirm. Maybe breaking down 47 into 50 minus 3. So 68*(50 - 3) = 68*50 - 68*3. 68*50 is 3400, and 68*3 is 204. Then 3400 - 204 is 3196. Yep, same answer. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe do the multiplication directly. 68 times 47. Let's do 68*7 first. 60*7=420, 8*7=56, so 420+56=476. Then 68*40. 60*40=2400, 8*40=320, so 2400+320=2720. Then add 476 and 2720. 476 + 2720. Let's see, 476 + 2000=2476, then +720=2476+700=3176, +20=3196. Yep, same result. \n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to 3196. So the product of 68 and 47 is 3196. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 68 and 47 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times 47 = (60 + 8) \\times (40 + 7)\n\\]\n\nFirst, distribute the multiplication:\n\n1. \\(60 \\times 40 = 2400\\)\n2. \\(60 \\times 7 = 420\\)\n3. \\(8 \\times 40 = 320\\)\n4. \\(8 \\times 7 = 56\\)\n\nAdd all the results together:\n\n\\[\n2400 + 420 + 320 + 56 = 3196\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm confirms the result. Breaking down 47 into 50 - 3 also yields the same answer:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times (50 - 3) = 68 \\times 50 - 68 \\times 3 = 3400 - 204 = 3196\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 68 and 47 is \\(\\boxed{3196}\\).",
