+ 50 is 590, +4 is 594. Wait, that's not right. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, I think I messed up the steps here.\n\nWait, no. If I'm adding 54 ten times, each time adding 54. So starting with 54, then 54 + 54 = 108, then 108 + 54 = 162, 162 + 54 = 216, 216 + 54 = 270, 270 + 54 = 324, 324 + 54 = 378, 378 + 54 = 432, 432 + 54 = 486, 486 + 54 = 540. Oh, right! So each time, adding 54 once. So after ten additions, it's 540. So that matches the previous results. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property. 54*10 = (50 + 4)*10 = 50*10 + 4*10 = 500 + 40 = 540. Yep, same answer.\n\nAnother check: 54*10. Since 54 is 5.4*10, so 5.4*10*10 = 5.4*100 = 540. That works too.\n\nAlternatively, using decimal multiplication. 54.0 * 10.0. Moving the decimal point one place to the right gives 540.0. So that's straightforward.\n\nI think all methods point to the same answer here. So, the product of 54 and 10 is 540. I can't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. Each different approach gives the same result, so I'm confident that's correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 54 and 10 is found by multiplying 54 by 10. When multiplying any number by 10, you simply add a zero at the end of the number. Thus, 54 becomes 540. Verifying through addition (10 times 54 added together) or breaking down 54 into tens and ones (50*10 + 4*10) also confirms the result. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{540}",
