      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 99 and 31. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying two-digit numbers... I remember there's a trick for multiplying numbers close to 100, maybe? Like, if you have something like (100 - 1) times something else. Let me think. \n\nWait, 99 is 100 minus 1, right? So maybe I can use that to make the multiplication easier. Let me try that. So, 99 times 31 is the same as (100 - 1) times 31. If I distribute that, it would be 100 times 31 minus 1 times 31. That makes sense. \n\nCalculating 100 times 31 is straightforward. 100 times anything is just the number itself, so that's 3100. Then subtract 1 times 31, which is 31. So, 3100 minus 31. Let me do that subtraction. 3100 minus 30 is 3070, and then minus 1 more would be 3069. So, is the answer 3069? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way. Let's see. 31 times 99. Breaking it down, 31 times 100 is 3100, and then subtract 31 times 1, which is 31. So that's the same as before. Yep, 3100 - 31 = 3069. That seems right.\n\nWait, another way to check is to do 31 times 100 minus 31. Which is exactly what I did. So, that's consistent. Let me verify with another method. Maybe using the distributive property differently. For example, 31 can be broken into 30 + 1. So, 99 times 30 plus 99 times 1. Let's compute that.\n\n99 times 30. Hmm, 100 times 30 is 3000, so subtract 1 times 30, which is 30. So, 3000 - 30 = 2970. Then 99 times 1 is 99. So, adding those together: 2970 + 99. Let's see, 2970 + 100 would be 3070, but since it's 99, subtract 1, so 3070 - 1 = 3069. Same result. Okay, that's reassuring.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the fact that 99 is 9 times 11. So, 9 times 11 times 31. Let's see. 9 times 11 is 99, so then 99 times 31. Wait, that's the same as before. Hmm. Alternatively, 9 times 31 is 279, then 279 times 11. Let's compute that. 279 times 10 is 2790, plus 279 is 2790 + 279. Let's add those. 2790 + 200 is 2990, then +79 is 3069. Again, same answer. So, that's three different methods all leading to 3069. Seems like that's the right answer.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can do a quick check. Let's see, 100 times 31 is 3100, subtract 1 times 31, which is 31, so 3100 - 31 = 3069. Yep, that's consistent. Alternatively, 31 times 99. Let's think of 31 as 30 + 1. So, 30*99 + 1*99. 30*99 is 2970, plus 99 is 3069. All methods point to the same number. \n\nI think that's solid. So, the product of 99 and 31 is 3069. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning, and multiple methods confirm the answer. So, I'm confident that's correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 99 and 31 can be calculated using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n99 \\times 31 = (100 - 1) \\times 31 = 100 \\times 31 - 1 \\times 31\n\\]  \nCalculating each term:  \n\\[\n100 \\times 31 = 3100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1 \\times 31 = 31\n\\]  \nSubtracting the results:  \n\\[\n3100 - 31 = 3069\n\\]  \n**Answer:** \\boxed{3069}",
      "logprobs": null,
