      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 82 and 20. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 82 by 20. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 82 times 20. Well, 20 is the same as 2 times 10. So maybe I can multiply 82 by 2 first and then by 10. That might be easier. Let me try that.\n\nMultiplying 82 by 2. Let's do 80 times 2 and then 2 times 2. 80 times 2 is 160, and 2 times 2 is 4. So adding those together, 160 + 4 = 164. So 82 times 2 is 164. Got that part.\n\nNow, I need to multiply that result by 10. So 164 times 10. Hmm, multiplying by 10 is straightforward, right? You just add a zero at the end. So 164 times 10 would be 1640. Let me check that again. 164 times 10, moving the decimal one place to the right, which gives 1640. Yep, that seems right.\n\nWait, let me verify another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe I can do the multiplication directly without breaking it down. Let's see. 82 times 20. So 82 times 2 is 164, and then times 10 is 1640. Yeah, same result. Alternatively, maybe I can think of 20 as 2 times 10, so 82 times 2 is 164, then times 10 is 1640. Either way, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. 82 multiplied by 20. Let's write it out:\n\n  82\nx 20\n-----\n\nStarting from the right, multiply 82 by 0 first. That's easy, 0. Then multiply 82 by 2, which is 164. Then add them together. But since the second number is 20, which is two zeros, the result is 1640. So that's the same as before. So that checks out.\n\nAnother way to check might be to use distributive property. 82 times 20 is the same as 80 times 20 plus 2 times 20. Let's compute that. 80 times 20 is 1600, and 2 times 20 is 40. Adding those together, 1600 + 40 = 1640. Yep, same answer again. So all methods are leading me to 1640. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can do a quick division check. If 82 times 20 is 1640, then dividing 1640 by 20 should give me 82. Let's see. 1640 divided by 20. 20 times 80 is 1600, so 1640 minus 1600 is 40. 40 divided by 20 is 2. So 80 + 2 = 82. Perfect, that works out. So that confirms that 82 times 20 is indeed 1640.\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods of multiplication\u2014breaking down the numbers, standard algorithm, distributive property\u2014give the same result. Also, verifying by division works. So I can be pretty confident that the product of 82 and 20 is 1640. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. It all lines up. So yeah, the answer should be 1640.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 82 and 20 can be calculated by breaking down 20 into 2 \u00d7 10. First, multiply 82 by 2:\n\n82 \u00d7 2 = 164  \nThen multiply the result by 10:  \n164 \u00d7 10 = 1640  \n\nVerification through division confirms the result:  \n1640 \u00f7 20 = 82  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{1640}",
      "logprobs": null,
