      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 69 and 80. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number multiplied by the second number multiple times. But 69 and 80 are both two-digit numbers, so maybe there's a trick to make it easier instead of doing long multiplication every time. Wait, maybe I can break them down into smaller parts. Let me think.\n\nFirst, 69 is close to 70. If I take 70 and multiply it by 80, that should be straightforward. Then, since 69 is 70 minus 1, I can subtract 1 times 80 from that product. That might work. Let me try that.\n\nSo, 70 times 80. Well, 70 times 8 is 560, right? Because 7 times 8 is 56, and then add a zero. So 70 times 80 is 5600. Then, since 69 is 70 minus 1, I subtract 1 times 80 from 5600. That would be 5600 minus 80. Let me calculate that. 5600 minus 80 is 5520. So, is the product 5520? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way. Let's try that. Multiply 69 by 80. Using the standard algorithm:\n\nFirst, multiply 69 by 0. That's 0. Then, multiply 69 by 80 by breaking it down into 69 times 8 times 10. Let's do 69 times 8 first. 60 times 8 is 480, and 9 times 8 is 72. Adding those together gives 480 + 72 = 552. Then, multiply by 10 to account for the 80, so 552 times 10 is 5520. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that seems right.\n\nWait, another way to check is to use distributive property. 69 times 80 is the same as (70 - 1) times 80. Which is 70*80 - 1*80. Which is 5600 - 80 = 5520. Yep, same answer. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of 80 as 8 times 10. So 69 times 80 is 69 times 8 times 10. Let's compute 69 times 8 first. 60 times 8 is 480, 9 times 8 is 72. So 480 + 72 = 552. Then multiply by 10 to get 5520. Again, same result.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are leading me to 5520. Let me just verify with another approach. Maybe using addition.