      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 30 and 35. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 30 by 35. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 35 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 30. Wait, 35 is the same as 30 plus 5. So maybe I can use the distributive property here. That would mean 30 times 35 is equal to 30 times (30 + 5). Let me write that out: 30 \u00d7 35 = 30 \u00d7 (30 + 5). \n\nNow, distributing the multiplication over addition, that would be 30 \u00d7 30 plus 30 \u00d7 5. Let me calculate each part separately. 30 times 30 is... 900, right? Because 30 multiplied by 30 is like 3 times 3 times 10 times 10, which is 9 times 100, so 900. Then, 30 times 5 is 150. So adding those two results together: 900 + 150. That should be 1050. \n\nWait, let me check if that's correct. Alternatively, maybe I can multiply 30 and 35 directly. Let's see. 30 times 35. Well, 35 times 10 is 350, so 30 times 10 is 300, so 300 times 3 is 900. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, no, hold on. Let me try that again. If I take 35 and multiply by 30, maybe breaking it down differently. 35 is 7 times 5, so 30 times 35 would be 30 times 7 times 5. 30 times 7 is 210, and 210 times 5 is 1050. Oh, that's the same answer as before. So that seems to confirm it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. Multiply 30 by 35. Starting from the rightmost digit. So 35 times 30. Let's write it as:\n\n   30\n\u00d7 35\n-------\n\nStarting with the 5 in the ones place. 30 times 5 is 150. Write down 0 and carry over 1. Then, multiply the 3 in the tens place by 30. 3 times 30 is 90, plus the carried over 1 makes 91. So the result is 9100? Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, maybe I messed up the alignment. Let me try again.\n\nWhen multiplying 30 by 35, it's the same as multiplying 30 by 35. Let's do it step by step. \n\nFirst, multiply 30 by 5. 30 \u00d7 5 = 150. Then, multiply 30 by 30. 30 \u00d7 30 = 900. Then, add those two results together: 150 + 900 = 1050. Oh, right, that's the same as before. So the standard multiplication method also gives 1050. So that must be the correct answer.\n\nWait, but when I tried the standard method earlier, I thought I got 9100. Let me check where I went wrong. Let me write it out properly:\n\n      30\n   \u00d7 35\n   -------\n      150  (30 \u00d7 5)\n     900   (30 \u00d7 30, shifted one position to the left)\n   -------\n    1050\n\nAh, right! Because when you multiply by the tens place (30), you have to shift the result one place to the left, which is why it's 900 instead of 9000. Then adding 150 gives 1050. So that was my mistake earlier; I forgot to shift the second partial product. So the correct answer is 1050.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 30 times 35. Since 35 is 7 times 5, as I thought earlier. So 30 times 7 is 210, and 210 times 5 is 1050. Yep, same result. So all methods point to 1050.\n\nJust to make sure, let me verify with a calculator. Well, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can check with another multiplication. For example, 35 times 30. Let's see, 35 times 10 is 350, so 35 times 30 is 350 times 3, which is 1050. Exactly. So that's another way to confirm.\n\nTherefore, I'm pretty confident that the product of 30 and 35 is 1050. All different methods of multiplication\u2014breaking down the numbers, distributive property, standard multiplication, and cross-checking with another multiplication strategy\u2014result in the same answer. So I don't think I made any mistakes here. The key was remembering to shift the second partial product in the standard multiplication method, which I initially forgot, but then corrected. But even after correcting that, all methods led to the same result. So yeah, 1050 is the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 30 and 35 is \\boxed{1050}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 30 and 35, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 35 = 30 \\times (30 + 5)\n\\]\n\nDistributing the multiplication over addition:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 35 = 30 \\times 30 + 30 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 30 = 900\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 5 = 150\n\\]\n\nAdding the results together:\n\n\\[\n900 + 150 = 1050\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 30 \\\\\n\\times 35 \\\\\n\\hline\n 150 \\quad \\text{(30 \\times 5)} \\\\\n900 \\quad \\text{(30 \\times 30, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n1050 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 30 and 35 is \\(\\boxed{1050}\\).",
