      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 22. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 90 by 22. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 90 times 20 would be easier, and then add 90 times 2. Let me check. 90 times 20 is 1800, because 90 times 2 is 180, so times 10 is 1800. Then 90 times 2 is 180. So adding those together: 1800 + 180. That should be 1980. Wait, is that right?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it the standard way. Let's write it out:\n\n   90\nx 22\n-----\n\nStarting from the right, multiply 90 by 2. That's 180. Then multiply 90 by 20. Wait, but in the second line, it's shifted one position to the left. So 90 times 20 is 1800. Then add 180 and 1800. 1800 + 180 is 1980. Yeah, same result.\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe using distributive property. 22 is 20 + 2, so 90*20 + 90*2. Which is 1800 + 180 = 1980. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do it digit by digit. Let's do 90*22. Let's ignore the zeros. 9*2=18, write down 8 carry over 1. Then 9*2=18 plus the carried 1 is 19. So that's 19, but since we ignored two zeros, we need to add two zeros. So 1900. Wait, that doesn't match. Wait, maybe I did that wrong.\n\nWait, no. Wait, 90 is 9*10. 22 is 2*10 + 2. So 9*10*2*10 + 9*10*2. Which is 9*2*10*10 + 9*2*10. So that's 18*100 + 18*10 = 1800 + 180 = 1980. So that works. But when I tried breaking down 90 into 9 and 10, maybe I messed up the digit multiplication.\n\nWait, let's try multiplying 90 and 22 step by step:\n\nFirst, multiply 22 by 9. 22*9. 20*9=180, 2*9=18. 180+18=198. Then multiply 22 by 0. That's 0. Then add them together: 198 + 0 = 1980. Oh, that's another way. So 22*90 is 1980. So that's the same answer. So all methods lead to 1980. So I think that's correct.\n\nWait, but when I first thought of 90*22, I thought maybe 9*2=18, then 9*20=180, then 0*2=0 and 0*20=0. But that's not the right way. Wait, no. Wait, actually, when multiplying two-digit numbers, you can do it as (9*2) and (0*20). But 9*2=18, 0*20=0. Then combine them? That seems confusing. Maybe that's not the right approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's do that again:\n\n     90\n   x 22\n   ----\n     180  (90 x 2)\n   1800   (90 x 20, shifted one position)\n   ----\n   1980\n\nYes, adding 180 and 1800 gives 1980. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator, I need to verify another way. Let's see, 90 times 20 is 1800, 90 times 2 is 180. 1800 + 180 is 1980. Yep. So all methods confirm that the product is 1980.\n\nI think that's solid. So the answer should be 1980.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 90 and 22 is \\boxed{1980}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 90 and 22, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 90 \\times 20 \\):\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 20 = 1800\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\( 90 \\times 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 2 = 180\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two steps above:\n   \\[\n   1800 + 180 = 1980\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  90 \\\\\n\\times 22 \\\\\n\\hline\n  180 \\quad \\text{(90 x 2)} \\\\\n1800 \\quad \\text{(90 x 20, shifted one position)} \\\\\n\\hline\n1980 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 90 and 22 is \\(\\boxed{1980}\\).",
