      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 19 and 44. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. So, 19 times 44 would be 19 added to itself 44 times. But that seems like a lot. Maybe there's a smarter way to do this without having to do all that addition.\n\nWait, I think I can use the distributive property here. The distributive property is a(b + c) = ab + ac. Maybe I can break down 44 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 19. Let me try that. 44 can be split into 40 and 4. So, 19 times 40 plus 19 times 4. Let me write that out:\n\n19 \u00d7 40 + 19 \u00d7 4\n\nOkay, now let's compute each part separately. First, 19 multiplied by 40. Hmm, 19 times 4 is 76, right? Because 20 times 4 is 80, and 19 is one less, so 80 minus 4 is 76. Then, 76 times 10 is 760. So, 19 \u00d7 40 is 760.\n\nNow, the other part is 19 times 4. That's straightforward. 19 times 4 is 76. So, adding those two results together: 760 + 76. Let me do that addition. 760 plus 70 is 830, and then plus 6 more is 836. So, 19 times 44 is 836. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it the standard multiplication method. Let's try that as a cross-check. Multiplying 19 by 44. Starting with the units digit: 4 times 19. 4 times 10 is 40, and 4 times 9 is 36. So, 40 plus 36 is 76. That's the units digit. Then, since 44 is two digits, we move to the tens place. But since we already multiplied by 4, we need to multiply by 40 (because 4 times 10 is 40) and add a zero. So, 19 times 40 is 760, and then adding a zero gives 7600. Wait, no, that doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe I confused the steps.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by two digits, you first multiply the first number by the tens digit, then multiply the first number by the units digit, and then add the two results. So, 19 times 44. The tens digit is 4 and the units digit is 4. So, 19 times 4 (tens place) is 76, and 19 times 4 (units place) is also 76. Then, since the tens place is worth ten times, you add 760 (from 76 times 10) and 76. So, 760 + 76 is 836. That matches my previous result. Okay, so that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's see. 19 is close to 20, so maybe I can use 20 times 44 and then subtract 1 times 44. Let's try that. 20 times 44 is 880. Then, subtract 44 from that. 880 minus 44. Let's compute that. 880 minus 40 is 840, then minus 4 more is 836. Again, the same answer. So, that's three different methods giving me 836. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, let me do the multiplication step by step. 19 times 44. Let's write it out:\n\n   19\n\u00d7 44\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 19 by 4 (units digit of 44). 19 \u00d7 4 = 76. Write down 6 and carry over 7.\n\nThen, multiply 19 by 4 (tens digit of 44). 19 \u00d7 4 = 76. Add the carryover 7, so 76 + 7 = 83. So, the result is 836. Yep, that's the same as before. So, all methods lead to 836. I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using prime factors. Let's see. 19 is a prime number, so it's just 19. 44 can be factored into 4 and 11. So, 19 \u00d7 4 \u00d7 11. 19 \u00d7 4 is 76, and 76 \u00d7 11. Hmm, 76 \u00d7 10 is 760, plus 76 is 836. Again, same result. So, all methods converge to 836.\n\nI think that's thorough enough. All different approaches confirm that the product of 19 and 44 is 836. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verifying with multiple methods gives me confidence. So, the answer should be 836.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 19 and 44 is \\boxed{836}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 19 and 44, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n19 \\times 44 = 19 \\times (40 + 4)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(19 \\times 40\\):\n\\[\n19 \\times 40 = (20 - 1) \\times 40 = 20 \\times 40 - 1 \\times 40 = 800 - 40 = 760\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\(19 \\times 4\\):\n\\[\n19 \\times 4 = 76\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n760 + 76 = 836\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 19 \\\\\n\\times 44 \\\\\n\\hline\n 76 \\quad \\text{(19 \\times 4)} \\\\\n760 \\quad \\text{(19 \\times 40)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 836 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method involves breaking down 44 into 40 and 4, then adjusting for the tens place:\n\\[\n20 \\times 44 = 880 \\\\\n880 - 44 = 836\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 19 and 44 is \\(\\boxed{836}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
