      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 99 and 18. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be tricky, especially when the numbers are close to 100. Maybe there's a way to break this down to make it easier. Let me think.\n\nFirst, 99 is just 100 minus 1, right? So maybe I can use that. If I have 99 times 18, that's the same as (100 - 1) times 18. Let me write that out: 100*18 - 1*18. That should work because of the distributive property. Let me check that. Yeah, (a - b)(c) = a*c - b*c. So that's correct.\n\nCalculating 100 times 18 first. Well, 100 times anything is just that number with an extra zero. So 100*18 is 1800. Then subtract 1*18, which is 18. So 1800 minus 18. Let me do that subtraction. 1800 minus 10 is 1790, then minus 8 more would be 1782. So is the answer 1782? Let me verify that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication directly. Let's try 99*18. Let's do it the standard way. Multiply 99 by 8 first, then add 99 by 10. Wait, that's another way. Because 18 is 10 + 8. So 99*10 is 990, and 99*8 is... let's calculate 99*8. 100*8 is 800, minus 1*8 is 8, so 800 - 8 = 792. Then add 990 + 792. Let's see, 990 + 700 is 1690, then plus 92 is 1782. Yep, same result. So that's good. Both methods give me 1782. So that seems right.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can check with another method. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 99 multiplied by 18. So:\n\n   99\nx  18\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 99 by 8. 99*8. Let's do 100*8 = 800, minus 1*8 = 8, so 800 - 8 = 792. Then multiply 99 by 10, which is 990. Then add 792 and 990. 792 + 990. Let's see, 700 + 900 = 1600, 92 + 90 = 182. So 1600 + 182 = 1782. Yep, same answer again. So that's three different methods, all leading to 1782. I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use decimal equivalents. But 99 and 18 are both whole numbers, so converting to decimals might not help here. But just for fun, 99 is approximately 100, and 18 is 18. 100*18 is 1800, subtract 1*18 (since 99 is 100 -1), so 1800 - 18 = 1782. Same result. So even estimation checks out.\n\nAnother way: Maybe use the fact that 99 is 9*11 and 18 is 2*9. So 99*18 = 9*11*2*9. Then combine the 9s: 9*9 = 81, and then 11*2 = 22. So 81*22. Let's compute that. 80*22 = 1760, and 1*22 = 22, so 1760 + 22 = 1782. Again, same answer. Wow, that's four different methods now. All leading to 1782. I think that's definitely the right answer.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe do the multiplication straight. Let's do 99*18. Let's do 99*10=990, 99*8=792. Then 990 + 792. Let's add the hundreds: 900 + 700 = 1600. Then the tens: 90 + 90 = 180. Then the ones: 0 + 2 = 2. So 1600 + 180 = 1780, plus 2 is 1782. Yep, same result. So even breaking it down into components works.\n\nI think that's all the methods I can think of. All of them confirm that the product is 1782. So I'm pretty confident that's the correct answer. Let me just write it out again to make sure I didn't transpose any numbers. 99*18. 99*10=990, 99*8=792. 990+792. 990+700=1690, 1690+92=1782. Yep, that's right. So the answer is 1782.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 99 and 18 is \\boxed{1782}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 99 and 18, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   99 \\times 18 = (100 - 1) \\times 18 = 100 \\times 18 - 1 \\times 18\n   \\]\n   Calculating each part:\n   \\[\n   100 \\times 18 = 1800 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1 \\times 18 = 18\n   \\]\n   Subtracting the results:\n   \\[\n   1800 - 18 = 1782\n   \\]\n\n2. **Direct multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   99 \\times 18 = 99 \\times (10 + 8) = 99 \\times 10 + 99 \\times 8\n   \\]\n   Calculating each part:\n   \\[\n   99 \\times 10 = 990 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 99 \\times 8 = 792\n   \\]\n   Adding the results:\n   \\[\n   990 + 792 = 1782\n   \\]\n\n3. **Breaking down into prime factors:**\n   \\[\n   99 \\times 18 = (9 \\times 11) \\times (2 \\times 9) = 9 \\times 9 \\times 11 \\times 2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 9 = 81 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 11 \\times 2 = 22\n   \\]\n   Multiplying the results:\n   \\[\n   81 \\times 22 = 1782\n   \\]\n\n4. **Standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    99 \\\\\n   \\times 18 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    792 \\quad \\text{(99 \\times 8)} \\\\\n   990 \\quad \\text{(99 \\times 10)} \\\\\n   \\hline\n   1782 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 99 and 18 is \\(\\boxed{1782}\\).",
