      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 19 and 75. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. So, 19 times 75 would be 19 added to itself 75 times. But that seems like a lot. Maybe there's a smarter way to do this without having to do all that addition.\n\nWait, I think I can use the distributive property here. The distributive property is a(b + c) = ab + ac. Maybe I can break down 75 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 19. Let me think. 75 is the same as 70 + 5, right? So, if I split 75 into 70 and 5, then the problem becomes 19 times 70 plus 19 times 5. That might be easier.\n\nLet me write that out: 19 \u00d7 70 + 19 \u00d7 5. Now, calculating each part separately. First, 19 times 70. Hmm, 19 times 70 is the same as 19 times 7 times 10. Because multiplying by 10 is easy, you just add a zero at the end. So, 19 times 7. Let me calculate that. 19 times 7... 10 times 7 is 70, 9 times 7 is 63, so 70 + 63 is 133. Then, multiply by 10 gives 1330. Okay, so 19 times 70 is 1330.\n\nNow, the other part is 19 times 5. That's simpler. 19 times 5. 10 times 5 is 50, and 9 times 5 is 45. So, 50 + 45 is 95. So, 19 times 5 is 95.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 1330 + 95. Let's see, 1330 + 95. 1330 + 90 is 1420, and then add 5 more to get 1425. So, 1330 + 95 = 1425.\n\nTherefore, the product of 19 and 75 is 1425. Let me check if that makes sense. If I do 19 times 70, which is 1330, and 19 times 5 is 95, adding them gives 1425. Alternatively, maybe I can check using another method. For example, 19 times 75. Let me think of another way. 75 is 3 times 25, so maybe 19 times 3 times 25. Let's try that. 19 times 3 is 57, then 57 times 25. Hmm, 57 times 25. Well, 50 times 25 is 1250, and 7 times 25 is 175. So, 1250 + 175 is 1425. That's the same result. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's see. 19 multiplied by 75. Starting from the rightmost digit. 5 times 19. 5 times 19 is 95. Write down 5 and carry over 9. Then, 7 times 19. 7 times 19 is 133. But since we already multiplied the 5, we need to add the carryover. So, 133 + 90 (from the carryover 9 times 10) is 142. Wait, that seems confusing. Let me do it step by step.\n\nMultiplying 19 by 75:\n\nFirst, write 19\n\u00d7 75\n--------\n\nStart with the units digit: 5\n5 \u00d7 19 = 95. Write down 5, carry over 9.\n\nNext, the tens digit: 7\n7 \u00d7 19 = 133. But since we already have a carryover of 9, it's 133 + 9 = 142. Write down 42, carry over 1.\n\nNow, there are no more digits to multiply, so we write the carryover 1.\n\nSo, putting it all together: 1425. Yep, that's the same result. So that confirms it.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the fact that 19 is 20 - 1. So, 20 times 75 is 1500, then subtract 1 times 75, which is 75. So, 1500 - 75 = 1425. That works too. That's a nice quick method. Instead of multiplying 19 directly, think of it as 20 - 1, multiply by 75, then subtract 75. That gives the same answer.\n\nSo, all methods lead to 1425. Therefore, I'm confident that the product of 19 and 75 is 1425.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 19 and 75 is \\boxed{1425}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 19 and 75, we can use the distributive property. \n\nFirst, break down 75 into 70 and 5:\n\\[\n19 \\times 75 = 19 \\times (70 + 5)\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate each part separately:\n\\[\n19 \\times 70 = 1330 \\quad \\text{(since } 19 \\times 7 = 133 \\text{ and } 133 \\times 10 = 1330\\text{)}\n\\]\n\\[\n19 \\times 5 = 95 \\quad \\text{(since } 19 \\times 5 = 95\\text{)}\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n1330 + 95 = 1425\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by recognizing that \\(75 = 3 \\times 25\\):\n\\[\n19 \\times 75 = 19 \\times 3 \\times 25 = (19 \\times 3) \\times 25\n\\]\n\\[\n19 \\times 3 = 57 \\quad \\text{(since } 19 \\times 3 = 57\\text{)}\n\\]\n\\[\n57 \\times 25 = 1425 \\quad \\text{(since } 57 \\times 25 = 1425\\text{)}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 19 and 75 is \\(\\boxed{1425}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
