0 is 2110, then subtract 4 more to get 2106. Yep, that works too.\n\nSo, multiple methods all leading to 2106. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all different approaches converge on the same number. So, I think the product of 54 and 39 is 2106.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 54 and 39 is \\boxed{2106}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 54 and 39, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Method:**\n   - Multiply 54 by 9: \n     \\[\n     54 \\times 9 = 486\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 54 by 30 (considering the tens place of 39):\n     \\[\n     54 \\times 30 = 1620\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     486 + 1620 = 2106\n     \\]\n\n2. **Breaking Down Numbers:**\n   - Decompose 54 into 50 + 4 and 39 into 40 - 1:\n     \\[\n     (50 + 4)(40 - 1) = 50 \\times 40 - 50 \\times 1 + 4 \\times 40 - 4 \\times 1\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 2000 - 50 + 160 - 4 = 2106\n     \\]\n\n3. **Factoring Method:**\n   - Factorize 54 as 6 \u00d7 9 and 39 as 3 \u00d7 13:\n     \\[\n     (6 \\times 9) \\times (3 \\times 13) = 6 \\times 9 \\times 3 \\times 13\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = (6 \\times 3) \\times (9 \\times 13) = 18 \\times 117 = 2106\n     \\]\n\n4. **Vertical Addition Check:**\n   - Break down the multiplication and add:\n     \\[\n     50 \\times 39 + 4 \\times 39 = 1950 + 156 = 2106\n     \\]\n\n5. **Subtraction Method:**\n   - Calculate 54 \u00d7 40 and subtract 54:\n     \\[\n     54 \\times 40 = 2160\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2160 - 54 = 2106\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 54 and 39 is \\(\\boxed{2106}\\).",
