      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 57 and 69. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the method where you split the numbers into tens and ones. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, let's take 57. If I split that into tens and ones, that's 50 + 7. Similarly, 69 can be split into 60 + 9. So, the problem becomes multiplying 50 by 60, 50 by 9, 7 by 60, and 7 by 9, and then adding all those results together. Let me write that out:\n\n57 \u00d7 69 = (50 + 7) \u00d7 (60 + 9)\n= 50\u00d760 + 50\u00d79 + 7\u00d760 + 7\u00d79\n\nAlright, let's compute each part step by step.\n\nFirst, 50 multiplied by 60. That's straightforward: 50\u00d760 = 3000.\n\nNext, 50 multiplied by 9. Hmm, 50\u00d79 is the same as 5\u00d79\u00d710, right? So 5\u00d79 is 45, then times 10 is 450. So that's 450.\n\nThen, 7 multiplied by 60. That's 7\u00d760. Well, 7\u00d76 is 42, so 42\u00d710 is 420. So that's 420.\n\nLastly, 7 multiplied by 9. That's 63. Okay, so 63.\n\nNow, adding all those together: 3000 + 450 + 420 + 63.\n\nLet me add them step by step. First, 3000 + 450. That's 3450. Then, 3450 + 420. Let's see, 3450 + 400 is 3850, then plus 20 is 3870. Then, 3870 + 63. Hmm, 3870 + 60 is 3930, and then +3 is 3933.\n\nWait, so does that mean the product of 57 and 69 is 3933? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 57 by 69. Let's do it the standard way:\n\nFirst, multiply 57 by 9 (the ones digit of 69). 57\u00d79. Let's compute that. 50\u00d79=450, 7\u00d79=63. So 450+63=513. That's the ones place.\n\nThen, multiply 57 by 60 (the tens digit of 69). But wait, actually, in standard multiplication, you might do it as:\n\n        57\n      \u00d7 69\n      ----\n        57\u00d79 (ones place)\n      57\u00d760 (tens place)\n      ----\n\nBut let me write it properly:\n\n        57\n      \u00d7 69\n      ----\n        57\u00d79\n      + 57\u00d760 (since 69 is 9 + 60)\n      ----\n\nSo first, 57\u00d79=513. Then, 57\u00d760. 57\u00d760 is the same as 57\u00d76\u00d710. 57\u00d76: 50\u00d76=300, 7\u00d76=42. So 300+42=342. Then, 342\u00d710=3420.\n\nNow, add 513 and 3420. Let's do that. 513 + 3420. 500 + 3400 = 3900. 13 + 20 = 33. So total is 3900 + 33 = 3933. Okay, same result as before. So that's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using a calculator. Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but maybe I can recall that 57\u00d770 is 3990, and then subtract 57 to get 3990 - 57 = 3933. Let me verify that. 57\u00d770: 57\u00d77=399, so 399\u00d710=3990. Then, 3990 - 57. 3990 - 50=3940, then -7=3933. Yep, that works too.\n\nSo all methods point to the product being 3933. Therefore, I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 57 and 69 is \\boxed{3933}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 57 and 69, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n57 \\times 69 = (50 + 7) \\times (60 + 9)\n\\]\n\nBreaking this down into parts:\n\n\\[\n= 50 \\times 60 + 50 \\times 9 + 7 \\times 60 + 7 \\times 9\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 60 = 3000\n\\]\n\\[\n50 \\times 9 = 450\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 60 = 420\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 9 = 63\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n3000 + 450 + 420 + 63 = 3933\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by breaking down the multiplication step-by-step:\n\n\\[\n57 \\times 69 = 57 \\times (9 + 60) = 57 \\times 9 + 57 \\times 60\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n\\[\n57 \\times 9 = 513\n\\]\n\\[\n57 \\times 60 = 3420\n\\]\n\nAdding these results:\n\n\\[\n513 + 3420 = 3933\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 57 and 69 is \\(\\boxed{3933}\\).",
