hrice 153, four times 204, five times 255, six times 306, seven times 357, eight times 408, nine times 459, ten times 510. Oh, there we go. Ten times 51 is 510. So that's another confirmation. \n\nSo all methods point to the product being 510. I think that's solid. No mistakes in my reasoning here. So the answer should be 510.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 51 and 10 is found by multiplying the two numbers. Since multiplying by 10 appends a zero to the product of the non-zero digits, \\(51 \\times 10 = 510\\). Verification through addition (\\(51 + 51 + \\dots + 51\\) ten times) also confirms the result.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{510}",
