      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 79 and 88. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down, but I'm not sure if that's the easiest way here. Let me think. Alternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write 79 multiplied by 88. Let me set it up like this:\n\n  79\n\u00d7 88\n-------\n\nNow, I need to multiply 79 by 80 and then by 8, and then add those two results together. That's because 88 is 80 + 8. So, 79 times 80 plus 79 times 8.\n\nStarting with 79 times 80. Hmm, multiplying by 80 is the same as multiplying by 8 and then by 10, right? So, 79 times 8 first, then times 10. Let me calculate 79 \u00d7 8.\n\nBreaking down 79 \u00d7 8: 70 \u00d7 8 is 560, and 9 \u00d7 8 is 72. Adding those together: 560 + 72 = 632. So, 79 \u00d7 80 is 632 \u00d7 10, which is 6320. Okay, that part seems straightforward.\n\nNow, the other part is 79 \u00d7 8. Let me do that. 70 \u00d7 8 is 560, and 9 \u00d7 8 is 72. Adding those gives 560 + 72 = 632. So, 79 \u00d7 8 is 632.\n\nNow, add the two results together: 6320 + 632. Let's see. 6320 + 600 is 6920, and then adding 32 more gives 6952. So, 79 \u00d7 88 equals 6952. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can verify using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nMultiplying 79 by 88:\n\nFirst, multiply 79 by 8. As before, 70\u00d78=560, 9\u00d78=72, total 632.\n\nThen multiply 79 by 80. Which is 79\u00d78\u00d710=632\u00d710=6320.\n\nAdding 6320 and 632: 6320 + 600 = 6920, then +32 = 6952. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property differently. Let's see, 79 \u00d7 88 can be thought of as (80 - 1) \u00d7 (80 + 8). Hmm, but that might complicate things. Let me try expanding it:\n\n(80 - 1)(80 + 8) = 80\u00d780 + 80\u00d78 - 1\u00d780 - 1\u00d78.\n\nCalculating eac