952. Which is the same as before. So, the exact answer is 6952. So, the estimation was wrong earlier. Maybe I miscalculated.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the difference method isn't straightforward here. But regardless, all the exact methods give me 6952, so I think that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check by adding 79 and 88 first. 79 + 88 = 167. Then, if I square 167, but that's not helpful. Wait, no, that's not the product. The product is 79*88, which is not the square. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another multiplication technique. Let's try multiplying 79 by 88 step by step:\n\nStart with 79\n\nMultiply by 88:\n\nFirst, multiply 79 by 8: 79\u00d78=632\n\nThen, multiply 79 by 80: 79\u00d780=6320\n\nAdd them: 6320 + 632 = 6952. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm with the minuend and multiplier:\n\n      79\n   \u00d7 88\n   -------\n      632  (79 \u00d7 8)\n   6320   (79 \u00d7 80, shifted one position to the left)\n   -------\n    6952\n\nYes, that's the standard multiplication layout. So, 632 \u00d7 10 is 6320, then adding 632 gives 6952. All right, so that's consistent.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the fact that 88 is 11\u00d78, so 79\u00d711\u00d78. Let's compute 79\u00d711 first. 79\u00d710=790, plus 79=790+79=869. Then, 869\u00d78. Let's compute that:\n\n800\u00d78=6400\n\n60\u00d78=480\n\n9\u00d78=72\n\nAdding those: 6400 + 480 = 6880; 6880 +72=6952. Again, same result.\n\nSo, all different methods of breaking down the multiplication lead to the same answer, 6952. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 79 and 88 is 6952.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 79 and 88 is \\boxed{6952}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 79 and 88, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Break down 88 into 80 and 8.\n2. Multiply 79 by 80:\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 80 = (70 \\times 80) + (9 \\times 80) = 5600 + 720 = 6320\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 79 by 8:\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 8 = (70 \\times 8) + (9 \\times 8)