y is to do 34*10 + 34*3, which gives 340 + 102 = 442. So both methods give the same result. Therefore, the product should be 442.\n\nWait, but let me verify with another method. Maybe using addition. 34 multiplied by 13. Let's do it step by step. 34 times 10 is 340. Then 34 times 3 is 102. Adding those together: 340 + 100 is 440, then plus 2 is 442. Yep, that's the same. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 13 is 100 divided by 7.5, but that seems more complicated. Alternatively, maybe breaking down 13 into 5 + 8. So 34*5 is 170, and 34*8 is 272. Then adding 170 + 272. 170 + 200 is 370, plus 72 is 442. Again, same result. So all methods point to 442. \n\nWait, but just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can do the multiplication on paper the standard way. Let's write it out:\n\n   34\nx 13\n-----\nFirst, multiply 34 by 3 (the units digit of 13). 34*3 is 102. Write down 02 and carry over 1.\nThen, multiply 34 by 1 (the tens digit of 13). 34*1 is 34. Write down 34. Now add the two results: 102 and 34. 102 + 34 is 136. Wait, that's different. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, when you do the standard multiplication, you first multiply by the units digit, then multiply by the tens digit, and then add. But in this case, the first step is 34*3=102, which is the 2 in the units place and carry over 1. Then, 34*1=34, but since it's the tens place, you write it as 340. Then add 102 and 340. 102 + 340 is 442. Oh, right! So I made a mistake earlier when I thought I added 340 + 1020. That was incorrect. The correct way is to write 34*1 as 34, then add a zero to make it 340. Then add 340 + 102. So that's 442. So that's correct. \n\nTherefore, all methods confirm that the product of 34 and 13 is 442. I think that's solid. So the answer should be 442.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 34 and 13 is \\boxed{442}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 34 and 13, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n34 \\times 13 = (30 + 4) \\times (10 + 3)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(30 \\times 10) + (30 \\times 3) + (4 \\times 10) + (4 \\times 3)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part step by step:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 10 = 300\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 3 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\times 10 = 40\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\times 3 = 12\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n300 + 90 + 40 + 12 = 442\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 34 \\\\\n\\times \\, 13 \\\\\n\\hline\n 102 \\quad \\text{(34 \\times 3)} \\\\\n340 \\quad \\text{(34 \\times 10)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 442 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the partial products:\n\n\\[\n102 + 340 = 442\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 34 and 13 is \\(\\boxed{442}\\).",
