      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 42 and 75. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. But 42 times 75 might be a bit tricky. Maybe there's a smarter way than just brute-forcing the multiplication.\n\nWait, I think I can break down 75 into 70 and 5. That might make it easier. So, 42 multiplied by 70 plus 42 multiplied by 5. Let me write that out:\n\n42 \u00d7 70 + 42 \u00d7 5\n\nAlright, let's calculate each part separately. First, 42 times 70. I know that multiplying by 70 is the same as multiplying by 7 and then by 10. So, 42 times 7. Let me do that step by step. 40 times 7 is 280, and 2 times 7 is 14. Adding those together gives 280 + 14 = 294. Then, multiply by 10 to get 70, so 294 \u00d7 10 = 2940. So, 42 \u00d7 70 is 2940.\n\nNow, the other part is 42 times 5. That's simpler. 40 times 5 is 200, and 2 times 5 is 10. Adding those gives 200 + 10 = 210. So, 42 \u00d7 5 is 210.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 2940 + 210. Let's see, 2940 + 200 is 3140, and then add the remaining 10 to get 3150. So, 42 \u00d7 75 equals 3150. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. Multiply 42 by 75. Starting from the rightmost digit, 5. 42 times 5 is 210. Write down 1 and carry over 2. Then, multiply the left digit, which is 7, by 42. But since it's the tens place, it's actually 420. So, 42 times 7 is 294, plus the carry-over 2 makes 296. So, putting it all together, we have 210 and 296. Wait, that seems confusing. Let me write it out step by step.\n\nMultiplying 42 by 75:\n\n      42\n    \u00d7 75\n    ----\n      210  (42 \u00d7 5)\n    294   (42 \u00d7 7, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n    3150\n\nYes, that makes sense. The first partial product is 210 (from multiplying 42 by 5), and the second partial product is 2940 (from multiplying 42 by 70, shifted one position left because 75 is 70 + 5, and the 70 is in the tens place). Adding 210 and 2940 gives 3150. So that checks out.\n\nAnother way to verify could be using addition. 75 added 42 times. Let's see, 75 + 75 is 150, so 42 times 75 is double that plus 42 times 75 - 150. Wait, maybe that's not helpful. Alternatively, 75 times 40 is 3000, and 75 times 2 is 150. So 3000 + 150 = 3150. That works too. So, 75 \u00d7 40 = 3000, 75 \u00d7 2 = 150, sum them to get 3150. Yep, same answer.\n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to 3150. So, the product of 42 and 75 is 3150. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 42 and 75 is \\boxed{3150}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 42 and 75, we can break down 75 into 70 and 5, then use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n42 \\times 75 = 42 \\times (70 + 5) = (42 \\times 70) + (42 \\times 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(42 \\times 70\\):\n\\[\n42 \\times 70 = 42 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 294 \\times 10 = 2940\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(42 \\times 5\\):\n\\[\n42 \\times 5 = 210\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n2940 + 210 = 3150\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n42 & \\\\\n\\times 75 & \\\\\n\\hline\n210 & \\quad \\text{(42 \\times 5)} \\\\\n2940 & \\quad \\text{(42 \\times 70, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n3150 & \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 42 and 75 is \\(\\boxed{3150}\\).",
