      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 34 and 19. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. So, 34 times 19 would be 34 added to itself 19 times. But that seems like a lot of addition. Maybe there's a smarter way to do this without having to do 19 separate additions.\n\nWait, I think there's the distributive property that might help here. The distributive property is a(b + c) = ab + ac. But how can I apply that here? Let me think. If I can break down 19 into two numbers that multiply with 34 and then add the results together, that might work. Let's try breaking down 19 into 10 and 9. So, 34 times 10 plus 34 times 9. That should be easier.\n\nFirst, 34 times 10. Well, multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end, right? So 34 times 10 is 340. Then, 34 times 9. Hmm, 34 times 10 is 340, so if I subtract 34 from that, I'll get 34 times 9. Let me check: 340 minus 34 is 306. So, 34 times 9 is 306. Then, adding those two results together: 340 plus 306. Let's do that step by step. 340 plus 300 is 640, and then plus 6 more is 646. So, 34 times 19 is 646. Let me verify that with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n   34\nx 19\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 34 by 9. 34 times 9. Let's do 30 times 9 and 4 times 9. 30 times 9 is 270, and 4 times 9 is 36. Adding those together: 270 + 36 = 306. Then, multiply 34 by 10 (since 19 is 10 + 9, but you have to remember to shift the result one place to the left because it's the tens place). So 34 times 10 is 340. Now, add 340 and 306. 340 + 300 is 640, plus 6 is 646. Yep, same result. So that seems to check out.\n\nWait, another way to think about it: 34 is close to 30, so maybe 30 times 19 is 570, and then add 4 times 19. 4 times 19 is 76. So 570 + 76 is 646. That works too. So three different methods all give me 646. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nAlternatively, using prime factors. Let's see. 34 is 2 times 17, and 19 is a prime number. So 2 times 17 times 19. Multiplying 2 and 17 first gives 34, then 34 times 19. Which again is 646. So that's the same as before.\n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to 646. So I can be pretty sure that's the right answer. Let me just do a quick check with a calculator in my head. 34 times 20 is 680. Subtract 34 from that, which is 680 - 34 = 646. Yep, that works. So that's another way to see it. 34 times 19 is 34 times (20 - 1) which is 680 - 34 = 646. Perfect.\n\nSo, after all these different methods and checks, I'm pretty confident that the product of 34 and 19 is 646. I don't think I made any mistakes here because all the steps line up and cross-verification works. So the answer should be 646.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 34 and 19 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n34 \\times 19 = 34 \\times (10 + 9) = (34 \\times 10) + (34 \\times 9)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(34 \\times 10 = 340\\)\n- \\(34 \\times 9 = 306\\)\n\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n340 + 306 = 646\n\\]\n\nVerification using alternative methods (e.g., \\(34 \\times 20 - 34\\)) confirms the result. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{646}",
