    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 44 and 56. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by adding the number to itself a certain number of times. But 44 times 56 seems a bit tricky. Maybe there's a trick to make it easier instead of just brute-forcing the multiplication.\n\nWait, 44 and 56... I notice that 44 is 4 times 11, and 56 is 7 times 8. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me write that down:\n\n44 \u00d7 56 = (4 \u00d7 11) \u00d7 (7 \u00d7 8)\n\nFirst, I can rearrange the multiplication because multiplication is commutative and associative. So, maybe group 4 and 7 together, and 11 and 8 together. Let's try that:\n\n(4 \u00d7 7) \u00d7 (11 \u00d7 8)\n\nCalculating 4 \u00d7 7 is straightforward, that's 28. Then 11 \u00d7 8... Hmm, 10 \u00d7 8 is 80, and 1 \u00d7 8 is 8, so 80 + 8 = 88. So now we have 28 \u00d7 88. Hmm, that's still a two-digit times a two-digit number. Maybe there's a way to break this down further.\n\nWait, 28 is 20 + 8. So, 28 \u00d7 88 can be written as (20 + 8) \u00d7 88. Let's distribute that:\n\n20 \u00d7 88 + 8 \u00d7 88\n\nCalculating 20 \u00d7 88: 20 times 80 is 1600, and 20 times 8 is 160, so 1600 + 160 = 1760.\n\nThen 8 \u00d7 88: 8 times 80 is 640, and 8 times 8 is 64, so 640 + 64 = 704.\n\nNow add those two results together: 1760 + 704. Let's see, 1760 + 700 is 2460, and then +4 more is 2464. So, 28 \u00d7 88 = 2464. Therefore, 44 \u00d7 56 = 2464.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's verify using another method. Maybe the difference of squares formula? I remember that (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2, but here we have 44 and 56. Hmm, 44 and 56 are 12 apart? Wait, 56 - 44 = 12. Not sure if that helps. Alternatively, maybe think of 44 as 50 - 6 and 56 as 60 - 4. Let's try that:\n\n(50 - 6)(60 - 4) = 50\u00d760 - 50\u00d74 - 6\u00d760 + 6\u00d74\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n50\u00d760 = 3000\n\n50\u00d74 = 200\n\n6\u00d760 = 360\n\n6\u00d74 = 24\n\nSo putting it all together: 3000 - 200 - 360 + 24\n\nFirst, 3000 - 200 = 2800\n\n2800 - 360 = 2440\n\n2440 + 24 = 2464\n\nOkay, same result. So that's a good sign. Both methods give 2464. Let me try another way just to be thorough. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 44 by 56:\n\nFirst, multiply 44 by 6 (the units digit of 56). 44 \u00d7 6 = 264. Write down 6 and carry over 264.\n\nThen multiply 44 by 5 (the tens digit of 56). 44 \u00d7 5 = 220. Add the two results: 220 + 264 = 484. But since we multiplied by the tens digit (5), we need to add a zero. So 4840. Wait, but that's not matching. Wait, maybe I messed up the steps.\n\nWait, no. Let me do it step by step.\n\nWhen multiplying 44 by 56, you can do it as:\n\n      44\n    \u00d756\n    ----\n      44 \u00d7 6 (units place)\n      44 \u00d7 5 (tens place, shifted one position to the left)\n\nCalculating 44 \u00d7 6 = 264. Write down 264.\n\nThen 44 \u00d7 5 = 220. Since it's the tens place, we write it as 2200.\n\nNow add 264 and 2200:\n\n264 + 2200 = 2464.\n\nYes, that works. So that's another confirmation. So all methods lead to 2464. Therefore, the product of 44 and 56 is 2464.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 44 and 56 is \\boxed{2464}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 44 and 56, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into simpler steps.\n\nFirst, we express 44 and 56 in terms of their prime factors:\n- \\(44 = 4 \\times 11\\)\n- \\(56 = 7 \\times 8\\)\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n44 \\times 56 = (4 \\times 11) \\times (7 \\times 8) = (4 \\times 7) \\times (11 \\times 8)\n\\]\n\nCalculating the products:\n- \\(4 \\times 7 = 28\\)\n- \\(11 \\times 8 = 88\\)\n\nNext, we multiply the results:\n\\[\n28 \\times 88\n\\]\n\nBreaking down 28 into 20 and 8:\n\\[\n28 \\times 88 = (20 + 8) \\times 88 = 20 \\times 88 + 8 \\times 88\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n- \\(20 \\times 88 = 1760\\)\n- \\(8 \\times 88 = 704\\)\n\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n1760 + 704 = 2464\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 44 by 6 (units digit of 56): \\(44 \\times 6 = 264\\)\n- Multiply 44 by 5 (tens digit of 56): \\(44 \\times 5 = 220\\)\n- Adding the results and shifting the second product one position left: \\(264 + 2200 = 2464\\)\n\nThus, the product of 44 and 56 is \\(\\boxed{2464}\\).",
