      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 21 and 41. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can be done by breaking them down. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication method? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I'll write 21 multiplied by 41. So, 21  \n\u00d7 41  \n------  \nNow, I need to multiply 21 by 1 first. That's straightforward. 21 \u00d7 1 = 21.  \nThen, I have to multiply 21 by the tens place, which is 4. But wait, since it's 40, I need to remember to multiply by 4 and then add a zero at the end. So, 21 \u00d7 4 = 84. Then add a zero: 840.  \nNow, add the two results together: 21 + 840. Let me add those. 21 + 840. Hmm, 20 + 840 is 860, and then add 1 more to get 861. So, does that mean the product is 861? Let me check if that's right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the distributive property. 21 is 20 + 1, and 41 is 40 + 1. So, (20 + 1)(40 + 1) = 20\u00d740 + 20\u00d71 + 1\u00d740 + 1\u00d71. Calculating each term:  \n20\u00d740 = 800  \n20\u00d71 = 20  \n1\u00d740 = 40  \n1\u00d71 = 1  \nNow, add them all up: 800 + 20 = 820; 820 + 40 = 860; 860 + 1 = 861. Okay, same result. That seems to confirm it.\n\nWait, maybe I can do it the standard way vertically to be absolutely sure. Let's line them up:\n\n   21  \n\u00d7 41  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 21 by 1 (the units digit of 41):  \n21 \u00d7 1 = 21.  \nThen, multiply 21 by 4 (the tens digit of 41), which is 40:  \n21 \u00d7 4 = 84.  \nNow, since the 4 is in the tens place, it's 840.  \nAdd 21 and 840:  \n21  \n+840  \n------  \n21 + 840. Let's add the units first: 1 + 0 = 1. Then tens: 2 + 4 = 6. Hundreds: 0 + 8 = 8. So, 861. Yep, same answer.\n\nJust to make sure I didn't make a mistake in addition earlier. Let's add 21 and 840 again. 840 + 20 is 860, then +1 is 861. Correct.\n\nAnother way: Maybe break down 41 into 40 and 1, then use distributive property as I did before. Which I did, and got the same answer. So, all methods point to 861.\n\nWait, maybe I can check with addition. If 21 \u00d7 40 = 840, then 21 \u00d7 41 is 840 + 21 = 861. That works too. So, 21 multiplied by 40 is 840, plus another 21 gives 861. Yep, that's consistent.\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods give the same result. So, the product of 21 and 41 is 861. I can't see any errors in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 21 and 41 is \\boxed{861}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 21 and 41, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 21 by 1 (the units digit of 41):\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 1 = 21\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 21 by 4 (the tens digit of 41), which is 40:\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 4 = 84\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   21 + 840 = 861\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   (20 + 1)(40 + 1) = 20 \\times 40 + 20 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 40 + 1 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 800 + 20 + 40 + 1 = 861\n   \\]\n\nAnother verification method is breaking down 41 into 40 and 1:\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 40 = 840 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 21 \\times 1 = 21\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   840 + 21 = 861\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 21 and 41 is \\(\\boxed{861}\\).",
