      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 32 and 71. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the smaller number multiple times. But 32 and 71 are both two-digit numbers, so maybe breaking them down would help. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can do 32 times 70 and then add 32 times 1, since 71 is 70 plus 1. That might be easier than multiplying them directly. Let me try that. \n\nSo, 32 times 70. Well, 32 times 7 is 224, right? Because 30 times 7 is 210, and 2 times 7 is 14, so 210 +14 =224. Then, since it's 70, not 7, I just add a zero. So 224 times 10 is 2240. Okay, that part makes sense. \n\nNow, 32 times 1 is just 32. So adding those two results together: 2240 +32. Let me add those. 2240 +30 is 2270, and then +2 is 2272. So, is the product 2272? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the standard multiplication method. Let's see. 32 times 71. \n\nStarting with the units place: 2 times 1 is 2. Then, 2 times 7 is 14. So write down 4 and carry over 1. Then, moving to the tens place: 3 times 1 is 3, plus the carried over 1 makes 4. Then, 3 times 7 is 21. So that's 21. So putting it all together: 2142. Wait, that's different from before. Hmm, so now I'm confused. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication. Let me try again. \n\nMultiplying 32 by 71. Let's do it step by step. \n\nFirst, multiply 32 by 1 (the units digit of 71). That's 32. Then, multiply 32 by 7 (the tens digit of 71). But since it's the tens digit, it's actually 70. So 32 times 70. \n\nSo, 32 times 70. Let's compute that. 32 times 7 is 224, so 224 times 10 is 2240. Then, add that to 32 times 1, which is 32. So 2240 +32 =2272. \n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication, I got 2142. That's conflicting. So where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, maybe I confused the digits. Let me try the standard multiplication again. \n\nMultiplying 32 by 71. \n\nStart with the ones place: 1 (from 71) times 32. 1 times 32 is 32. Write that down. \n\nThen, the tens place: 7 (from 71) times 32. But since it's the tens place, it's 70. So 70 times 32. Let's compute that. 70 times 30 is 2100, and 70 times 2 is 140. So 2100 +140 =2240. \n\nNow, add the two results: 32 +2240. That's 2272. \n\nWait, so why did I get 2142 before? Maybe I added the digits incorrectly. Let me check the standard multiplication again. \n\nWait, perhaps I mixed up the digits. Let me write it out properly. \n\nMultiplying 32 by 71:\n\n      32\n   x71\n   ----\n      32  (32 x 1)\n    224   (32 x 70, shifted one position to the left)\n   ----\n    2272\n\nAh, there we go. So when you do the standard multiplication, you write the 32 under 71, then multiply 32 by 1 (units place of 71) to get 32. Then multiply 32 by 7 (tens place of 71), which is 224, and then shift one position to the left (because it's the tens place) to get 2240. Then add 32 and 2240 to get 2272. \n\nSo my initial calculation was correct. The confusion came when I did the standard multiplication in my head incorrectly. So the correct product is 2272. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's see. 32 times 71. \n\nAnother way is to break down 71 into 70 +1, which is what I did first. 32*70=2240, 32*1=32, sum them to get 2272. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use distributive property differently. 32*71 =32*(70+1)=32*70 +32*1=2240 +32=2272. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another multiplication method. Let's try adding 32 multiple times 71 times. But that would take too long. \n\nAlternatively, use calculator-like multiplication. Let's see:\n\n32\nx71\n-----\n32 (32x1)\n224 (32x70, shifted)\n-----\n2272\n\nYes, that's correct. So the product is 2272. \n\nWait, but just to make sure, maybe do the multiplication the long way:\n\n32\nx71\n----\n32 (32 x1)\n224 (32 x7, but shifted one position)\n----\n2272\n\nYes, adding 32 and 2240 gives 2272. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check with another example. For instance, 32*70=2240. Then 32*71=2240+32=2272. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, 32*75=2400, but that's not needed here. \n\nAlternatively, 32*10=320, so 32*71=32*70 +32*1=2240 +32=2272. \n\nYes, that's consistent. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 2272. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 32 and 71 is \\boxed{2272}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 32 and 71, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps. \n\nFirst, we calculate 32 multiplied by 70:\n\\[\n32 \\times 70 = 2240\n\\]\nNext, we calculate 32 multiplied by 1:\n\\[\n32 \\times 1 = 32\n\\]\nAdding these two results together gives:\n\\[\n2240 + 32 = 2272\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 32 & \\\\\n\\times 71 & \\\\\n\\hline\n 32 & \\quad (\\text{32} \\times 1) \\\\\n2240 & \\quad (\\text{32} \\times 70, \\text{ shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n2272 & \\quad (\\text{sum of the results}) \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 32 and 71 is \\(\\boxed{2272}\\).",
