      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 47 and 96. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done step by step, but maybe there's a quicker way to do it mentally. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 96 into smaller parts. I know that 96 is 90 plus 6. So, if I split 96 into 90 and 6, then the multiplication becomes 47 times 90 plus 47 times 6. That might be easier because multiplying by 90 and 6 separately could be simpler. Let me write that down:\n\n47 \u00d7 96 = 47 \u00d7 (90 + 6) = (47 \u00d7 90) + (47 \u00d7 6)\n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. Starting with 47 \u00d7 90. Hmm, 47 times 90. Well, 47 times 10 is 470, right? So if I take that and multiply by 9, since 90 is 9 times 10, then 470 \u00d7 9. Let me calculate that. \n\n470 \u00d7 9: 400 \u00d7 9 is 3600, and 70 \u00d7 9 is 630. Adding those together: 3600 + 630 = 4230. So, 47 \u00d7 90 is 4230. Got that part.\n\nNow, the other part is 47 \u00d7 6. Let me do that. 40 \u00d7 6 is 240, and 7 \u00d7 6 is 42. Adding those gives 240 + 42 = 282. So, 47 \u00d7 6 is 282.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 4230 + 282. Let's see. 4230 + 200 is 4430, and then adding 82 more gives 4430 + 82 = 4512. So, adding them together gives 4512.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. So, 47 \u00d7 90: 47 \u00d7 9 is 423, so 423 \u00d7 10 is 4230. That's correct. Then 47 \u00d7 6 is 282. Adding 4230 + 282. Let's do it step by step. 4230 + 200 = 4430. Then 4430 + 80 = 4510, and then +2 = 4512. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm to verify. \n\nMultiplying 47 by 96. Let's do it the standard way:\n\n      47\n    \u00d7 96\n    ----\n      282  (47 \u00d7 6)\n    4230   (47 \u00d7 9, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n    4512\n\nAdding 282 and 4230 gives 4512. Yep, same result. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using distributive property in another way. Let's see, 47 \u00d7 96. Hmm, 96 is 80 + 16. So, 47 \u00d7 80 + 47 \u00d7 16. Let's try that.\n\n47 \u00d7 80: 47 \u00d7 8 is 376, so 376 \u00d7 10 = 3760.\n\n47 \u00d7 16: 47 \u00d7 10 = 470, 47 \u00d7 6 = 282. So 470 + 282 = 752.\n\nAdding 3760 + 752. Let's compute that. 3760 + 700 = 4460, then +52 = 4512. Same answer. So that works too.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the fact that 47 \u00d7 96 = 47 \u00d7 (100 - 4) = 47 \u00d7 100 - 47 \u00d7 4 = 4700 - 188 = 4512. Oh, that's a nice way too. Let me check that. 47 \u00d7 100 is 4700. 47 \u00d7 4 is 188. So 4700 - 188. 4700 - 200 would be 4500, but since it's 188, which is 12 less than 200, so 4500 + 12 = 4512. Correct.\n\nSo all methods lead to 4512. Therefore, the product of 47 and 96 is 4512. I think that's solid. Let me just verify with a calculator to be absolutely sure. Well, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can check with another multiplication method.\n\nAlternatively, 47 \u00d7 96. Let's compute 47 \u00d7 96:\n\n47\n\u00d796\n----\n282\n4230\n----\n4512\n\nYes, same as before. So, all methods confirm that the product is 4512. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 47 and 96 is \\boxed{4512}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 47 and 96, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down 96 into 90 and 6:**\n   \\[\n   47 \\times 96 = 47 \\times (90 + 6) = (47 \\times 90) + (47 \\times 6)\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(47 \\times 90\\):\n     \\[\n     47 \\times 90 = 47 \\times 9 \\times 10 = 423 \\times 10 = 4230\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(47 \\times 6\\):\n     \\[\n     47 \\times 6 = 282\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     4230 + 282 = 4512\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   \\[\n     47 \\\\\n   \\times 96 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     282 \\quad (\\text{47} \\times 6) \\\\\n   4230 \\quad (\\text{47} \\times 9, \\text{ shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     4512\n     \\]\n\n3. **Using the distributive property with 80 and 16:**\n   \\[\n   47 \\times 96 = 47 \\times (80 + 16) = 47 \\times 80 + 47 \\times 16\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(47 \\times 80\\):\n     \\[\n     47 \\times 80 = 3760\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(47 \\times 16\\):\n     \\[\n     47 \\times 16 = 752\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     3760 + 752 = 4512\n     \\]\n\n4. **Using the fact that \\(96 = 100 - 4\\):**\n   \\[\n   47 \\times 96 = 47 \\times (100 - 4) = 4700 - 188 = 4512\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 47 and 96 is \\(\\boxed{4512}\\).",
