      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 56 and 20. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 56 by 20. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 56 times 20. Well, 20 is the same as 2 times 10. So maybe I can do 56 times 2 first and then multiply that result by 10. That might be easier. Let me try that.\n\n56 times 2. Let's do the multiplication. 50 times 2 is 100, and 6 times 2 is 12. So adding those together, 100 plus 12 is 112. So 56 times 2 is 112. Then I need to multiply that by 10. 112 times 10 is straightforward, just moving the decimal point one place to the right. So 112 times 10 is 1120. So that would be the product of 56 and 20. Let me check if that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it the standard way, multiplying 56 by 20. Let's write it out:\n\n   56\nx  20\n-------\n\nStarting from the right, multiply 56 by 0 (the units place of 20), which is 0. Then multiply 56 by 2 (the tens place of 20), which is 112. Then since the 2 is in the tens place, we add a zero to make it 1120. Yeah, that matches what I got before. So that seems right.\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe using distributive property. 56 times 20 is the same as 56 times (2 times 10). Which is the same as (56 times 2) times 10. Which again is 112 times 10, which is 1120. So all methods point to the same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using addition. 20 times 56. If I add 56 twenty times. But that would take longer. Let's see, 56 plus 56 is 112. Then 112 plus 56 is 168, and so on. But doing that 20 times would take time. Maybe I can do it in pairs. 56 plus 56 is 112, which is 2 times 56. Then 112 plus 56 is 168, which is 3 times 56. Then 168 plus 56 is 224, which is 4 times. Continuing this way would take too long. Alternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one, I need to rely on my previous calculations.\n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate. 56 is close to 60, and 60 times 20 is 1200. Since 56 is 4 less than 60, the difference is 4. So 4 less than 1200 would be 1200 minus (4 times 20). 4 times 20 is 80. So 1200 minus 80 is 1120. That works too. So that's another way to approximate and check. So 1120 is the answer.\n\nWait, let me check once more. 56 times 20. Let's do 56 times 10 first. 56 times 10 is 560. Then double that, because 20 is 10 times 2. So 560 times 2 is 1120. Yep, that's the same result. So all different methods give me 1120. So I think that's correct.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. Each step checks out. So the product of 56 and 20 is 1120.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 56 and 20 can be calculated by breaking down 20 into 2 \u00d7 10. Multiplying 56 by 2 first:\n\n56 \u00d7 2 = 112  \nThen multiplying by 10:  \n112 \u00d7 10 = 1120  \n\nVerification using different methods (standard multiplication, distributive property, approximation) all confirm the result.  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{1120}",
