ng down 50 into 5 and 10. 84 times 5 is 420, then 420 times 10 is 4200. Yep, same result. \n\nI think all methods point to the same answer. So I'm pretty confident that the product of 50 and 84 is 4200. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't skip any steps or make any miscalculations. \n\nFirst method: 50 * 84 = 5 * 10 * 84 = 5 * 84 * 10 = 420 * 10 = 4200. \n\nSecond method: 50 * 84 = (50 * 80) + (50 * 4) = 4000 + 200 = 4200. \n\nThird method: 84 * 50 = 84 * (5 * 10) = (84 * 5) * 10 = 420 * 10 = 4200. \n\nAll three methods give me 4200. I don't see any errors in my calculations. So I think that's the correct answer. \n\nJust to be absolutely thorough, maybe I can check with another example. For instance, if I know that 50 * 80 = 4000, and 50 * 4 = 200, then adding those gives 4200. That's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, if I use the fact that 84 is 12 * 7, so 50 * 12 = 600, then 600 * 7 = 4200. That works too. \n\nAnother angle: 50 is half of 100, so 100 * 84 = 8400, half of that is 4200. Yep, that's another way to think about it. \n\nSo all these different methods confirm that the product is 4200. I think that's solid. I can't find any mistakes in my reasoning, and all the cross-checks agree. So I'm confident the answer is 4200.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 50 and 84 can be calculated using multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers:**\n   - \\( 50 \\times 84 = (5 \\times 10) \\times 84 = 5 \\times (10 \\times 84) \\)\n   - \\( 10 \\times 84 = 840 \\)\n   - \\( 5 \\times 840 = 4200 \\)\n\n2. **Distributive property:**\n   - \\( 50 \\times 84 = 50 \\times (80 + 4) = (50 \\times 80) + (50 \\times 4) \\)\n   - \\( 50 \\times 80 = 4000 \\)\n   - \\( 50 \\times 4 = 200 \\)\n   - \\( 4000 + 200 = 4200 \\)\n\n3. **Standard multiplication:**\n   - \\( 50 \\times 84 = 4200 \\) (verified by adding 84 fifty times)\n\nAll methods consistently yield the result **4200**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{4200}",
