, not 64. So that was my error. So that entire calculation was wrong. So that's why the two methods gave different results. So that was a critical mistake. So scratch that method. So the correct way is to do 64*37 as 64*(30 +7)=1920 +448=2368. So that's the correct answer.\n\nBut then why did I think 64 is 80 -6? Maybe a brain fart. So that was a mistake. So the correct answer is 2368. Let me verify with another method. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiply 64 by 37:\n\nFirst, multiply 64 by 7:\n\n64\nx  7\n-----\n448\n\nThen multiply 64 by 30 (note the zero):\n\n64\nx 30\n-----\n1920\n\nNow add them together:\n\n  448\n+1920\n-----\n2368\n\nYes, that's correct. So the product is 2368. So my initial mistake was thinking of 64 as 80 -6, which is wrong. So the correct answer is 2368.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a smarter way to compute 64*37. Let's see. 64 is 60 +4. So 60*37 +4*37=2220 +148=2368. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, 37 is 40 -3. So 64*(40 -3)=64*40 -64*3=2560 -192=2368. Yep, same answer.\n\nSo the correct product is 2368. The earlier method with (80 -6) was wrong because 80 -6 is 74, not 64. So that was a miscalculation. So the answer is 2368.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 64 and 37 is \\boxed{2368}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 64 and 37, we can use the distributive property. \n\nFirst, break down 37 into 30 and 7:\n\\[\n64 \\times 37 = 64 \\times (30 + 7)\n\\]\n\nNext, distribute the multiplication:\n\\[\n64 \\times 30 + 64 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\\[\n64 \\times 30 = 1920\n\\]\n\\[\n64 \\times 7 = 448\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n1920 + 448 = 2368\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 64 and 37 is \\(\\boxed{2368}\\).",
