      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 11 and 54. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 11 by 54. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 11 times 50 plus 11 times 4. Because 54 is 50 plus 4. That might be easier than multiplying them together all at once. Let me try that.\n\nSo, 11 times 50. Hmm, 11 times 5 is 55, so 11 times 50 should be 550. Wait, is that right? Let me check. 11 multiplied by 50. Yeah, because 10 times 50 is 500, and 1 times 50 is 50, so 500 plus 50 is 550. That seems correct.\n\nThen, 11 times 4. That's straightforward. 11 times 4 is 44. Right? Because 10 times 4 is 40, and 1 times 4 is 4, so 40 plus 4 is 44.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 550 plus 44. Let me do that. 550 plus 40 is 590, and then plus 4 more is 594. So, 550 plus 44 equals 594. Therefore, 11 times 54 should be 594. Let me verify that with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  11\nx 54\n----\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit. 11 times 4 is 44. Write down 4 and carry over 4. Then, 11 times 5 is 55, plus the carried over 4 makes 59. So the result is 594. Yep, that matches what I got before. So that's good.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can check using addition. If I add 11 fifty-four times, that should equal 594. But that seems tedious. Let me see. 11 plus 11 is 22. 22 plus 11 is 33. 33 plus 11 is 44. 44 plus 11 is 55. 55 plus 11 is 66. 66 plus 11 is 77. 77 plus 11 is 88. 88 plus 11 is 99. 99 plus 11 is 110. 110 plus 11 is 121. 121 plus 11 is 132. 132 plus 11 is 143. 143 plus 11 is 154. 154 plus 11 is 165. 165 plus 11 is 176. 176 plus 11 is 187. 187 plus 11 is 198. 198 plus 11 is 209. 209 plus 11 is 220. 220 plus 11 is 231. 231 plus 11 is 242. 242 plus 11 is 253. 253 plus 11 is 264. 264 plus 11 is 275. 275 plus 11 is 286. 286 plus 11 is 297. 297 plus 11 is 308. 308 plus 11 is 319. 319 plus 11 is 330. 330 plus 11 is 341. 341 plus 11 is 352. 352 plus 11 is 363. 363 plus 11 is 374. 374 plus 11 is 385. 385 plus 11 is 396. 396 plus 11 is 407. 407 plus 11 is 418. 418 plus 11 is 429. 429 plus 11 is 440. 440 plus 11 is 451. 451 plus 11 is 462. 462 plus 11 is 473. 473 plus 11 is 484. 484 plus 11 is 495. 495 plus 11 is 506. 506 plus 11 is 517. 517 plus 11 is 528. 528 plus 11 is 539. 539 plus 11 is 550. 550 plus 11 is 561. 561 plus 11 is 572. 572 plus 11 is 583. 583 plus 11 is 594. Wow, that took a while, but it checks out. So adding 11 a total of 54 times gives 594. So that confirms it.\n\nAnother way could be using distributive property differently. For example, 54 is 60 minus 6. So 11 times 60 is 660, and 11 times 6 is 66. Then subtract 660 minus 66. 660 minus 60 is 600, minus 6 more is 594. That works too. So that's another verification.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using prime factors. Let's see. 11 is a prime number, so it's just 11. 54 is 6 times 9, which is 2 times 3 to the third power. So 11 times 2 times 3^3. But that might not be helpful here. But just for fun, multiplying those together: 11*2=22, 22*3=66, 66*3=198, 198*3=594. Yep, same result.\n\nSo all methods lead to 594. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Each step checks out with different methods. So I'm confident the product of 11 and 54 is 594.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 11 and 54 is \\boxed{594}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 11 and 54, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\(11 \\times 50\\):\n   \\[\n   11 \\times 50 = 550\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\(11 \\times 4\\):\n   \\[\n   11 \\times 4 = 44\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two steps above:\n   \\[\n   550 + 44 = 594\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  11 \\\\\n\\times 54 \\\\\n\\hline\n  44 \\quad \\text{(11 \\times 4)} \\\\\n550 \\quad \\text{(11 \\times 50, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n  594 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nVerification by adding 11 fifty-four times (though tedious, confirms the result):\n\\[\n\\text{Adding 11 fifty-four times results in 594.}\n\\]\n\nVerification using distributive property:\n\\[\n54 = 60 - 6 \\implies 11 \\times 60 - 11 \\times 6 = 660 - 66 = 594\n\\]\n\nVerification using prime factors:\n\\[\n11 \\times (2 \\times 3^3) = 11 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 594\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 11 and 54 is \\(\\boxed{594}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
