d 30 x 46 + 2 x 46. \n\n30 x 46 is 1380, 2 x 46 is 92. 1380 + 92 is 1472. \n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication, I thought 46 x 3 is 138, which is correct, but then added 138 to 92. Wait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, the first partial product is 92 (from 46 x 2), and the second partial product is 138 shifted left by one (so 1380). Then adding 92 + 1380 gives 1472. \n\nSo why did I get confused earlier? Because when I first did the second method, I thought of 32 as 30 + 2, so 30 x 46 + 2 x 46. 30 x 46 is 1380, 2 x 46 is 92. 1380 + 92 is 1472. \n\nBut when I tried the second method again, I thought I had 192 + 128, which was wrong. Wait, no. Wait, maybe I miscalculated. Let me try again. \n\nWait, if I do 32 x 46 as (30 + 2) x 46, that's 30x46 + 2x46. \n\n30x46: 30x40=1200, 30x6=180. 1200+180=1380. \n\n2x46: 92. \n\n1380 + 92: 1380 + 90 = 1470, 1470 + 2 = 1472. \n\nSo that's correct. \n\nEarlier, when I tried to do the standard multiplication, I thought I had 192 + 128, but that was a mistake. Wait, no. Let me check again. \n\nWait, if I do 32 x 46, standard multiplication:\n\nMultiply 46 by 2: 92. \n\nMultiply 46 by 3: 138. \n\nThen add them: 92 + 138. \n\nBut 92 + 138 is 230. Wait, that's conflicting. \n\nWait, no. Wait, 92 is the result of multiplying 46 by the units digit (6), but in reality, when multiplying 32 by 46, you have to do 46 x 2 and 46 x 3, then add. \n\nBut 46 x 2 is 92, and 46 x 3 is 138. Then adding 92 + 138 gives 230. But that contradicts the other method. \n\nWait, so which is correct? \n\nWait, no, hold on. There's a confusion here. Let me clarify. \n\nIn the standard multiplication algorithm, when you multiply by the tens digit (4 in 46), you have to shift the partial product one position to the left. So 46 x 3 is 138, but since it's the tens digit, it's 1380. Then adding 1380 + 92 gives 1472. \n\nBut if you do it as (30 + 2) x 46, you get 30x46 + 2x46 = 1380 + 92 = 1472. \n\nBut if you do 46 x 3 first, which is 138, and then 46 x 2 is 92, and then adding 138 + 92, which is 230. Wait, that can't be. \n\nWait, no. Wait, 46 x 3 is 138, which is the same as 46 x 3.0. But 32 is 30 + 2, so 30 x 46 + 2 x 46. \n\nBut 30 x 46 is 1380, 2 x 46 is 92. 1380 + 92 is 1472. \n\nBut if you do 46 x 3 first, which is 138, and then 46 x 2 is 92. Then adding 138 + 92 gives 230. But that's not the same as 1472. \n\nWait, so there's a mistake here. How can that be? \n\nWait, no. Wait, 32 x 46 is 1472. So why does the second method give 230? \n\nAh, I see. The confusion is because when you do (30 + 2) x 46, you have to compute 30 x 46 and 2 x 46, then add. But 30 x 46 is 1380, and 2 x 46 is 92. 1380 + 92 is 1472. \n\nBut if you do 46 x 3 first, which is 138, and then 46 x 2 is 92. Then adding 138 + 92 gives 230. But that's not the same. \n\nWait, but 32 is 30 + 2. So 32 x 46 = (30 + 2) x 46 = 30 x 46 + 2 x 46. \n\nBut 30 x 46 is 1380, and 2 x 46 is 92. 1380 + 92 is 1472. \n\nBut if you do 46 x 3 first, which is 138, and then 46 x 2 is 92. Then adding 138 + 92 gives 230. But that's not the same. \n\nWait, so why is there a discrepancy? \n\nWait, no. Wait, 32 x 46 is 1472. So the correct answer is 1472. The mistake in the second method was that when I did 32 as 30 + 2, I thought of multiplying 30 x 46 and 2 x 46, but then added them. But 30 x 46 is 1380, and 2 x 46 is 92. 1380 + 92 is 1472. \n\nBut when I tried to do the standard multiplication, I thought of multiplying 46 x 3 and 46 x 2, then adding. But 46 x 3 is 138, and 46 x 2 is 92. Then adding 138 + 92 gives 230. But that's wrong. \n\nWait, but 32 x 46 is 1472. So why does the standard multiplication give 230? \n\nWait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, the first partial product is 92 (from 46 x 2), and the second partial product is 138 shifted left by one (so 1380). Then adding 92 + 1380 gives 1472. \n\nAh, so I made a mistake earlier when I thought the standard multiplication was giving 230. Actually, when you do the standard multiplication, you have to shift the second partial product. So 46 x 3 is 138, which is the same as 1380 when shifted left by one. Then adding 92 + 1380 gives 1472. \n\nSo both methods give the same result. My confusion came from not properly shifting the partial product in the standard multiplication. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 1472. \n\nBut let me verify with another method. Let's use the distributive property again. \n\n32 x 46 = (30 + 2)(40 + 6) = 30x40 + 30x6 + 2x40 + 2x6 = 1200 + 180 + 80 + 12 = 1200 + 180 is 1380, plus 80 is 1460, plus 12 is 1472. \n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, using the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. \n\nSo 30x40=1200, 30x6=180, 2x40=80, 2x6=12. Adding all together: 1200 + 180 + 80 + 12 = 1472. \n\nTherefore, the product of 32 and 46 is 1472. \n\nBut just to make sure, let me check with a calculator. \n\n32 multiplied by 46. \n\n32 x 40 = 1280, 32 x 6 = 192. 1280 + 192 = 1472. \n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nSo the answer is 1472. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 32 and 46 is \\boxed{1472}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 32 and 46, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n32 \\times 46 = (30 + 2)(40 + 6)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(30 \\times 40) + (30 \\times 6) + (2 \\times 40) + (2 \\times 6)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 40 = 1200\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 6 = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\times 40 = 80\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\times 6 = 12\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n1200 + 180 + 80 + 12 = 1472\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 46 by 2 (units digit of 32):\n   \\[\n   46 \\times 2 = 92\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 46 by 3 (tens digit of 32), then shift the result one position to the left (since it's the tens digit):\n   \\[\n   46 \\times 3 = 138 \\quad \\text{(shifted left to make 1380)}\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   92 + 1380 = 1472\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 32 and 46 is 