      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 12 and 63. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 12 by 63. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 12 times 60 would be 720, because 12 times 6 is 72, so times 10 is 720. Then, 12 times 3 is 36. So if I add those together, 720 plus 36 is 756. Wait, does that make sense? Let me check another way to verify.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's write 12 multiplied by 63. Multiplying 2 times 3 is 6, write down 6 and carry over 0. Then, 2 times 6 is 12, plus the carried 0 is 12. So that gives 126. Wait, that's not right. Wait, no, hold on. Maybe I mixed up the digits. Let me try again.\n\nActually, when multiplying 12 by 63, it's like multiplying 12 by 60 and 12 by 3, then adding them. So 12*60=720 and 12*3=36. Then 720+36=756. That seems correct. But when I tried the standard multiplication, I thought I got 126. Wait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication steps.\n\nLet me try the standard multiplication again. Let's do it properly. Multiply 12 by 63. \n\nStarting with the units digit: 3. Multiply 3 by 12. 3*12=36. Write down 6, carry over 3. \n\nNext, the tens digit: 6. Multiply 6 by 12. 6*12=72. Add the carried 3, which gives 75. So the result is 756. Oh, right! I must have messed up the order before. So both methods give 756. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using addition. 12 added 63 seven hundred fifty-six times? That doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Alternatively, maybe use distributive property differently. 12*63 can be thought of as (10+2)*63 = 10*63 + 2*63 = 630 + 126 = 756. Yep, that works too. So all methods point to 756. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe do the multiplication vertically. \n\n   12\nx 63\n-----\n   36  (12 x 3)\n 72   (12 x 6, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n 756\n\nYes, that's correct. The 36 is the units place, and the 72 is the tens place, so adding them gives 756. \n\nAnother way: 63 times 12. Let's see, 63 times 10 is 630, 63 times 2 is 126. 630 + 126 is 756. Yep, same result. \n\nI think that's solid. All different methods confirm that the product is 756. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 12 and 63 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Breaking it down:\n\n12 \u00d7 63 = (12 \u00d7 60) + (12 \u00d7 3)  \n= 720 + 36  \n= 756\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication method:\n```\n    12\n  \u00d7 63\n -----\n    36  (12 \u00d7 3)  \n  72   (12 \u00d7 6, shifted one position left)  \n  -----\n  756  (Sum the results)  \n```\n\nAll methods confirm the result is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
