      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 27 and 31. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 27 by 31. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can just do the multiplication straight out. 27 times 30 is a common number, and then add 27 more. Wait, that might work. Let me try that. 27 times 30. Well, 27 times 10 is 270, so 27 times 30 should be 270 times 3, which is 810. Then, adding 27 to that because 31 is 30 plus 1. So 810 plus 27. Let me add those together. 810 + 20 is 830, and then +7 is 837. So is the product 837?\n\nWait, let me check another way to make sure. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n   27\nx 31\n-----\nFirst, multiply 27 by 1. That's 27.\nThen, multiply 27 by 30. That's 810.\nNow, add them together: 27 + 810. 27 + 800 is 827, plus 10 is 837. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 31 into 30 + 1 and multiplying each by 27, then adding. Which is what I did first. So that gives the same answer. Let me try another method just to be thorough. Maybe using distributive property differently.\n\n27 times 31 can be thought of as 27 times (30 + 1) which is 27*30 + 27*1. Which again is 810 + 27 = 837. Same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using addition multiple times. 27 added 31 times. But that would be tedious. Let's see, 27 + 27 is 54, plus 27 is 81, plus 27 is 108, plus 27 is 135, plus 27 is 162, plus 27 is 189, plus 27 is 216, plus 27 is 243, plus 27 is 270, plus 27 is 297, plus 27 is 324, plus 27 is 351, plus 27 is 378, plus 27 is 405, plus 27 is 432, plus 27 is 459, plus 27 is 486, plus 27 is 513, plus 27 is 540, plus 27 is 567, plus 27 is 594, plus 27 is 621, plus 27 is 648, plus 27 is 675, plus 27 is 702, plus 27 is 729, plus 27 is 756, plus 27 is 783, plus 27 is 810, plus 27 is 837. Wow, that's a lot of additions, but it still ends up at 837. So that confirms it again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, I need to rely on my mental math. Let me see. 27 times 30 is 810, as before. Then 27 times 1 is 27. So 810 + 27 is 837. Yep, that's consistent.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the fact that 27 is 3 cubed and 31 is a prime number? Not sure if that helps. 27 times 31 is 3^3 * 31. But unless I need to factor it further, that might not be helpful here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the formula (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2, but that's for difference of squares. Doesn't apply here. How about other algebraic identities? Maybe not necessary here.\n\nAlternatively, breaking down 31 into 20 + 11. So 27*20 + 27*11. Let's compute that. 27*20 is 540. 27*11. Hmm, 27*10 is 270, plus 27 is 297. So 540 + 297. 540 + 200 is 740, plus 97 is 837. Again, same answer. So that works too.\n\nAlternatively, 27*31. Let's see, 27*3 is 81, so 27*30 is 810, as before. Then 27*1 is 27. So 810 +27=837. All methods lead to the same answer.\n\nI think that's thorough enough. All different methods of breaking down the multiplication lead to 837, so I can be confident that's the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning, and multiple checks confirm the result. So yeah, the product of 27 and 31 is 837.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 27 and 31 is \\boxed{837}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 27 and 31, we can use the multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   27 \\\\\nx 31 \\\\\n\\hline\n   27 \\quad \\text{(27 multiplied by 1)} \\\\\n 810 \\quad \\text{(27 multiplied by 30)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 837 \\quad \\text{(Adding the results from the two steps above)} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBreaking down the multiplication step-by-step:\n1. Calculate \\(27 \\times 30\\):\n   \\[\n   27 \\times 30 = 810\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\(27 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   27 \\times 1 = 27\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   810 + 27 = 837\n   \\]\n\nAnother method involves breaking down 31 into 30 and 1:\n1. Calculate \\(27 \\times 30\\):\n   \\[\n   27 \\times 30 = 810\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\(27 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   27 \\times 1 = 27\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   810 + 27 = 837\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 27 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{837}\\).",
