      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 74 and 95. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think. \n\nFirst, 74 times 95. If I break 95 into 90 and 5, then the equation becomes 74 times 90 plus 74 times 5. That might be easier to handle. Let me write that out:\n\n74 \u00d7 95 = 74 \u00d7 (90 + 5) = (74 \u00d7 90) + (74 \u00d7 5)\n\nAlright, now I need to calculate each part separately. Let's start with 74 \u00d7 90. Hmm, 74 times 90. Well, 74 times 10 is 740, right? So if I take 740 and multiply by 9, that should give me the answer. Let me check:\n\n74 \u00d7 90 = 74 \u00d7 9 \u00d7 10. First, 74 \u00d7 9. Let's do that step by step. 70 \u00d7 9 is 630, and 4 \u00d7 9 is 36. Adding those together gives 630 + 36 = 666. Then multiply by 10, so 666 \u00d7 10 = 6660. Okay, so 74 \u00d7 90 is 6660. That seems right.\n\nNow the other part: 74 \u00d7 5. That's simpler. 70 \u00d7 5 is 350, and 4 \u00d7 5 is 20. Adding those gives 350 + 20 = 370. So 74 \u00d7 5 is 370.\n\nNow add those two results together: 6660 + 370. Let's see. 6660 + 300 is 6960, and then add 70 more to get 7030. So 6660 + 370 = 7030. Therefore, 74 \u00d7 95 should be 7030. \n\nWait, let me verify that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using another method like the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that.\n\nMultiplying 74 by 95. Let's do it the standard way:\n\nFirst, write 74\nMultiply by 95\nSo, 74\n\u00d7 95\n--------\nMultiply 74 by 5: 74 \u00d7 5 = 370. Write down 70 and carry over 3.\nThen multiply 74 by 90: 74 \u00d7 90 = 6660. Write down 6660. Now add the two parts together: 70 + 6660 = 6730? Wait, that doesn't match my previous result. Hmm, did I do something wrong here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, when you do the standard multiplication, you actually split the multiplication into two steps: multiply by the units digit and then multiply by the tens digit, shifted one position to the left. So let me try again.\n\nFirst, multiply 74 by 5 (the units digit of 95). 74 \u00d7 5 = 370. Write that down.\n\nThen multiply 74 by 9 (the tens digit of 95), which is 90. So 74 \u00d7 9 = 666. Then you write that number one position to the left, so it's 6660.\n\nNow add 370 and 6660. Let's add them:\n\n370\n+6660\n------\nStart from the right: 0 + 0 = 0. 7 + 6 = 13. Carry over 1. 3 + 6 + 1 = 10. So total is 7030. Oh, right! So that's 7030. So my first calculation was correct. I must have misaligned the digits when I first thought about it. So the standard method gives 7030. So that's consistent with the first method. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's see. 74 \u00d7 95. Hmm, 95 is 100 - 5. So maybe (100 - 5) \u00d7 74. Let's try that.\n\n(100 - 5) \u00d7 74 = 100 \u00d7 74 - 5 \u00d7 74 = 7400 - 370 = 7030. Yep, same result. So that's another way to confirm. So that's three different methods, all leading to 7030. So I think that's the correct answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, but let me just do a quick check. 70 \u00d7 95 = 6650, and 4 \u00d7 95 = 380. Then 6650 + 380 = 7030. Yep, that works too. So 70 + 4 = 74, so 70 \u00d7 95 + 4 \u00d7 95 = 7030. Perfect.\n\nSo all methods lead to 7030. Therefore, the product of 74 and 95 is 7030. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. So I'm confident the answer is 7030.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 74 and 95 is \\boxed{7030}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 74 and 95, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n74 \\times 95 = 74 \\times (90 + 5) = (74 \\times 90) + (74 \\times 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(74 \\times 90\\):\n\n\\[\n74 \\times 90 = 74 \\times 9 \\times 10 = (70 \\times 9 + 4 \\times 9) \\times 10 = (630 + 36) \\times 10 = 666 \\times 10 = 6660\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(74 \\times 5\\):\n\n\\[\n74 \\times 5 = 370\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\n\\[\n6660 + 370 = 7030\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by recognizing that \\(95 = 100 - 5\\):\n\n\\[\n(100 - 5) \\times 74 = 100 \\times 74 - 5 \\times 74 = 7400 - 370 = 7030\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 74 and 95 is \\(\\boxed{7030}\\).",
