      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 43 and 100. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 43 by 100. Alright, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I know that multiplying by 100 is the same as multiplying by 10 twice. Because 10 times 10 is 100. So maybe I can do 43 times 10 first, and then multiply the result by another 10. Let me try that.\n\n43 times 10. Well, multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end of the number. So 43 times 10 would be 430. That makes sense. Then, if I take that result, 430, and multiply by 10 again, I just add another zero. So 430 times 10 is 4300. So that would be 43 times 100 equals 4300. \n\nWait, let me check if there's another way to do this to confirm. Maybe breaking down the numbers. 43 is 40 plus 3. So 40 times 100 is 4000, and 3 times 100 is 300. Then adding those together: 4000 plus 300 is 4300. Yep, same answer. That seems right.\n\nAnother way to think about it: 100 is a round number, so maybe using the distributive property. 43 times 100 is the same as (40 + 3) times 100, which is 40*100 + 3*100. Which again is 4000 + 300 = 4300. \n\nI can also verify using a calculator if I have one, but since I don't, maybe I can do another check. Let's see, 43 times 10 is 430, so 43 times 100 should be 10 times that, which is 4300. That's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, thinking about the place values. 43 is in the tens and ones place. 100 is in the hundreds place. So when you multiply them, the product should be in the thousands place. 43 is 40 + 3, so 40*100 is 4000 (four thousands) and 3*100 is 300 (three hundreds). Adding those gives 4300. \n\nIs there any chance I made a mistake here? Let me do the multiplication step by step. 43 times 100. Let's write it out:\n\n\u202f\u00a0\u00a043\n\u00d7\u00a0100\n-------\n\nStarting from the right, multiply 43 by 0 (the ones place), which is 0. Then multiply 43 by 0 (the tens place), which is 0. Then multiply 43 by 1 (the hundreds place), which is 4300. So yes, that works out. \n\nAnother check: 43 multiplied by 100. Since 100 has two zeros, it's equivalent to multiplying by 10 twice. So 43*10=430, then 430*10=4300. Correct. \n\nI think that's solid. All different methods give me 4300. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 43 and 100 can be calculated by recognizing that multiplying by 100 is equivalent to multiplying by 10 twice. \n\nFirst, multiply 43 by 10:\n\\[\n43 \\times 10 = 430\n\\]\nThen, multiply the result by 10 again:\n\\[\n430 \\times 10 = 4300\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, breaking down 43 into 40 and 3:\n\\[\n(40 + 3) \\times 100 = 40 \\times 100 + 3 \\times 100 = 4000 + 300 = 4300\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{4300}\\).",
