      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 27 and 43. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. So, 27 times 43. Let me think. Maybe breaking it down would help. \n\nFirst, I can try breaking down 43 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 27. Let's see, 43 is 40 plus 3. So, 27 times 40 plus 27 times 3. That might be easier than multiplying them together all at once. Let me write that out:\n\n27 \u00d7 40 + 27 \u00d7 3\n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. Starting with 27 times 40. I know that multiplying by 40 is the same as multiplying by 4 and then by 10. So, 27 times 4. Let me calculate that. 27 times 4. 20 times 4 is 80, and 7 times 4 is 28. So, 80 plus 28 is 108. Then, multiplying by 10 gives 1080. So, 27 times 40 is 1080.\n\nNow, the other part is 27 times 3. That's simpler. 27 times 3. 20 times 3 is 60, and 7 times 3 is 21. Adding those together gives 60 + 21 = 81. So, 27 times 3 is 81.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 1080 + 81. Let's do that. 1080 plus 80 is 1160, and then plus 1 more is 1161. So, adding them together gives 1161.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. So, 27 times 40 is 1080. 27 times 3 is 81. Adding 1080 and 81. Let me add the hundreds first. 1000 + 80 is 1080, and then the remaining 80 + 1 is 81. So, 1080 + 81 is indeed 1161. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. Multiply 27 by 43. \n\nStarting with the units digit of 43, which is 3. Multiply 27 by 3. 27 times 3 is 81. So, I write down 1 and carry over 8.\n\nNext, multiply 27 by the tens digit, which is 4. But since it's the tens place, it's actually 40. So, 27 times 4 is 108, plus the carry-over 8 makes 116. So, we write down 6 and carry over 11.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits to multiply, we write down the carry-over 11. So, putting it all together, we have 1161. That matches the previous result. So, that's good. Both methods give the same answer. \n\nAnother way to check could be using addition. 27 added 43 times. Let's see. 27 + 27 is 54. 54 + 27 is 81. 81 + 27 is 108. 108 + 27 is 135. 135 + 27 is 162. 162 + 27 is 189. 189 + 27 is 216. 216 + 27 is 243. 243 + 27 is 270. 270 + 27 is 297. 297 + 27 is 324. 324 + 27 is 351. 351 + 27 is 378. 378 + 27 is 405. 405 + 27 is 432. 432 + 27 is 459. 459 + 27 is 486. 486 + 27 is 513. 513 + 27 is 540. 540 + 27 is 567. 567 + 27 is 594. 594 + 27 is 621. 621 + 27 is 648. 648 + 27 is 675. 675 + 27 is 702. 702 + 27 is 729. 729 + 27 is 756. 756 + 27 is 783. 783 + 27 is 810. 810 + 27 is 837. 837 + 27 is 864. 864 + 27 is 891. 891 + 27 is 918. 918 + 27 is 945. 945 + 27 is 972. 972 + 27 is 999. 999 + 27 is 1026. 1026 + 27 is 1053. 1053 + 27 is 1080. 1080 + 27 is 1107. 1107 + 27 is 1134. 1134 + 27 is 1161. \n\nWait, that's a lot of additions, but it confirms that adding 27 forty-three times gives 1161. So, all methods point to the same answer. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 27 and 43 is 1161.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 27 and 43 is \\boxed{1161}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 27 and 43, we can break down 43 into 40 and 3, then multiply each by 27 and add the results.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(27 \\times 40\\):\n\\[\n27 \\times 40 = 27 \\times 4 \\times 10 = 108 \\times 10 = 1080\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(27 \\times 3\\):\n\\[\n27 \\times 3 = 81\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n1080 + 81 = 1161\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 27 by 3 (units digit of 43): \\(27 \\times 3 = 81\\) (write down 1, carry over 8)\n- Multiply 27 by 4 (tens digit of 43, which is 40): \\(27 \\times 4 = 108\\), add the carry-over 8: \\(108 + 8 = 116\\) (write down 6, carry over 11)\n- Write down the final carry-over 11.\n\nThis gives the result 1161.\n\nThus, the product of 27 and 43 is \\(\\boxed{1161}\\).",
