t, you have to shift one position. So maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nLet me try again. When multiplying 17 by 15:\n\nStart with the ones digit: 7 \u00d7 15. 7 \u00d7 5 is 35. Write down 5, carry over 3.\n\nThen 7 \u00d7 1 (tens place) is 7. Add that to the carryover: 35. So that's 35. Then move to the next digit. Wait, maybe I need to do it step by step.\n\nWait, actually, the standard multiplication is:\n\n      15\n\n\u00d7    17\n\n----------\n\nFirst, multiply 15 by 7:\n\n15 \u00d7 7 = 105. Write down 05 and carry over 1.\n\nThen multiply 15 by 1 (tens place):\n\n15 \u00d7 1 = 15. Add the carryover 1: 15 + 1 = 16.\n\nNow combine the two results: 05 and 16. So 05 is 5, and 16 is 16. So 5 + 16 = 21. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no, maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nWait, no, actually, when you do multiplication, you write the 15 as:\n\n      15\n\n\u00d7    17\n\n----------\n\nFirst, multiply 15 by 7 (units place of 17):\n\n15 \u00d7 7 = 105. So you write 05 and carry over 1.\n\nThen multiply 15 by 1 (tens place of 17):\n\n15 \u00d7 1 = 15. Add the carryover 1: 15 + 1 = 16.\n\nNow, since the 17 is a two-digit number, the second product (15 \u00d7 1) is shifted one place to the left. So 15 \u00d7 17 is 105 + 150 = 255. Wait, that's the same as before. So maybe I confused myself in the steps, but the result is still 255. So that's consistent.\n\nOkay, so all methods point to 255. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just verify with a calculator in my mind. 15 times 10 is 150, 15 times 7 is 105. 150 + 105 is 255. Yep, that's right. So I'm confident the product of 15 and 17 is 255.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 15 and 17 is \\boxed{255}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 15 and 17, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n15 \\times 17 = (10 + 5)(10 + 7)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 10 \\times 10 + 10 \\times 7 + 5 \\times 10 + 5 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 10 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n10 \\times 7 = 70\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\times 10 = 50\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\times 7 = 35\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n100 + 70 + 50 + 35 = 255\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n15 \\\\\n\\times 17 \\\\\n\\hline\n105 \\quad \\text{(15 \u00d7 7)} \\\\\n150 \\quad \\text{(15 \u00d7 10)} \\\\\n\\hline\n255 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 15 and 17 is \\(\\boxed{255}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
