 86*12 = (80 + 6)*12 = 80*12 + 6*12. Let's compute that. 80*12 is 960, 6*12 is 72. Then 960 + 72 is 1032. Again, same answer. So all methods point to 1032.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me verify with a calculator. Well, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can check with another multiplication method. Let's try adding 86 twelve times. But that's tedious. Alternatively, maybe use another decomposition. 12 is 3*4. So 86*3*4. Let's compute 86*3 first. 80*3 is 240, 6*3 is 18, so 240 + 18 = 258. Then 258*4. 200*4=800, 50*4=200, 8*4=32. So 800 + 200 = 1000, 1000 + 32 = 1032. Again, same result. So that's four different methods all leading to 1032. I think that's solid.\n\nTherefore, the product of 86 and 12 is 1032. I feel confident about that now. I just need to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors in my head. Let me check once more with the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n      86\n    x 12\n    ----\n      172  (Multiply 86 by 2)\n    86    (Multiply 86 by 1, shifted one position)\n    ----\n    1032\n\nYes, adding 172 and 860 (which is 86 shifted left by one) gives 1032. All right, that seems correct. So the answer is 1032.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 86 and 12 is \\boxed{1032}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 86 and 12, we can break down 12 into 10 and 2, then multiply and add the results:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 86 \\times 10 \\):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 10 = 860\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 86 \\times 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 2 = 172\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results from the two steps above:\n   \\[\n   860 + 172 = 1032\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 86 by 2 (units digit of 12):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 2 = 172\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 86 by 1 (tens digit of 12), shifting the result one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 1 = 86 \\quad \\text{(shifted to the left becomes 860)}\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   172 + 860 = 1032\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 86 and 12 is \\(\\boxed{1032}\\).",
