+ 1 is 8, and 6 + 0 is 6. So 737. Oh, right, I messed up my initial addition step. So 67 + 670 is 737. That's correct. So all methods lead to the same answer. I think that's solid. So the product is 737.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 67 and 11 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 11 = 67 \\times (10 + 1) = (67 \\times 10) + (67 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(67 \\times 10 = 670\\)\n- \\(67 \\times 1 = 67\\)\n\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n670 + 67 = 737\n\\]\n\nVerification by division confirms \\(737 \\div 11 = 67\\), and standard multiplication steps also validate the answer. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{737}",
