      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 62 and 19. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down, maybe using the distributive property. Let me think. 62 is 60 plus 2, right? And 19 is 10 plus 9. So if I distribute, that would be (60 + 2) multiplied by (10 + 9). \n\nWait, actually, maybe it's easier to just do straight multiplication. Let me try that. 62 times 19. Hmm, 62 times 20 would be 1240, but since it's 19, which is one less than 20, I need to subtract 62 once. So 1240 minus 62. Let me calculate that. 1240 minus 60 is 1180, then minus 2 more is 1178. So is the answer 1178? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do it the standard multiplication method. Let's line them up:\n\n  62\nx 19\n----\n\nStarting with the 9. 9 times 60 is 540, and 9 times 2 is 18. Then add those together: 540 + 18 = 558. Then the 1 (from 19) times 62. 1 times 60 is 60, and 1 times 2 is 2. So 60 + 2 = 62. Now add 558 and 62. 558 + 60 is 618, plus 2 is 620. Wait, that's different from before. Hmm, so now I get 620 versus 1178. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the first method. Let me check again. If 62 times 20 is 1240, then subtracting 62 gives 1240 - 62. Let's do that step by step. 1240 minus 60 is 1180, then minus 2 is 1178. But when I did the standard multiplication, I got 620. That can't be right. There's a mistake here. Let me figure out where I went wrong.\n\nWait, no, actually, when I did the standard multiplication, I might have added incorrectly. Let me redo that. So 62 times 19. Let's do it step by step. Multiply 62 by 10, which is 620. Then multiply 62 by 9. 62 times 9: 60 times 9 is 540, and 2 times 9 is 18. So 540 + 18 = 558. Then add 620 and 558. 620 + 500 is 1120, then +58 is 1178. Oh! Wait, I think I added wrong earlier. So 620 + 558. Let's check that again. 620 + 500 is 1120, then 1120 + 58. 1120 + 50 is 1170, then +8 is 1178. So both methods actually give the same result. I must have made a mistake when I thought I got 620. So the correct answer is 1178. \n\nBut why did I get confused earlier? Let me see. When I first did the 62 times 20 minus 62, that's 1240 - 62 = 1178. Then when I did the standard multiplication, I split 19 into 10 and 9, multiplied each by 62, then added. So 62*10=620, 62*9=558, 620+558=1178. So both methods confirm that the product is 1178. Therefore, the answer should be 1178. \n\nJust to be absolutely sure, maybe I can do another check. Let's use another multiplication technique. For example, breaking down 19 into 20 -1. So 62*(20 -1) = 62*20 - 62*1 = 1240 - 62 = 1178. Yep, same result. Alternatively, maybe using a calculator. But since I don't have a calculator here, I can try another way. Let's see, 62*19. Let's compute 62*20 = 1240, subtract 62, which is 1240 - 60 = 1180, then subtract 2 more gives 1178. That's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 19 is 20 -1, so as above. Alternatively, maybe using the distributive property in another way. For example, 62*19 = (60 + 2)*(20 -1) = 60*20 - 60*1 + 2*20 - 2*1 = 1200 - 60 + 40 - 2. Let's compute that: 1200 - 60 is 1140, plus 40 is 1180, minus 2 is 1178. Again, same answer. \n\nSo all methods lead to 1178. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 62 and 19 is 1178. I think earlier confusion was a momentary lapse when I thought I got a different answer, but upon checking again, everything aligns. So the final answer is 1178.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 62 and 19 can be calculated using the distributive property. Breaking down 19 into 20 - 1, we have:\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 19 = 62 \\times (20 - 1) = (62 \\times 20) - (62 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part:\n- \\(62 \\times 20 = 1240\\)\n- \\(62 \\times 1 = 62\\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n1240 - 62 = 1178\n\\]\n\nVerification through the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n62 & \\\\\n\\times & 19 \