      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 39 and 60. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 39 by 60. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 39 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 60. Wait, 39 is close to 40, right? So 39 is 40 minus 1. That might make it easier. So if I write it as (40 - 1) multiplied by 60, that could work. Let me try that.\n\nSo, (40 - 1) \u00d7 60. Using the distributive property, that would be 40\u00d760 - 1\u00d760. Let me calculate each part separately. 40 times 60... Hmm, 40 times 60 is like 4 times 6 times 10 times 10, which is 24 times 100, so 2400. Then, 1 times 60 is just 60. So subtracting those, 2400 minus 60. That would be 2340. So is the product 2340? Let me check if that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can multiply 39 by 60 directly. Let's see. 39 times 60. Well, 39 times 6 is 234, right? Because 30 times 6 is 180, and 9 times 6 is 54, so 180 + 54 is 234. Then, since it's 39 times 60, which is 39 times 6 times 10, so 234 times 10 is 2340. Okay, same answer. That seems consistent.\n\nWait, another way to check. Maybe using another method. Let's do it the standard multiplication way. Let's multiply 39 by 60. Let's ignore the zeros first. 39 times 60. Well, 39 times 6 is 234, then add a zero because 60 has a zero at the end. So 2340. Yep, same result. So that's three different methods, all leading to 2340. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nBut just to make sure I didn't make any calculation errors. Let me verify each step again. First method: 39 = 40 - 1. Then 40\u00d760 = 2400, 1\u00d760 = 60. 2400 - 60 = 2340. Correct. Second method: 39\u00d760. Break down 39 into 30 + 9. So 30\u00d760 = 1800, 9\u00d760 = 540. Then 1800 + 540 = 2340. Third method: standard multiplication. 39\u00d760. Multiply 39\u00d76=234, then add a zero: 2340. All methods agree. So I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another approach. Let's think of 60 as 6 times 10. So 39 times 60 is 39 times 6 times 10. As before, 39\u00d76=234, then 234\u00d710=2340. Same answer. So no matter how I approach it, the product is 2340. Therefore, I can be confident that the answer is 2340.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 39 and 60 is \\boxed{2340}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 39 and 60, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down 39 into 40 - 1:**\n   \\[\n   (40 - 1) \\times 60 = 40 \\times 60 - 1 \\times 60\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(40 \\times 60\\):\n     \\[\n     40 \\times 60 = 2400\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(1 \\times 60\\):\n     \\[\n     1 \\times 60 = 60\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the results:\n     \\[\n     2400 - 60 = 2340\n     \\]\n\n2. **Multiplying directly:**\n   - Break down 39 into 30 + 9:\n     \\[\n     (30 + 9) \\times 60 = 30 \\times 60 + 9 \\times 60\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(30 \\times 60\\):\n     \\[\n     30 \\times 60 = 1800\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(9 \\times 60\\):\n     \\[\n     9 \\times 60 = 540\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     1800 + 540 = 2340\n     \\]\n\n3. **Standard multiplication:**\n   - Multiply 39 by 60:\n     \\[\n     39 \\times 60 = 2340\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 39 and 60 is \\(\\boxed{2340}\\).",
