      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 75 and 54. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think. The distributive property is a(b + c) = ab + ac. But here, both numbers are two-digit numbers. Maybe breaking them down into tens and ones would help.\n\nAlright, so 75 can be written as 70 + 5, and 54 can be written as 50 + 4. If I apply the distributive property, I can multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number and then add them all together. Let me write that out:\n\n75 \u00d7 54 = (70 + 5) \u00d7 (50 + 4)\n\nFirst, multiply 70 by 50. That's straightforward. 70 times 50 is 3500. Then, 70 times 4 is 280. Next, 5 times 50 is 250. Finally, 5 times 4 is 20. Now, add all those results together: 3500 + 280 + 250 + 20.\n\nLet me add them step by step. 3500 + 280 is 3780. Then, 3780 + 250 is 4030. Finally, 4030 + 20 equals 4050. So, is the product 4050? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way, multiplying each digit and carrying over as needed. Let's try that method to verify. \n\nMultiplying 75 by 54. Let's do it step by step. First, multiply 75 by 4 (the ones digit of 54). 75 \u00d7 4. 70 \u00d7 4 is 280, and 5 \u00d7 4 is 20. So, 280 + 20 = 300. That's the ones place. Then, multiply 75 by 50 (the tens digit of 54). 75 \u00d7 50. 70 \u00d7 50 is 3500, and 5 \u00d7 50 is 250. So, 3500 + 250 = 3750. Now, add the two results together: 300 + 3750. That's 4050. Okay, same answer. So that seems to check out.\n\nWait, but let me try another method just to be thorough. Maybe using the difference of squares formula? Hmm, not sure if that applies here. The difference of squares is (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. But 75 and 54 don't seem to form a pair that's a sum and difference. Maybe that's not the right approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 54 into 50 + 4 and multiplying each by 75, which I already did earlier, and that gave me 4050. So both methods give the same result. That gives me more confidence that 4050 is the correct product.\n\nLet me do a quick check with another multiplication technique. For example, using the fact that 75 is 3 times 25. So, 75 \u00d7 54 = 3 \u00d7 25 \u00d7 54. Then, 3 \u00d7 54 is 162, and 162 \u00d7 25. Hmm, 162 \u00d7 25. Well, 162 \u00d7 10 is 1620, 162 \u00d7 20 is 3240, so 1620 + 3240 = 4860. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, hold on. Wait, 25 is a quarter of 100, so 162 \u00d7 25 is 162 \u00d7 (100/4) = (162 \u00d7 100)/4 = 16200/4 = 4050. Oh, right, that works. So that also gives 4050. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm step by step. Let's write it out:\n\n      75\n    \u00d754\n    ----\n      300  (75 \u00d7 4)\n     3750   (75 \u00d7 50, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n     4050\n\nYes, adding 300 and 3750 gives 4050. So that's the same as before. So all methods point to 4050. Therefore, I think the product of 75 and 54 is 4050. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm the same result. So I'm pretty confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 75 and 54 is \\boxed{4050}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 75 and 54, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n75 \\times 54 = (70 + 5) \\times (50 + 4)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. Multiply 70 by 50: \\(70 \\times 50 = 3500\\)\n2. Multiply 70 by 4: \\(70 \\times 4 = 280\\)\n3. Multiply 5 by 50: \\(5 \\times 50 = 250\\)\n4. Multiply 5 by 4: \\(5 \\times 4 = 20\\)\n\nNext, we add all these results together:\n\n\\[\n3500 + 280 + 250 + 20\n\\]\n\nAdding step by step:\n\n1. \\(3500 + 280 = 3780\\)\n2. \\(3780 + 250 = 4030\\)\n3. \\(4030 + 20 = 4050\\)\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 75 \\\\\n\\times 54 \\\\\n\\hline\n 300 \\quad \\text{(75 \\times 4)} \\\\\n3750 \\quad \\text{(75 \\times 50, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n4050 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the partial products \\(300\\) and \\(3750\\) gives \\(4050\\).\n\nThus, the product of 75 and 54 is \\(\\boxed{4050}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
