      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 13 and 11. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 13 by 11. Let me think about how to do that step by step.\n\nFirst, I can do it the standard way, like multiplying the units place first. Let me write it out:\n\n\u2003\u200313\n\u00d7\u202f11\n-------\n\nStarting with the ones place. 3 times 1 is 3. Then, moving to the tens place, 1 times 1 is 1. But wait, there's also the 1 in the 11, which is actually in the tens place. So maybe I need to consider that as well. Let me try again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 11 into 10 and 1. So 13 times 10 is 130, and 13 times 1 is 13. Then adding those together: 130 + 13. Let's see, 130 plus 10 is 140, and then plus 3 more is 143. So is the product 143?\n\nWait, let me check that another way to make sure. If I multiply 13 by 11 directly, maybe using the distributive property. 13 times 11 is the same as 13 times (10 + 1), which is 13*10 + 13*1. That's 130 + 13, which is indeed 143. Okay, that seems right.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me do the multiplication the long way. Multiplying 13 by 11:\n\n\u2003\u200313\n\u00d7\u202f11\n-------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit. 1 times 3 is 3. Then, moving left, 1 times 1 is 1. But since the next digit is also 1, we have to carry over. Wait, maybe I should do it step by step.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying two-digit numbers, you can do it as:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u200313\n\u00d7\u202f\u202f11\n--------\n\nBut maybe aligning them properly:\n\n\u2003\u200313\n\u00d7\u202f11\n-------\n\nStarting with the ones place: 3 (from 13) multiplied by 1 (from 11) is 3. Write down 3.\n\nThen, the tens place: 1 (from 13) multiplied by 1 (from 11) is 1. But since the 1 in 11 is in the tens place, it's actually 10. So 1 times 10 is 10. So we need to add a zero at the end. So 10. Then add that to the previous 3. 3 + 10 is 13. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, no. Let me try again. When multiplying 13 by 11, you can think of 13 as 10 + 3. So 10*11 = 110 and 3*11 = 33. Then 110 + 33 = 143. That works. But when doing it the standard multiplication method:\n\n\u2003\u200313\n\u00d7\u202f11\n-------\n\nMultiply 13 by 1 (units place of 11): 13*1 = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nThen multiply 13 by 1 (tens place of 11, which is 10): 13*10 = 130. Write down 130. Now, add the two results: 13 + 130. 13 + 130 is 143. So that works. The carry-over 1 from the first multiplication is added to the second one, but since the second multiplication is 130, adding 13 (from the first step) gives 143. Wait, actually, when you write down 13 and then 130, you have:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u200313\n\u00d7\u202f\u202f11\n--------\n\u2003\u200313\n+130\n-----\n143\n\nYes, that adds up. So the product is 143. Let me verify with another method. Maybe using addition: 11 added to itself 13 times. 11 + 11 = 22, 22 + 11 = 33, 33 + 11 = 44, 44 + 11 = 55, 55 + 11 = 66, 66 + 11 = 77, 77 + 11 = 88, 88 + 11 = 99, 99 + 11 = 110, 110 + 11 = 121, 121 + 11 = 132, 132 + 11 = 143. Yep, that's 13 times 11 added step by step, and each time adding 11. So that also gives 143. So all methods point to 143. Therefore, the product of 13 and 11 is 143.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 11 is \\boxed{143}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 11, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n13 \\times 11 = 13 \\times (10 + 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(13 \\times 10\\):\n\\[\n13 \\times 10 = 130\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(13 \\times 1\\):\n\\[\n13 \\times 1 = 13\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n130 + 13 = 143\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this by multiplying step-by-step:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 13 \\\\\n\\times 11 \\\\\n\\hline\n 13 \\quad \\text{(13 multiplied by 1)} \\\\\n130 \\quad \\text{(13 multiplied by 10)} \\\\\n\\hline\n143 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 13 and 11 is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).",
