      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 39 and 73. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by adding the number to itself as many times as the other number. But 39 and 73 are both two-digit numbers, so maybe breaking them down would help. Let me try the standard multiplication method.\n\nFirst, I can write 39 as 40 minus 1, right? So 39 is 40 - 1. Then, multiplying 39 by 73 is the same as multiplying (40 - 1) by 73. Maybe I can distribute this multiplication over the subtraction. That would give me 40*73 minus 1*73. Let me check if that works.\n\nCalculating 40*73 first. 40 times 70 is 2800, and 40 times 3 is 120. Adding those together, 2800 + 120 = 2920. So 40*73 is 2920. Then, 1*73 is just 73. So subtracting those, 2920 - 73. Let me do that subtraction. 2920 minus 70 is 2850, and then minus 3 more is 2847. So is the product 2847?\n\nWait, let me verify that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard algorithm for multiplying 39 by 73.\n\nMultiplying 39 by 73. Starting from the rightmost digits. 3 times 3 is 9. Then, 3 times 9 is 27. Write down 7 and carry over 2. Then, moving to the next digit, 7 times 3 is 21, plus the carried over 2 is 23. Then, 7 times 9 is 63. So putting it all together, 2379? Wait, that doesn't match the previous result. Hmm, something's wrong here.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication steps. Let me try again. Let's do it step by step.\n\nMultiplying 39 by 73:\n\nFirst, write 39 as 30 + 9. Then, 73 can be written as 70 + 3. So, (30 + 9) * (70 + 3) = 30*70 + 30*3 + 9*70 + 9*3. Let's compute each part:\n\n30*70 = 2100\n\n30*3 = 90\n\n9*70 = 630\n\n9*3 = 27\n\nNow, add all those together: 2100 + 90 = 2190; 2190 + 630 = 2820; 2820 + 27 = 2847. Okay, that's the same result as before. So why did the standard algorithm give me 2379? Let me check that again.\n\nWait, maybe I confused the digits. Let me try multiplying 39 by 73 using the standard multiplication method properly.\n\nMultiply 39 by 73:\n\nStart with the units digit: 3 (from 73). Multiply 3 by 9 (units digit of 39). 3*9=27. Write down 7, carry over 2.\n\nNext, multiply 3 by 3 (tens digit of 73). 3*3=9, plus the carried over 2 is 11. Write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nNow, multiply the tens digit of 39, which is 3. Multiply by 7 (tens digit of 73). 3*7=21, plus the carried over 1 is 22. Write down 22.\n\nSo putting it all together: 22 (from the last step) and 17 (from the first step). Wait, no, that doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe I need to structure it differently.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying two two-digit numbers, you start with the units digit of the second number (3) multiplied by the entire first number (39), which gives 27. Then, multiply the tens digit of the second number (7) by the first number (39), which gives 273. Then, add those two results. So 27 + 273 = 2847. Oh, that's the same answer as before. So maybe I made a mistake in my initial standard algorithm attempt.\n\nLet me try again. Multiplying 39 by 73:\n\nFirst, multiply 39 by 3 (units digit of 73). 39*3=117. Then, multiply 39 by 70 (tens digit of 73). 39*70=2730. Then, add 117 + 2730. 117 + 2730: 2730 + 100 = 2830, 2830 + 17 = 2847. There we go. So that works. So the correct product is 2847.\n\nEarlier, when I tried the standard algorithm, I might have mixed up the digits or the steps. But breaking it down into parts and verifying with the distributive property both gave me 2847, so that must be the correct answer. Let me just confirm with another method, maybe using prime factors.\n\n39 factors into 3*13, and 73 is a prime number. So 3*13*73. Let's compute that. 3*13 is 39, then 39*73. Wait, that's the same as the original problem. Hmm, so that doesn't help. Alternatively, maybe compute 73*10=730, 73*20=1460, 73*30=2190. Then, 730 + 1460 = 2190, plus 2190 = 4380. Wait, that's not right. Wait, 73*10=730, 73*20=1460, 73*30=2190. But 73*39 is 73*(30+9)=73*30 +73*9=2190 + 657=2190+657. Let's compute 2190 + 600=2790, then +57=2847. There we go. So that's another way to get 2847. So all methods point to 2847. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 39 and 73 is 2847.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 39 and 73 is \\boxed{2847}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 39 and 73, we can use the distributive property:\n\n1. Break down 39 into 40 - 1.\n2. Multiply each term separately:\n   - \\(40 \\times 73 = 2920\\)\n   - \\(1 \\times 73 = 73\\)\n3. Subtract the results:\n   - \\(2920 - 73 = 2847\\)\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication method:\n1. Multiply 39 by 73 by breaking down each number:\n   - \\(30 \\times 70 = 2100\\)\n   - \\(30 \\times 3 = 90\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 70 = 630\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 3 = 27\\)\n2. Add the results together:\n   - \\(2100 + 90 + 630 + 27 = 2847\\)\n\nAnother verification using prime factors:\n1. \\(73 \\times 39 = 73 \\times (30 + 9) = 73 \\times 30 + 73 \\times 9\\)\n2. Calculate each part:\n   - \\(73 \\times 30 = 2190\\)\n   - \\(73 \\times 9 = 657\\)\n3. Add the results:\n   - \\(2190 + 657 = 2847\\)\n\nThus, the product of 39 and 73 is \\(\\boxed{2847}\\).",
