ify with another multiplication. Let's see, 94*97. Let's think of 94*100 is 9400, minus 94*3. 94*3 is 282. So 9400 - 282 is 9118. Oh, that's another way. So that's four different methods. All leading to the same answer. So I'm confident that the product is 9118.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 94 and 97 is \\boxed{9118}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 94 and 97, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers:**\n   - \\(94 = 90 + 4\\)\n   - \\(97 = 90 + 7\\)\n   - Expanding \\((90 + 4)(90 + 7)\\):\n     \\[\n     90 \\times 90 + 90 \\times 7 + 4 \\times 90 + 4 \\times 7\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 8100 + 630 + 360 + 28\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 8100 + 630 = 8730\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     8730 + 360 = 9090\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     9090 + 28 = 9118\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the difference of squares:**\n   - Rewrite 94 as \\(95 - 1\\) and 97 as \\(95 + 2\\):\n     \\[\n     (95 - 1)(95 + 2)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 95^2 + 95 \\times 2 - 1 \\times 95 - 1 \\times 2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 9025 + 190 - 95 - 2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 9025 + 190 = 9215\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     9215 - 95 = 9120\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     9120 - 2 = 9118\n     \\]\n\n3. **Standard multiplication:**\n   - Calculate \\(94 \\times 7 = 658\\) and \\(94 \\times 90 = 8460\\):\n     \\[\n     658 + 8460 = 9118\n     \\]\n\n4. **Alternative method:**\n   - Calculate \\(94 \\times 100 = 9400\\) and subtract \\(94 \\times 3 = 282\\):\n     \\[\n     9400 - 282 = 9118\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 94 and 97 is \\(\\boxed{9118}\\).",
