e absolutely sure. Since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can break down 89 into 100 - 11. So 26*(100 - 11) = 26*100 - 26*11 = 2600 - 286. 2600 - 200 is 2400, then subtract 86: 2400 - 86 = 2314. Yep, same answer. So that's three different methods giving me 2314. So I think that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another multiplication. Let's see, 26*89. If I consider 26 as 25 +1, then 25*89 +1*89. 25*89. 25*90 is 2250, minus 25 is 2225. Then 1*89 is 89. So 2225 +89. 2225 + 80 is 2305, then +9 is 2314. Again, same result. \n\nOkay, so all these methods are leading me to 2314. So I think that's the right answer. But just to make sure, maybe I can try adding 26 and 89 first. 26 +89 is 115. Then, if I multiply 115 by 2, that's 230. But that's not helpful. Wait, no, that's not the same as multiplying 26 and 89. Wait, 26*89 is not 115*2. That's a different number. So that approach doesn't help. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 89 is 100 -11, as before. But I think I've already confirmed it enough times. So I think the answer is 2314. \n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me do the multiplication step by step. \n\nMultiplying 26 by 89:\n\nStart with the units digit: 6*9=54. Write down 4, carry over 5.\n\nNext, the tens digit: 2*9=18, plus the carried over 5 is 23. Write down 3, carry over 2.\n\nNow, since 26 has a tens digit of 2, multiply that by 8 (since 89 is in the tens place now? Wait, no. Wait, when multiplying 26 by 89, you have to consider each digit. Let me do it properly.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying two-digit numbers, you can do it as:\n\n        26\n      x 89\n      ----\n        26*9 (units place)\n      26*8 (tens place, shifted one position to the left)\n      ----\n\nSo first, 26*9. 20*9=180, 6*9=54. So 180+54=234. That's the first partial product.\n\nNext, 26*8. 20*8=160, 6*8=48. So 160+48=208. Then, since it's the tens place, we shift it one position to the left, making it 2080.\n\nNow, add 234 and 2080. 234 + 2080. Let's add 2000 + 200 = 2200, 34 +80=114. So 2200 +114=2314. Yep, same result. So that's the standard multiplication method. \n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I'm confident that the product of 26 and 89 is 2314. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all cross-checks lead to the same answer. So I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 89 is \\boxed{2314}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 89, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n26 \\times 89 = (20 + 6) \\times (80 + 9)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down into parts:\n\n\\[\n(20 \\times 80) + (20 \\times 9) + (6 \\times 80) + (6 \\times 9)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part step by step:\n\n\\[\n20 \\times 80 = 1600\n\\]\n\\[\n20 \\times 9 = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 80 = 480\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n1600 + 180 + 480 + 54 = 1780 + 534 = 2314\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by considering the distributive property in a different way:\n\n\\[\n26 \\times 89 = 26 \\times (100 - 11) = 26 \\times 100 - 26 \\times 11\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n\\[\n26 \\times 100 = 2600\n\\]\n\\[\n26 \\times 11 = 286\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[\n2600 - 286 = 2314\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 26 and 89 is \\(\\boxed{2314}\\).",
