) divided by 2. 73 times 100 is 7300, divided by 2 is 3650. Oh, that's another way to look at it. So that also gives 3650. So all methods point to the same answer.\n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 50 and 73 is 3650. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. Each step checks out with different methods, so I'm confident that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 50 and 73 is \\boxed{3650}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 50 and 73, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\(50 \\times 70\\):\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 70 = 3500\n   \\]\n   This is because \\(50 \\times 7 = 350\\), and then multiplying by 10 gives \\(350 \\times 10 = 3500\\).\n\n2. Calculate \\(50 \\times 3\\):\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 3 = 150\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two steps:\n   \\[\n   3500 + 150 = 3650\n   \\]\n\nAnother method is to use the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 73 = 50 \\times (70 + 3) = 50 \\times 70 + 50 \\times 3 = 3500 + 150 = 3650\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 50 and 73 is \\(\\boxed{3650}\\).",
