      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 47 and 11. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. So, 47 times 11 would be 47 added to itself 11 times. But that seems like a lot of addition. There must be a smarter way to do this without having to do all that addition manually. \n\nWait, maybe I can use the distributive property here. The distributive property is a(b + c) = ab + ac. But in this case, both numbers are just single-digit numbers, so maybe breaking them down into smaller parts would help. Let me think. 11 is close to 10, right? So maybe I can write 11 as 10 + 1. Then, using the distributive property, 47 times 10 plus 47 times 1. That should work. Let me check that.\n\nFirst, 47 multiplied by 10. Well, multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end, so 47 times 10 is 470. Then, 47 times 1 is just 47. So now I need to add those two results together: 470 + 47. Let me do that addition. 470 plus 40 is 510, and then plus 7 more would be 517. So, 47 times 11 is 517. Let me verify that with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can do the multiplication directly. Let's use the standard algorithm. Multiply 47 by 11. Starting from the rightmost digit, 7 times 1 is 7. Then, the next digit is 4, but since we're multiplying by 11, it's like adding the original number and shifting one position to the left. Wait, maybe I should write it out step by step.\n\nMultiplying 47 by 11:\n      47\n    x 11\n    ----\n      47  (47 times 1)\n    470   (47 times 10, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n    517\n\nYes, that's the same result as before. So adding 47 and 470 gives 517. Let me check with another method. Maybe using addition step by step. 47 times 11 is the same as 47 times (10 + 1), which we already did. Alternatively, maybe breaking down 47 into 40 + 7. Let's try that.\n\n40 times 11 is 440, and 7 times 11 is 77. Then add those together: 440 + 77. 440 + 70 is 510, and then +7 is 517. Yep, same answer. So that seems consistent. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me try another way. Let's see, 47 times 10 is 470, and 47 times 1 is 47. So 470 + 47. 470 + 40 is 510, then +7 is 517. Definitely, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, if I didn't remember the distributive property, I could do the multiplication by expanding 11 as 10 + 1 and multiplying each part separately. So 47*10 is 470, 47*1 is 47, sum them. That works. \n\nAnother way: Maybe using the fact that 11 is a prime number, but I don't know if that helps here. Alternatively, think of 47*11 as (50 - 3)*11. Let's try that. 50*11 is 550, and -3*11 is -33. Then 550 - 33 is 517. That also works. So that's another method. \n\nSo, all these different methods give me the same answer, 517. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 47 and 11 is 517. I don't think I made any mistakes here because all the different approaches led to the same result. So yeah, the answer should be 517.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 47 and 11 can be calculated using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n47 \\times 11 = 47 \\times (10 + 1) = (47 \\times 10) + (47 \\times 1)\n\\]  \nCalculating each term:  \n\\[\n47 \\times 10 = 470  \n\\]  \n\\[\n47 \\times 1 = 47  \n\\]  \nAdding the results:  \n\\[\n470 + 47 = 517  \n\\]  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{517}",
