      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 40 and 30. Hmm, let me think. The product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 40 by 30. Let me write that down: 40 \u00d7 30. \n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down to make it easier. I know that 40 is 4 times 10, and 30 is 3 times 10. So if I multiply them all together, it would be (4 \u00d7 10) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 10). Then, using the commutative property of multiplication, I can rearrange the numbers. So that becomes 4 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 10 \u00d7 10. \n\nCalculating 4 \u00d7 3 first, that's 12. Then, 10 \u00d7 10 is 100. So now it's 12 \u00d7 100. Hmm, 12 times 100 is just 1200. Wait, is that right? Let me check another way. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of 40 \u00d7 30 as (4 \u00d7 10) \u00d7 (3 \u00d7 10). Multiplying the 10s first: 10 \u00d7 10 is 100. Then multiply that by 4 \u00d7 3. 4 \u00d7 3 is 12, so 100 \u00d7 12 is 1200. Yeah, that matches. \n\nAnother way to check might be to use addition. 40 added 30 times. Let's see, 40 + 40 is 80, then +40 is 120, and so on. Each time adding 40. So how many times do I need to add 40 to get to 30 times? Well, 30 is 3 times 10, so 40 added 30 times is 40 \u00d7 30. Let's count the additions: 10 times would be 400, but since it's 30, that's 3000 divided by 10? Wait, maybe not. Let me think again. \n\nIf I have 40 added 30 times, that's the same as 40 \u00d7 30. Let's do it step by step. 40 + 40 = 80 (1), 80 + 40 = 120 (2), 120 + 40 = 160 (3), 160 + 40 = 200 (4), 200 + 40 = 240 (5), 240 + 40 = 280 (6), 280 + 40 = 320 (7), 320 + 40 = 360 (8), 360 + 40 = 400 (9), 400 + 40 = 440 (10), 440 + 40 = 480 (11), 480 + 40 = 520 (12), 520 + 40 = 560 (13), 560 + 40 = 600 (14), 600 + 40 = 640 (15), 640 + 40 = 680 (16), 680 + 40 = 720 (17), 720 + 40 = 760 (18), 760 + 40 = 800 (19), 800 + 40 = 840 (20), 840 + 40 = 880 (21), 880 + 40 = 920 (22), 920 + 40 = 960 (23), 960 + 40 = 1000 (24), 1000 + 40 = 1040 (25), 1040 + 40 = 1080 (26), 1080 + 40 = 1120 (27), 1120 + 40 = 1160 (28), 1160 + 40 = 1200 (29). Wait, that's 29 times adding 40 to get to 1200. Hmm, so that's 29 additions. But that seems tedious. Maybe there's a better way. \n\nAlternatively, since 40 \u00d7 30 is the same as 4 \u00d7 10 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 10, which is (4 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 (10 \u00d7 10) = 12 \u00d7 100 = 1200. Yeah, that's the same as before. So that seems correct. \n\nAnother method: Maybe use distributive property differently. 40 \u00d7 30 can be thought of as (40 \u00d7 3) \u00d7 10. Let's compute 40 \u00d7 3 first. 40 \u00d7 3 is 120. Then, 120 \u00d7 10 is 1200. That works too. \n\nOr, (30 \u00d7 40) can be (30 \u00d7 4) \u00d7 10. 30 \u00d7 4 is 120, then 120 \u00d7 10 is 1200. Same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 30 into 10 + 20. So 40 \u00d7 30 = 40 \u00d7 (10 + 20) = 40\u00d710 + 40\u00d720. 40\u00d710 is 400, and 40\u00d720 is 800. Then 400 + 800 is 1200. That's another way to verify. \n\nSo all these different methods give me 1200. That seems consistent. \n\nWait, let me check with actual multiplication. 40 multiplied by 30. Let's do it digit by digit. \n\n40\n\u00d730\n------\nWe can ignore the zeros at the end for a moment. 40 \u00d7 3 = 120. Then, since 30 is 3 \u00d7 10, we need to multiply by 10 again. So 120 \u00d7 10 = 1200. Yep, that's the same. \n\nAlternatively, if I write it out:\n\n     40\n   \u00d7 30\n   ------\n     40 \u00d7 3 = 120\n   40 \u00d7 0 = 0\n   Adding them together: 120 + 0 = 1200. \n\nPerfect. So all these methods converge to 1200. \n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 40 and 30 is 1200. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 40 and 30 is \\boxed{1200}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 40 and 30, we can use multiplication. \n\nFirst, we recognize that 40 is \\(4 \\times 10\\) and 30 is \\(3 \\times 10\\). Multiplying these together, we get:\n\\[\n(4 \\times 10) \\times (3 \\times 10) = 4 \\times 3 \\times 10 \\times 10\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the commutative property of multiplication to rearrange the factors:\n\\[\n4 \\times 3 \\times 10 \\times 10\n\\]\n\nCalculating the multiplication step-by-step:\n1. Multiply 4 and 3: \\(4 \\times 3 = 12\\)\n2. Multiply the result by 10: \\(12 \\times 10 = 120\\)\n3. Multiply the result by the remaining 10: \\(120 \\times 10 = 1200\\)\n\nWe can also verify this by breaking down the numbers:\n- \\(40 \\times 30 = (40 \\times 3) \\times 10\\)\n- \\(40 \\times 3 = 120\\)\n- \\(120 \\times 10 = 1200\\)\n\nAnother verification method involves adding 40 thirty times, which also results in 1200. \n\nThus, the product of 40 and 30 is \\(\\boxed{1200}\\).",
