      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 45 and 60. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 45 by 60. Let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 45 into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply by 60. I know that 45 is 40 plus 5. So, if I split 45 into 40 and 5, then the multiplication becomes 40 times 60 plus 5 times 60. That might be easier than multiplying them directly.\n\nLet me write that out: 40 \u00d7 60 + 5 \u00d7 60. Okay, let's calculate each part separately. \n\nStarting with 40 \u00d7 60. Well, 40 times 60... I remember that multiplying by 60 is the same as multiplying by 6 and then by 10. So 40 \u00d7 6 is 240, and then times 10 would be 2400. So that part is 2400.\n\nNext, 5 \u00d7 60. That's straightforward. 5 times 60 is 300. So now I have 2400 + 300. Adding those together gives me 2700. So, is the product 2700? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can multiply 45 and 60 directly. Let's try that method to confirm. 45 times 60. Hmm, 45 times 6 is 270, and then times 10 would be 2700. Wait, that's the same result as before. So both methods give me 2700. That seems consistent.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me do it another way. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 45 \u00d7 60. \n\nMultiplying 45 by 60. So, 45\n\u00d7 60\n--------\nFirst, multiply 45 by 0 (the units place of 60). That's 0.\nThen multiply 45 by 6 (the tens place of 60). That's 270.\nBut since the 6 is in the tens place, we have to add a zero to make it 2700.\nAdding the two results: 0 + 2700 = 2700. Yep, same answer.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using distributive property differently. 45 \u00d7 60 = (40 + 5) \u00d7 60 = 40\u00d760 + 5\u00d760 = 2400 + 300 = 2700. Yep, same result.\n\nWait, maybe I can check with addition. If I have 45 multiplied by 60, how else can I think about it? 45 multiplied by 60 is the same as 45 multiplied by 6 times 10. So 45 \u00d7 6 is 270, then 270 \u00d7 10 is 2700. That works too.\n\nAlternatively, 60 is 6 times 10, so 45 times 60 is 45 times 6 times 10. Which again is 270 times 10, which is 2700. All methods lead to the same answer. So I think that's solid.\n\nJust to make sure, maybe I can use another example. Let's say 45 \u00d7 60. If I know that 45 \u00d7 6 = 270, then 45 \u00d7 60 is 45 \u00d7 (6 \u00d7 10) = (45 \u00d7 6) \u00d7 10 = 270 \u00d7 10 = 2700. Yep, that's the same.\n\nAlternatively, if I think of 60 as 50 + 10, then 45 \u00d7 50 + 45 \u00d7 10. Let's see: 45 \u00d7 50 is 2250, and 45 \u00d7 10 is 450. Adding those together: 2250 + 450 = 2700. Oh, that's another way to split it. So even breaking it into 50 and 10 works. So all different methods confirm that the product is 2700.\n\nI think that's thorough enough. All different approaches give me the same answer, so I can be confident that the product of 45 and 60 is 2700. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think that's correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 45 and 60 can be calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Here's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. **Break down 45 into 40 and 5**:\n   - \\( 40 \\times 60 = 2400 \\)\n   - \\( 5 \\times 60 = 300 \\)\n   - **Add the results**: \\( 2400 + 300 = 2700 \\)\n\n2. **Multiply directly**:\n   - \\( 45 \\times 60 = 2700 \\)\n   - Breaking down 60 into 6 and 10:\n     - \\( 45 \\times 6 = 270 \\)\n     - \\( 270 \\times 10 = 2700 \\)\n\n3. **Verification using alternative methods**:\n   - \\( 45 \\times 60 = (40 + 5) \\times 60 = 40 \\times 60 + 5 \\times 60 \\)\n   - \\( 45 \\times 60 = 45 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (45 \\times 6) \\times 10 \\)\n   - All methods consistently yield **2700**.\n\n**Answer**: \\boxed{2700}",
