      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 36 and 46. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think. \n\nFirst, 36 is the same as 30 plus 6, right? And 46 is 40 plus 6. So if I distribute each part, maybe that will make it easier. Let me write that out:\n\n36 \u00d7 46 = (30 + 6) \u00d7 (40 + 6)\n\nNow, using the distributive property, which is (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. So applying that here:\n\n= 30\u00d740 + 30\u00d76 + 6\u00d740 + 6\u00d76\n\nLet me calculate each term step by step. \n\nFirst term: 30\u00d740. That's straightforward. 30 times 40 is 1200. \n\nSecond term: 30\u00d76. 30 multiplied by 6 is 180. \n\nThird term: 6\u00d740. That's 6 times 40, which is 240. \n\nFourth term: 6\u00d76. That's 36. \n\nNow, adding all those up: 1200 + 180 + 240 + 36. Let me add them one by one. \n\nStarting with 1200 + 180. That equals 1380. \n\nThen, 1380 + 240. Hmm, 1380 + 200 is 1580, and then +40 makes 1620. \n\nNext, adding 36 to 1620. 1620 + 30 is 1650, and then +6 is 1656. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nFirst term: 30\u00d740=1200. Correct. \n\nSecond term: 30\u00d76=180. Correct. \n\nThird term: 6\u00d740=240. Correct. \n\nFourth term: 6\u00d76=36. Correct. \n\nAdding them up: 1200 + 180 is 1380. Then 1380 + 240. Let's see, 1380 + 200 is 1580, plus 40 is 1620. Then 1620 + 36. 1620 + 30 is 1650, plus 6 is 1656. \n\nHmm, so the total is 1656. Let me verify this another way to be sure. Maybe by doing the multiplication directly. \n\nMultiplying 36 by 46. Let's do it the standard way. \n\nFirst, multiply 36 by 6. 36\u00d76. 30\u00d76=180, 6\u00d76=36. So 180+36=216. \n\nThen, multiply 36 by 40. 36\u00d740. 36\u00d74=144, so 144\u00d710=1440. \n\nNow, add those two results together: 216 + 1440. \n\n216 + 1440. Let's see, 200 + 1440 is 1640, and then 16 + 40 is 56. So 1640 + 56 = 1696. Wait, that's different from before. Hmm, so now I get 1696, but earlier I got 1656. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere. \n\nWait, let me check again. \n\nFirst method: (30+6)(40+6) = 30\u00d740 + 30\u00d76 + 6\u00d740 + 6\u00d76. \n\n30\u00d740=1200, 30\u00d76=180, 6\u00d740=240, 6\u00d76=36. \n\nAdding them: 1200 + 180 = 1380; 1380 + 240 = 1620; 1620 + 36 = 1656. \n\nSecond method: 36\u00d746. \n\nBreak down 46 into 40 + 6. \n\n36\u00d740=1440, 36\u00d76=216. \n\nAdding them: 1440 + 216. \n\n1440 + 200 = 1640; 1640 + 16 = 1656. \n\nWait, so in the second method, when I broke down 46 into 40 and 6, then multiplied 36 by 40 and 36 by 6, and added them, I get 1440 + 216 = 1656. \n\nBut earlier, when I did the first method, I thought I got 1656, but when I first did the standard multiplication, I thought I got 1696. Wait, no, actually, let me check again. \n\nWait, no, in the second method, when I first thought I did 36\u00d746, I broke it into 36\u00d740 and 36\u00d76. 36\u00d740 is 1440, 36\u00d76 is 216. Then 1440 + 216. Let's compute that again. \n\n1440 + 216. Let's add 1440 + 200 first, which is 1640. Then add 16: 1640 + 16 = 1656. So that's correct. \n\nEarlier, when I thought I added 1440 + 216, I thought I got 1696, but that was a mistake. Wait, no, 1440 + 216 is indeed 1656. So both methods give me 1656. \n\nWait, so where did the 1696 come from? Maybe I miscalculated that. Let me check again. \n\nWait, no, 36\u00d746. Let's do it step by step. \n\nFirst, multiply 36 by 40: 36\u00d740. 36\u00d74=144, so 144\u00d710=1440. \n\nThen, multiply 36 by 6: 36\u00d76=216. \n\nAdd them together: 1440 + 216. \n\n1440 + 200 = 1640. 1640 + 16 = 1656. \n\nYes, that's correct. So both methods give me 1656. So my initial calculation was correct. \n\nBut why did I think there was a discrepancy? Maybe I confused the two different methods. The first method was breaking down 36 and 46 into their tens and ones, then distributing, which gave