 1600. \n\n16 + 40 = 56. \n\nSo 1600 + 56 = 1656. Then add the remaining 0. So 1656. \n\nYes, that's correct. So 216 + 1440 is 1656. \n\nTherefore, all methods confirm that the product is 1656. \n\nI think I was just second-guessing myself unnecessarily. It's easy to make a mistake in arithmetic, but going through multiple methods and verifying each step helps ensure accuracy. \n\nSo, the answer is 1656.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 46 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n36 \\times 46 = (30 + 6) \\times (40 + 6)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 30 \\times 40 + 30 \\times 6 + 6 \\times 40 + 6 \\times 6\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 40 = 1200\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 6 = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 40 = 240\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 6 = 36\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n1200 + 180 + 240 + 36 = 1656\n\\]\n\nVerification through alternative methods (direct multiplication and breaking down the numbers) confirms the result. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{1656}",
