      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 26 and 36. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the method where you split the numbers into smaller numbers that are easier to multiply. Let me think.\n\nFirst, 26 is 20 plus 6, right? And 36 is 30 plus 6. So if I split both numbers, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let me write that out:\n\n26 \u00d7 36 = (20 + 6) \u00d7 (30 + 6)\n\nNow, using the distributive property, which is (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. Let me apply that here.\n\nFirst, multiply 20 by 30. That's straightforward: 20 \u00d7 30 = 600.\n\nNext, 20 multiplied by 6. That's 20 \u00d7 6 = 120.\n\nThen, 6 multiplied by 30. That's 6 \u00d7 30 = 180.\n\nFinally, 6 multiplied by 6. That's 6 \u00d7 6 = 36.\n\nNow, add all those results together: 600 + 120 + 180 + 36.\n\nLet me add them step by step. 600 + 120 is 720. Then, 720 + 180 is 900. Finally, 900 + 36 is 936.\n\nWait, so does that mean the product of 26 and 36 is 936? Let me check that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can multiply 26 by 36 directly. Let's do it step by step. 26 times 30 is 780, and 26 times 6 is 156. Then, adding those together: 780 + 156. Let's see, 780 + 100 is 880, then +56 is 936. Yep, same result. So that seems to check out.\n\nAnother way to verify might be to use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n   26\n\u00d7 36\n-----\n  156  (26 \u00d7 6)\n  780   (26 \u00d7 30, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n 936\n\nAdding 156 and 780 gives 936. So that's consistent. Hmm, seems like 936 is the correct answer. I don't think I made any mistakes here. Both methods gave me the same result, so I'm pretty confident.\n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can use prime factorization. Let's see. 26 is 2 \u00d7 13, and 36 is 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3\u00b2. Multiplying them together: 2 \u00d7 13 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 3\u00b2. Combining the like terms: 2^(1+2) \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 13 = 2\u00b3 \u00d7 3\u00b2 \u00d7 13. Calculating that: 8 \u00d7 9 \u00d7 13. 8 \u00d7 9 is 72, and 72 \u00d7 13. Let's compute 72 \u00d7 10 = 720, 72 \u00d7 3 = 216, so 720 + 216 = 936. Again, same answer. Okay, so three different methods all leading to 936. Definitely, the product is 936.\n\nI think that's solid. No mistakes detected in the process. All methods converge to the same number, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 36 is \\boxed{936}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 36, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n26 \\times 36 = (20 + 6) \\times (30 + 6)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(20 + 6) \\times (30 + 6) = 20 \\times 30 + 20 \\times 6 + 6 \\times 30 + 6 \\times 6\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n20 \\times 30 = 600\n\\]\n\\[\n20 \\times 6 = 120\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 30 = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n6 \\times 6 = 36\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n600 + 120 + 180 + 36 = 936\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this by another method:\n\n\\[\n26 \\times 36 = (26 \\times 30) + (26 \\times 6)\n\\]\n\\[\n26 \\times 30 = 780\n\\]\n\\[\n26 \\times 6 = 156\n\\]\n\\[\n780 + 156 = 936\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 26 and 36 is \\(\\boxed{936}\\).",
