tens. Wait, maybe I need to do it step by step.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying two-digit numbers, you can do it as:\n\n      59\n    x 28\n    ----\n      472  (59 x 8)\n    1180   (59 x 20, shifted one position to the left)\n    ----\n    1652\n\nSo adding 472 and 1180 gives 1652. Yeah, that's the same as before. So all methods lead to 1652. Therefore, I think the product of 59 and 28 is 1652. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 59*28. 50*28=1400, 9*28=252, so 1400+252=1652. Yep, that's correct. So I'm confident the answer is 1652.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 59 and 28 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 28 = (50 + 9) \\times (20 + 8)\n\\]\n\nExpanding the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 20 + 50 \\times 8 + 9 \\times 20 + 9 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n1000 + 400 + 180 + 72 = 1652\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 59 \\\\\n\\times 28 \\\\\n\\hline\n 472 \\quad \\text{(59 x 8)} \\\\\n1180 \\quad \\text{(59 x 20, shifted one position)} \\\\\n\\hline\n1652 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the results of \\(472\\) and \\(1180\\) gives \\(1652\\).\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{1652}",
