alculator here, but I can check with another multiplication approach. Let's see, 39*61. Let's do 39*60 = 2340, plus 39*1 = 39. 2340 + 39 = 2379. Yep, that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm step by step. Let's write it out:\n\n     39\n   x 61\n   ----\n     39  (39 x 1)\n   2340  (39 x 60, shifted one position to the left)\n   ----\n   2379\n\nAdding 39 and 2340 gives 2379. Correct.\n\nAnother way: Maybe breaking down 61 into 50 + 10 + 1. So, 39*(50 + 10 + 1) = 39*50 + 39*10 + 39*1. Let's compute each part:\n\n39*50: 39*5*10 = 195*10 = 1950\n\n39*10: 390\n\n39*1: 39\n\nAdding them up: 1950 + 390 = 2340; 2340 + 39 = 2379. Same answer.\n\nOkay, so all different methods give me 2379. I think that's solid. So the product of 39 and 61 is 2379.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 39 and 61 is \\boxed{2379}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 39 and 61, we can use the distributive property. \n\nFirst, break down 39 into 40 - 1:\n\\[\n39 \\times 61 = (40 - 1) \\times 61\n\\]\n\nNext, apply the distributive property:\n\\[\n(40 - 1) \\times 61 = 40 \\times 61 - 1 \\times 61\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\\[\n40 \\times 61 = 2440\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 61 = 61\n\\]\n\nSubtract the second result from the first:\n\\[\n2440 - 61 = 2379\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this by direct multiplication:\n\\[\n39 \\times 61 = 2379\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 39 and 61 is \\(\\boxed{2379}\\).",
