      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 12 and 17. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 12 by 17. Let me think about how to do that step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 12 times 17. Well, 12 times 10 is 120, and 12 times 7 is 84. Then if I add those together, 120 plus 84. Let me check that. 120 plus 80 is 200, and then plus 4 more is 204. So is the product 204? Wait, let me verify another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write 12 multiplied by 17. Starting from the right, 7 times 2 is 14. Write down 4 and carry over 1. Then 7 times 1 is 7, plus the carried 1 makes 8. Then the next digit, 2 times 1 is 2. So putting it all together, that's 204. Yeah, that matches the first method. So that seems right.\n\nWait, maybe I can check with addition. If I add 12 repeatedly 17 times, that would be tedious, but maybe 12 times 10 is 120, 12 times 7 is 84. 120 plus 84 is 204. Yep, same answer. So I think that's correct. Let me try another method just to be thorough. Maybe using distributive property. 17 can be written as 10 + 7. So 12 times 10 is 120, and 12 times 7 is 84. Then 120 + 84 is 204. Same result again. Okay, so all methods point to 204. I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 12 and 17 is 204.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 12 and 17 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Breaking it down:\n\n12 \u00d7 17  \n= (10 + 2) \u00d7 (10 + 7)  \n= 10\u00d710 + 10\u00d77 + 2\u00d710 + 2\u00d77  \n= 100 + 70 + 20 + 14  \n= 204  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{204}",
