0, 6*60=360, 4200+360=4560. Yep, that's right. \n\nAnother way: 76*60. Let's think of 76 as 70 + 6. So (70 + 6)*60 = 70*60 + 6*60 = 4200 + 360 = 4560. Same answer. \n\nI think that's solid. All different approaches lead to the same result, so I can be pretty sure that 76 multiplied by 60 is 4560. I don't see any other way to approach this problem, so I think that's the correct product.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 76 and 60, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. **Break down 60 into 6 and 10:**\n   - \\( 76 \\times 6 = 456 \\)\n   - \\( 456 \\times 10 = 4560 \\)\n\n2. **Alternatively, multiply directly by recognizing 60 as \\( 6 \\times 10 \\):**\n   - \\( 76 \\times 60 = 76 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (76 \\times 6) \\times 10 = 456 \\times 10 = 4560 \\)\n\n3. **Verification using the distributive property:**\n   - \\( 76 \\times 60 = (70 + 6) \\times 60 = 70 \\times 60 + 6 \\times 60 = 4200 + 360 = 4560 \\)\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{4560}\\).",
