    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 90 and 29. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the smaller number multiple times. But 90 and 29, which one is bigger? 90 is larger than 29. So maybe I should start by multiplying 29 by 90. Wait, does it matter which order I do it in? I think multiplication is commutative, right? So 29 times 90 is the same as 90 times 29. That might make it easier.\n\nAlright, let's break it down. 90 times 29. Maybe breaking 29 into smaller parts would help. Let's see, 29 is close to 30. If I take 90 and multiply it by 30, that's straightforward. 90 times 30 is 2700. But since it's 29, which is one less than 30, I need to subtract 90 from that result. So 2700 minus 90 would be... let me do the subtraction. 2700 minus 90 is 2610. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 30 times 90 is 2700. Subtract 1 times 90, which is 90. So 2700 - 90 is indeed 2610. So does that mean 90 times 29 is 2610? Let me verify another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n   90\nx 29\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 90 by 9. 90 times 9. 9 times 9 is 81, so 90 times 9 is 810. Then, multiply 90 by 20 (since 29 is 20 + 9). 90 times 20 is 1800. Now add those two results together: 810 + 1800. Let's see, 800 + 1800 is 2600, and then 10 + 0 is 10, so total is 2610. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that's good. Both methods give me 2610. So I think that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, maybe I can check using another method. Let's use distributive property. 90 times 29 is the same as 90 times (30 - 1). Which is 90*30 - 90*1. Which is again 2700 - 90 = 2610. Yep, same answer. So three different methods all leading to 2610. I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, I can do a quick check. Let's see, 29 times 100 is 2900. So 29 times 90 is 2900 minus 29 times 10, which is 2900 - 290 = 2610. Oh, that's another way. So 29*100 is 2900, subtract 29*10 (which is 290) gives 2610. Yep, same result. So all methods converge to 2610. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 90 and 29 is 2610.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 90 and 29 is \\boxed{2610}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 90 and 29, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps. \n\nFirst, recognize that 29 is close to 30. Calculate 90 multiplied by 30, which is straightforward:\n\\[\n90 \\times 30 = 2700\n\\]\nSince 29 is one less than 30, subtract 90 from 2700:\n\\[\n2700 - 90 = 2610\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 90 \\\\\n\\times 29 \\\\\n\\hline\n 810 \\quad \\text{(90 times 9)} \\\\\n1800 \\quad \\text{(90 times 20)} \\\\\n\\hline\n2610 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nAdding the results of 810 and 1800 gives:\n\\[\n810 + 1800 = 2610\n\\]\n\nAnother method using the distributive property confirms the result:\n\\[\n90 \\times 29 = 90 \\times (30 - 1) = 90 \\times 30 - 90 \\times 1 = 2700 - 90 = 2610\n\\]\n\nAll methods consistently yield the same result. Therefore, the product of 90 and 29 is \\(\\boxed{2610}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
