s digit, you have to shift one position. So 79 is 7 in the tens place and 9 in the units. So first multiply 11 by 9 (units digit), which is 99. Then multiply 11 by 7 (tens digit), which is 77, then add a zero to make it 770. Then add 99 + 770. \n\n99 + 770: 99 + 700 = 799, 799 + 70 = 869. \n\nYes, that's correct. So both methods give 869. So my initial confusion was due to a miscalculation when I thought 790 + 79 was 809, but that was wrong. So the correct answer is 869. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's see. \n\nAnother way is to use the distributive property differently. For example, 11*79 can be thought of as 11*(80 - 1). So 11*80 - 11*1. 11*80 is 880, and 11*1 is 11. So 880 - 11 = 869. That's another way to get the same result. \n\nSo all methods point to 869. Therefore, the product of 11 and 79 is 869. \n\nI think that's solid. Initially, I made a mistake in the first addition step, but upon checking again, all methods confirm that 11*79=869. So I can be confident that's the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 11 and 79 can be calculated using the distributive property:  \n\\[\n11 \\times 79 = (10 + 1) \\times 79 = 10 \\times 79 + 1 \\times 79 = 790 + 79 = 869\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{869}",
