2790. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 90 and 31 is 2790. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning, and multiple methods confirm the same answer. So I think that's solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 90 and 31 is \\boxed{2790}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 90 and 31, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 90 \\times 30 \\):\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 30 = (9 \\times 10) \\times 30 = 9 \\times (10 \\times 30) = 9 \\times 300 = 2700\n   \\]\n\n2. Add the remaining 90 (since 31 is 30 + 1):\n   \\[\n   2700 + 90 = 2790\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 31 = 90 \\times (30 + 1) = (90 \\times 30) + (90 \\times 1) = 2700 + 90 = 2790\n   \\]\n\nAnother method involves calculating \\( 9 \\times 31 \\) first and then multiplying by 10:\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 31 = 279\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   279 \\times 10 = 2790\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 90 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{2790}\\).",
