      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 64 and 13. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the smaller number multiple times. But 64 times 13 might be a bit tricky because 13 isn't a very round number. Maybe I can break it down to make it easier. \n\nFirst, I know that 13 is 10 plus 3. So maybe I can split 13 into 10 and 3 and multiply each part by 64 separately, then add the results together. Let me try that. \n\nStarting with 64 times 10. Well, 64 times 10 is straightforward. Multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end, so that's 640. \n\nNow, 64 times 3. Hmm, 64 times 3. Let me calculate that. 60 times 3 is 180, and 4 times 3 is 12. So adding those together, 180 + 12 = 192. \n\nNow, add the two results together: 640 + 192. Let me add those. 640 + 100 is 740, then plus 92 more. 740 + 90 is 830, and then +2 is 832. So, 640 + 192 = 832. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 64 times 10 is definitely 640. 64 times 3: 60*3=180, 4*3=12, so 180+12=192. Adding 640 and 192. Let's do it step by step. 640 + 192. Break down 192 into 100 + 90 + 2. So 640 + 100 = 740. Then 740 + 90 = 830. Then 830 + 2 = 832. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nMultiplying 64 by 13. \n\nFirst, multiply 64 by 3 (the units digit of 13). 64*3=192. Then multiply 64 by 1 (the tens digit of 13), but since it's in the tens place, it's actually 64*10=640. Then add the two results: 192 + 640. \n\nAdding 192 and 640. Let's see, 192 + 600 = 792, then +40 = 832. Yep, same result. \n\nAnother way: Maybe using distributive property differently. 64*13 can be thought of as (60 + 4)*13 = 60*13 + 4*13. \n\n60*13: 60*10=600, 60*3=180, so 600+180=780. \n\n4*13: 4*10=40, 4*3=12, so 40+12=52. \n\nAdding 780 + 52: 780 + 50 = 830, then +2 = 832. \n\nSame answer again. So that's reassuring. \n\nWait, maybe I can check using addition