boxed{832}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 64 and 13, we can break down 13 into 10 and 3, then multiply each part by 64 and add the results together.\n\n1. Calculate \\(64 \\times 10\\):\n   \\[\n   64 \\times 10 = 640\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(64 \\times 3\\):\n   \\[\n   64 \\times 3 = 192\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   640 + 192 = 832\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 64 by 3 (units digit of 13):\n  \\[\n  64 \\times 3 = 192\n  \\]\n- Multiply 64 by 1 (tens digit of 13, shifted one position to the left):\n  \\[\n  64 \\times 1 = 64 \\quad \\text{(shifted to the left by one position gives 640)}\n  \\]\n- Add the results:\n  \\[\n  192 + 640 = 832\n  \\]\n\nThus, the product of 64 and 13 is \\(\\boxed{832}\\).",
