   (13 x 9, shifted one position)\n   -----\n   1196\n\nYes, that's correct. So the answer is 1196. Therefore, my initial mistake was a miscalculation when breaking down 13*90. So the correct answer is 1196.\n\nBut wait, now I'm even more confused because I thought the answer was 143, but that was a mistake. So the correct answer is 1196. Let me check once again with another method. Let's use the fact that 13*92 is the same as (10 + 3)*(90 + 2) = 10*90 + 10*2 + 3*90 + 3*2 = 900 + 20 + 270 + 6 = 900 + 20 is 920, 920 + 270 is 1190, 1190 +6 is 1196. So that's correct. Therefore, the product of 13 and 92 is 1196.\n\nSo, after correcting my initial mistake of miscalculating 13*90 as 117 instead of 1170, the correct answer is 1196. Therefore, the product of 13 and 92 is 1196.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 92 is \\boxed{1196}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 92, we can use the distributive property. \n\nFirst, break down 92 into 90 and 2:\n\\[\n13 \\times 92 = 13 \\times (90 + 2)\n\\]\n\nNext, apply the distributive property:\n\\[\n13 \\times 90 + 13 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\\[\n13 \\times 90 = 1170 \\quad \\text{(since } 13 \\times 9 = 117\\text{ and adding a zero gives 1170)}\n\\]\n\\[\n13 \\times 2 = 26\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n1170 + 26 = 1196\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n1. Multiply 13 by 2 (units digit of 92):\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 2 = 26 \\quad \\text{(write down 6, carry over 2)}\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 13 by 9 (tens digit of 92, shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 9 = 117 \\quad \\text{(write down 117, add the carried over 2)}\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   117 + 26 = 1196\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 13 and 92 is \\(\\boxed{1196}\\).",
