th the 0 in the ones place. 11 times 0 is 0. Write down 0.  \nThen move to the 6 in the tens place. 11 times 6 is 66. But since it's in the tens place, we need to add a zero. So 66 becomes 660.  \nSo the result is 660. Yep, same as before.\n\nAnother way: using the fact that 60 is 6*10, so 11*6*10. As I did earlier. 11*6=66, 66*10=660. All methods converge to 660.\n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 11 and 60 is 660. I can't see any mistakes in my reasoning, and all different approaches confirm the same answer. So I'm confident that's correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 11 and 60 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Breaking down 60 into 6 and 10 makes the multiplication easier:\n\n\\[\n11 \\times 60 = 11 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (11 \\times 6) \\times 10\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(11 \\times 6\\):\n\\[\n11 \\times 6 = 66\n\\]\n\nThen multiply the result by 10:\n\\[\n66 \\times 10 = 660\n\\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n11 \\times 60 = (10 + 1) \\times 60 = 10 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 60 = 600 + 60 = 660\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{660}\\).",
