      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 59 and 75. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by breaking them down, maybe using the distributive property. Let me think. 59 is close to 60, right? So maybe I can use that to make the multiplication easier. \n\nIf I take 60 and multiply it by 75 first, that should give me a known value. Let me calculate that. 60 times 75... Well, 60 times 70 is 4200, and 60 times 5 is 300. So adding those together, 4200 + 300 = 4500. So 60 times 75 is 4500. But wait, the original number is 59, not 60. So I need to adjust for that difference. \n\nSince I added 1 to make 60 from 59, that means I need to subtract 1 times 75 from the total. Because 60 is 1 more than 59, so 59 is 60 minus 1. Therefore, 59 times 75 is the same as (60 - 1) times 75. Which would be 60*75 - 1*75. \n\nWe already know 60*75 is 4500, so subtracting 75 from that. Let me do that. 4500 minus 75. Hmm, 4500 minus 70 is 4430, and then minus 5 more is 4425. So does that mean the product of 59 and 75 is 4425? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication directly without breaking it down. Let's try that method to verify. 59 multiplied by 75. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nFirst, multiply 59 by 50, since 75 is 70 + 5. So 59*50. Well, 59*5 is 295, so 59*50 is 2950. Then, multiply 59 by 7 (the remaining part of 75). 59*7. Let's compute that. 50*7 is 350, and 9*7 is 63, so 350 + 63 = 413. Then add those two results together: 2950 + 413. \n\nAdding 2950 + 400 would be 3350, and then add 13 more to get 3363. Wait, that's different from the previous answer. Hmm, so now I have two different results. That's confusing. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in one of the steps. Let me check again. \n\nFirst method: (60 - 1)*75 = 60*75 - 75 = 4500 - 75 = 4425. \n\nSecond method: 59*75 = 59*(70 + 5) = 59*70 + 59*5. \n\n59*70: 59*7*10. 59*7 is 413, so 413*10 = 4130. \n\n59*5: 295. \n\nAdding 4130 + 295. Let's do that again. 4130 + 200 = 4330, then +95 = 4425. Oh! Wait, I must have miscalculated earlier when I thought the second method gave 3363. Let me check that again. \n\nWait, no, actually, when I first did the second method, I thought 59*7 is 413 and 59*50 is 2950. Then adding 2950 + 413. Let me add them step by step. 2950 + 400 is 3350, then +13 is 3363. Wait, that's conflicting. But now, when I recompute, I get 4130 + 295 = 4425. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, no, hold on. 59*70 is 4130, right? Because 59*7 is 413, so 59*70 is 413*10 = 4130. Then 59*5 is 295. So 4130 + 295. Let's add 4130 + 200 = 4330, then +95. 4330 + 95. 4330 + 90 = 4420, then +5 = 4425. Oh! Wait, I must have added wrong earlier. So 4130 + 295 is indeed 4425. So both methods give the same result. Phew, that was confusing. So the answer is 4425. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication:\n\n   59\nx 75\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 59 by 5:\n\n59\nx  5\n-----\n295\n\nThen multiply 59 by 7 (remember to shift one position to the left because it's the tens place):\n\n59\nx  7\n-----\n413\n   --\n   0\n   --\n   4130\n\nNow add them together:\n\n  295\n+4130\n------\n4425\n\nYes, that's 4425. So that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another approach. Let's think of 75 as 3*25. So 59*3*25. Let's compute 59*3 first. 59*3 is 177. Then 177*25. Hmm, 177*25. Well, 177*20 = 3540, and 177*5 = 885. Adding those together: 3540 + 885. 3540 + 800 = 4340, then +85 = 4425. Again, same result. \n\nSo all methods lead to 4425. Therefore, the product of 59 and 75 is 4425. \n\nI think that's solid. All different methods confirm the same answer. So I can be confident that 59 multiplied by 75 is 4425.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 59 and 75 is \\boxed{4425}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 59 and 75, we can use the distributive property. \n\nFirst, we recognize that 75 can be broken down into 70 and 5. Therefore, we calculate:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 75 = 59 \\times (70 + 5)\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the multiplication over addition:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 70 + 59 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n1. \\(59 \\times 70\\):\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 70 = (60 - 1) \\times 70 = 60 \\times 70 - 1 \\times 70 = 4200 - 70 = 4130\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(59 \\times 5\\):\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 5 = 295\n   \\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n4130 + 295 = 4425\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by breaking down 75 into 50 and 25:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 75 = 59 \\times (50 + 25)\n\\]\n\nDistributing the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n59 \\times 50 + 59 \\times 25\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n1. \\(59 \\times 50\\):\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 50 = 2950\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(59 \\times 25\\):\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 25 = 1475\n   \\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n2950 + 1475 = 4425\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 59 and 75 is \\(\\boxed{4425}\\).",
