      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 31 and 39. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done by adding the number to itself as many times as the other number. But 31 and 39 are both two-digit numbers, so maybe there's a smarter way than just brute-forcing the multiplication.\n\nWait, maybe I can use some kind of shortcut or trick here. Let me think. If I have 31 times 39, that's like (30 + 1)(30 + 9). Oh, right! Because 31 is 30 + 1 and 39 is 30 + 9. So expanding that, it would be 30*30 + 30*9 + 1*30 + 1*9. Let me calculate each part step by step.\n\nFirst, 30 times 30 is 900. Then, 30 times 9 is 270. Next, 1 times 30 is 30. And finally, 1 times 9 is 9. Now, adding all those together: 900 + 270 is 1170. Then, 1170 + 30 is 1200. Finally, adding 9 gives 1209. So, is the product 1209? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to verify. I know that 31 times 40 would be 1240, right? Because 31*40 is the same as 31*4*10, which is 124*10=1240. But since it's 31 times 39, that's 31 times 40 minus 31. So 1240 - 31. Let's subtract: 1240 minus 30 is 1210, then minus 1 more is 1209. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that seems correct.\n\nWait, another way to check. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's do 31 times 39. Multiply 31 by 9 first, then multiply 31 by 30, and add them together. So 31*9. Let's compute that. 30*9 is 270, and 1*9 is 9, so 270 + 9 = 279. Then, 31*30. 30*30 is 900, 30*1 is 30, so 900 + 30 = 930. Then add 279 and 930. 279 + 900 is 1179, then +30 is 1209. Yep, same answer. So that's three different methods giving me 1209. I think that's solid.\n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, let me try another verification. Let's see, 31*39. If I add 31 and 39 first, that's 70. Then, 70 times 50 would be 3500, but that's way too high. Wait, no, that's not the way. Wait, actually, 31*39 is (30 +1)(30 +9) which we already did. Alternatively, maybe use distributive property differently.\n\nAlternatively, think of 39 as 40 -1. So 31*(40 -1) = 31*40 - 31*1 = 1240 -31 = 1209. That's the same as before. So that's another way to see it. So all these methods converge on 1209. I think that's correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, let me do the multiplication step by step. 31 times 39. Let's write it out:\n\n   31\nx 39\n-----\n  279   (31 x 9)\n 930    (31 x 30, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n1209\n\nYes, adding 279 and 930 gives 1209. Perfect. So all the different methods confirm that the product is 1209. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. So I'm confident that the answer is 1209.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 31 and 39 is \\boxed{1209}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 31 and 39, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n31 \\times 39 = (30 + 1)(30 + 9)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this, we get:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 30 + 30 \\times 9 + 1 \\times 30 + 1 \\times 9\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term step by step:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 30 = 900\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 9 = 270\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 30 = 30\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 9 = 9\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n900 + 270 + 30 + 9 = 1209\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by recognizing that \\(31 \\times 39 = 31 \\times (40 - 1)\\):\n\n\\[\n31 \\times 40 - 31 \\times 1 = 1240 - 31 = 1209\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 31 and 39 is \\(\\boxed{1209}\\).",
