      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 30 and 75. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 30 by 75. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe breaking down the numbers would help. I know that 30 is 3 times 10, and 75 is 70 plus 5. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. So, if I take 3 times 10 and multiply that by 75, then add the other part. Wait, actually, the distributive property is a*(b + c) = ab + ac. So maybe I can write 75 as 70 + 5 and then distribute the multiplication over addition.\n\nLet me try that. So, 30 times 75 is the same as 30*(70 + 5). That would be 30*70 + 30*5. Let me compute each part separately. 30 times 70... Well, 3 times 7 is 21, and then there are two zeros from the 10 and 10, so 21 followed by two zeros is 2100. Then 30 times 5 is 150. So adding those together, 2100 + 150. That should be 2250. So is the product 2250? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can multiply 30 by 75 directly. Let's see. 30 times 75. Well, 30 times 70 is 2100, and 30 times 5 is 150. So again, adding those gives 2100 + 150 = 2250. Hmm, same result. That seems consistent.\n\nWait, another way to check might be to do it in another method. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that. Multiply 30 by 75. Let's ignore the zeros first. 30 is 3*10, 75 is 75. So 3*75 is 225, then multiply by 10 gives 2250. Yeah, that works too. So 3*75 is 225, then times 10 is 2250. So that's another way to get the same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 75 is 3*25. So 30*75 is 30*3*25. 30*3 is 90, then 90*25. Hmm, 90*25. Well, 9*25 is 225, so 90*25 is 2250. Yep, same answer again. So all these different methods are giving me 2250. That gives me more confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can do the multiplication directly. Let's write it out:\n\n   30\nx 75\n-----\n?\n\nMultiplying 30 by 75. Starting from the right, 5 times 30 is 150. Then the 7 in the tens place. So 7 times 30 is 210, but since it's in the tens place, it's 2100. Then add 150 and 2100. 150 + 2100 is 2250. Yep, same result. So all methods converge to 2250.\n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 30 and 75 is 2250. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm the same answer. So I'm pretty confident that's the right one.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 30 and 75 is \\boxed{2250}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 30 and 75, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 75 = 30 \\times (70 + 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, distribute the multiplication over addition:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 70 + 30 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 70 = 2100\n\\]\n\\[\n30 \\times 5 = 150\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\n\\[\n2100 + 150 = 2250\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by recognizing that \\(75 = 3 \\times 25\\):\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 75 = 30 \\times (3 \\times 25) = (30 \\times 3) \\times 25\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 3 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 25 = 2250\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 30 and 75 is \\(\\boxed{2250}\\).",
