      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 68 and 77. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done step by step, but maybe there's a quicker way to do it mentally. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 68 into 70 minus 2. That might make it easier to multiply by 77. So, 68 is 70 - 2. Then, the problem becomes (70 - 2) multiplied by 77. If I distribute that, it would be 70 times 77 minus 2 times 77. Let me write that out:\n\n70 \u00d7 77 - 2 \u00d7 77\n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. First, 70 times 77. I know that 70 times 70 is 4900, and 70 times 7 is 490. So adding those together, 4900 + 490 = 5390. So, 70 \u00d7 77 is 5390.\n\nNext, 2 times 77. That's straightforward. 2 \u00d7 70 is 140, and 2 \u00d7 7 is 14, so 140 + 14 = 154. So, 2 \u00d7 77 is 154.\n\nNow, subtract the second result from the first: 5390 - 154. Let me do that subtraction. 5390 minus 100 is 5290, minus 50 is 5240, minus 4 is 5236. Wait, is that right? Let me check again. 5390 - 154. Maybe another way: 5390 - 100 = 5290, then subtract 50 more to get 5240, then subtract 4 more: 5240 - 4 = 5236. Yeah, that seems correct.\n\nSo, according to this method, the product of 68 and 77 is 5236. Let me verify that with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  68\n\u00d7 77\n-------\n\nFirst, multiply 68 by 7. 60 \u00d7 7 = 420, and 8 \u00d7 7 = 56. So, 420 + 56 = 476. That's the result of multiplying 68 by 7.\n\nNext, multiply 68 by 70 (since 77 is 70 + 7). 68 \u00d7 70. Well, 68 \u00d7 7 is 476, so 68 \u00d7 70 is 4760. Then, add those two results together: 476 + 4760. Let's see, 476 + 4760. 4760 + 400 = 5160, then +76 = 5236. So, same result. That's reassuring.\n\nWait, so both methods give me 5236. Let me try one more way to confirm. Maybe using distributive property differently. 77 is 70 + 7, so 68 \u00d7 77 = 68 \u00d7 (70 + 7) = 68 \u00d7 70 + 68 \u00d7 7. Which is exactly what I did in the second method. So that's the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 77 is 7*11. So, 68 \u00d7 7 \u00d7 11. Let's compute 68 \u00d7 7 first, which we already know is 476. Then, 476 \u00d7 11. Hmm, how do I compute that? Well, 476 \u00d7 10 is 4760, plus 476 is 4760 + 476. Let's add those. 4760 + 400 = 5160, then +76 = 5236. Again, same answer. So, three different methods all leading to 5236. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can check with another multiplication. Let's see, 5236 divided by 68 should be 77. Let's check that division. 68 \u00d7 70 = 4760. 5236 - 4760 = 476. Then, 68 \u00d7 7 = 476. So, 70 + 7 = 77. Therefore, 68 \u00d7 77 = 68 \u00d7 (70 + 7) = 4760 + 476 = 5236. Yep, that checks out.\n\nSo, all methods converge to 5236. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 68 and 77 is 5236.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 68 and 77 is \\boxed{5236}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 68 and 77, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times 77 = (70 - 2) \\times 77 = 70 \\times 77 - 2 \\times 77\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(70 \\times 77\\):\n\n\\[\n70 \\times 77 = 70 \\times (70 + 7) = 70 \\times 70 + 70 \\times 7 = 4900 + 490 = 5390\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(2 \\times 77\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 77 = 154\n\\]\n\nSubtract the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n5390 - 154 = 5236\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by breaking down 77 into 70 and 7:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times 77 = 68 \\times (70 + 7) = 68 \\times 70 + 68 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times 70 = 4760\n\\]\n\\[\n68 \\times 7 = 476\n\\]\n\nAdd the results:\n\n\\[\n4760 + 476 = 5236\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 68 and 77 is \\(\\boxed{5236}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
