      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 13 and 60. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I just have to multiply 13 by 60. Let me think about how to do that step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe breaking down the numbers would help. I know that 60 is 6 times 10, so maybe I can use that to make the multiplication easier. So, 13 multiplied by 60 is the same as 13 times 6 times 10. Let me write that out: 13 \u00d7 60 = 13 \u00d7 (6 \u00d7 10). \n\nAlright, multiplication is associative, so I can rearrange the order. That means 13 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 10 is the same as (13 \u00d7 6) \u00d7 10. Let me compute 13 \u00d7 6 first. 13 times 6... Well, 10 times 6 is 60, and 3 times 6 is 18. So adding those together, 60 + 18 = 78. So now I have 78 \u00d7 10. That's straightforward, multiplying by 10 just adds a zero, so 78 \u00d7 10 = 780. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. If I do 13 \u00d7 60 directly, maybe another way. Let's try the standard multiplication method. \n\n13\n\u00d760\n------\nFirst, multiply 13 by 0, which is 0. Then multiply 13 by 60. Wait, maybe I should do it step by step. \n\nAlternatively, think of 60 as 6 tens. So 13 times 6 tens. So 13 times 6 is 78, and then times 10 is 780. Yeah, that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe do it the standard algorithm. Let's write it out:\n\n   13\n\u00d7 60\n-------\n   0 (13 \u00d7 0)\n 78  (13 \u00d7 6, shifted one position to the left)\n-------\n 780\n\nYes, adding those up gives 780. So that checks out. \n\nAnother way to verify could be to use addition. 60 added six times 13. Let's see: 60 + 60 = 120, plus 60 = 180, plus 60 = 240, plus 60 = 300, plus 60 = 360. Wait, that's only four times. Wait, no, 60 added six times is 60\u00d76. Wait, but 13\u00d760 is 13\u00d76\u00d710, which is 78\u00d710=780. So adding 60 six times would be 60\u00d76=360, then times 10 is 3600. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, no, 13\u00d760 is 13 multiplied by 60, not 13 multiplied by 60 times. Wait, no, 13\u00d760 is 13\u00d76\u00d710. So 13\u00d76 is 78, then 78\u00d710 is 780. \n\nBut if I do 60 added six times, that's 60\u00d76=360, then 360\u00d710=3600. Wait, that's conflicting. Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, 13\u00d760 is 13 multiplied by 60, not 60 multiplied by 60. So 60\u00d760 is 3600, but that's different. So maybe I confused something here. \n\nWait, let's clarify. 13 multiplied by 60. If I break down 60 into 6 and 10, then 13\u00d76=78, then 78\u00d710=780. That's correct. Alternatively, 60\u00d713 is the same as 60\u00d710 + 60\u00d73 = 600 + 180 = 780. Yes, that works too. So both methods give 780. \n\nSo, I think that's the right answer. Let me just do a quick check with another method. Maybe using distributive property differently. 13\u00d760 = (10 + 3)\u00d760 = 10\u00d760 + 3\u00d760 = 600 + 180 = 780. Yep, that's the same result. \n\nAlternatively, using decimal multiplication. 13.0 \u00d7 60.0. Let's do it step by step. 13 \u00d7 60. 13 \u00d7 6 is 78, so 13 \u00d7 60 is 780. \n\nI think all methods lead to 780. So I'm pretty confident that the product of 13 and 60 is 780. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 60 is \\boxed{780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 60, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps. \n\nFirst, recognize that 60 can be expressed as \\(6 \\times 10\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the multiplication as:\n\\[\n13 \\times 60 = 13 \\times (6 \\times 10)\n\\]\n\nNext, use the associative property of multiplication to rearrange the order:\n\\[\n13 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (13 \\times 6) \\times 10\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(13 \\times 6\\) first:\n\\[\n13 \\times 6 = 78\n\\]\n\nThen multiply the result by 10:\n\\[\n78 \\times 10 = 780\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by breaking down 60 into 6 and 10:\n\\[\n13 \\times 60 = (10 + 3) \\times 60 = 10 \\times 60 + 3 \\times 60 = 600 + 180 = 780\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 13 and 60 is \\(\\boxed{780}\\).",
