      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 17 and 55. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the first number to itself as many times as the second number. So, 17 times 55 would be 17 added to itself 55 times. But that seems like a lot of addition. There must be a smarter way to do this without having to do all that work manually.\n\nWait, maybe I can break it down using the distributive property. I know that 55 can be written as 50 plus 5. So, 17 multiplied by 50 plus 17 multiplied by 5. Let me check if that works. Yeah, because 50 + 5 is 55, so that should be correct. \n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. First, 17 times 50. Hmm, 17 times 5 is 85, right? Because 10 times 5 is 50, and 7 times 5 is 35, so 50 + 35 is 85. Then, 17 times 5 is 85. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, 17 times 50 is actually 17 multiplied by 5 times 10. So, 17 times 5 is 85, then times 10 would be 850. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me recalculate.\n\nWait, 17 times 50. Let's think of 50 as 5 times 10. So, 17 times 5 is 85, then times 10 is 850. Yeah, that's correct. Then 17 times 5 is 85. So, adding those together: 850 plus 85. Let's do that. 850 + 80 is 930, and then +5 is 935. So, 17 times 55 is 935. Hmm, that seems right. Let me verify with another method to make sure.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n  17\nx 55\n----\n\nFirst, multiply 17 by 5. 17 times 5 is 85. Then, multiply 17 by 50 (since 5 in the tens place). That would be 850. Then add 85 and 850. 85 + 850 is 935. Yep, same result. So that checks out.\n\nWait, another way to check could be using prime factors. Let's see. 17 is a prime number, so it's just 17. 55 is 5 times 11. So, 17 times 5 is 85, and 85 times 11. Hmm, 85 times 11. That's the same as 850 plus 85, which is 935. So that's another confirmation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using estimation. 17 is approximately 20, and 55 is approximately 50. 20 times 50 is 1000. Since 17 is 3 less than 20 and 55 is 5 less than 50, the actual product should be a bit less than 1000. 3 less than 20 is 17, and 5 less than 50 is 45. So, 17 times 45. Let's compute that. 17 times 40 is 680, and 17 times 5 is 85. So, 680 + 85 is 765. Then, since we estimated 1000 and the actual numbers are 3 less and 5 less, the difference would be 3*50 + 5*20 = 150 + 100 = 250. So, 1000 - 250 = 750. Wait, but our actual answer was 935, which is way higher than 750. Hmm, maybe my estimation wasn't as good. Maybe that method isn't the best here. Maybe I should stick with the exact methods.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator. But since I don't have a calculator, I need to rely on my mental math. Let me try another approach. 17 times 55. Let's think of 55 as 5 times 11. So, 17 times 5 is 85, then 85 times 11. 85 times 10 is 850, plus 85 is 935. Yep, same answer. So, that's consistent.\n\nWait, maybe breaking down 55 into different components. For example, 55 is 60 minus 5. So, 17 times 60 is 1020, and 17 times 5 is 85. Then, 1020 minus 85 is 935. That works too. So, 17*(60-5) = 17*60 - 17*5 = 1020 - 85 = 935. Perfect, that's another way to get the same result.\n\nSo, all these different methods are leading me to 935. Let me just verify once more with a different multiplication technique. Let's use the fact that 17*55 = (10 + 7)*55 = 10*55 + 7*55. 10*55 is 550, and 7*55 is 385. Then, 550 + 385. 550 + 300 is 850, plus 85 is 935. Yep, same answer again.\n\nAlright, so all these different methods are converging on 935. I think that's solid. Therefore, the product of 17 and 55 is 935.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 17 and 55 is \\boxed{935}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 17 and 55, we can use the distributive property. \n\nFirst, break down 55 into 50 and 5:\n\\[\n17 \\times 55 = 17 \\times (50 + 5)\n\\]\n\nNext, distribute the multiplication:\n\\[\n17 \\times 50 + 17 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\\[\n17 \\times 50 = 850 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 17 \\times 5 = 85\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n850 + 85 = 935\n\\]\n\nVerification using different methods confirms the result. For example, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 17 \\\\\n\\times 55 \\\\\n\\hline\n 85 \\quad \\text{(17 \u00d7 5)} \\\\\n850 \\quad \\text{(17 \u00d7 50)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 935 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method involves breaking down 55 into 60 and -5:\n\\[\n17 \\times (60 - 5) = 17 \\times 60 - 17 \\times 5 = 1020 - 85 = 935\n\\]\n\nAll methods consistently lead to the same result. Therefore, the product of 17 and 55 is \\(\\boxed{935}\\).",
