      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 51 and 33. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers together gives the total size when you combine their groups. But I'm not exactly sure how to do it step by step. Let me think.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 51 into smaller parts. I know that 51 is 50 plus 1. So, if I multiply 50 by 33 and then add 1 times 33, that should give me the same result as multiplying 51 by 33. That makes sense because of the distributive property, right? So, 51 \u00d7 33 = (50 + 1) \u00d7 33 = 50\u00d733 + 1\u00d733.\n\nAlright, let's calculate each part separately. Starting with 50 times 33. Hmm, 50 times anything is just 50 multiplied by the units digit, so 50\u00d730 is 1500, and 50\u00d73 is 150. Adding those together, 1500 + 150 = 1650. So, 50\u00d733 is 1650.\n\nNow, the other part is 1 times 33. That's straightforward, right? 1 multiplied by any number is just the number itself. So, 1\u00d733 = 33.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 1650 + 33. Let me do that addition. 1650 + 30 is 1680, and then adding the remaining 3 gives 1683. So, 51\u00d733 should be 1683.\n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Sometimes when I do mental math, I might slip up. Let me verify each step.\n\nFirst, breaking down 51 into 50 and 1 is correct. Then, distributing the multiplication over addition: (50 + 1) \u00d7 33 = 50\u00d733 + 1\u00d733. That's the distributive property, so that's right. Then, calculating 50\u00d733. Breaking down 50 into 5\u00d710, so 5\u00d733 is 165, and 10\u00d733 is 330. Adding 165 and 330 gives 495. Wait, hold on, that contradicts my earlier calculation. Wait, no, maybe I messed up here.\n\nWait, no, actually, 50\u00d733. Let me do it another way. 50\u00d733 is the same as 33\u00d750. 33 times 50. 33 times 10 is 330, so 330 times 5 is 1650. Oh, okay, that's correct. So 50\u00d733 is indeed 1650. Then 1\u00d733 is 33. Adding 1650 + 33 gives 1683. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication directly without breaking it down. Let's try that method to cross-verify.\n\nMultiplying 51 by 33. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nFirst, write 51 \u00d7 33.\n\nMultiply 33 by 1 (the units digit of 51). That's 33. Then multiply 33 by 50 (the tens digit of 51, but since it's actually 5 in the tens place and 1 in the units place, maybe I need to adjust). Wait, actually, in the standard multiplication method, you multiply the entire number. So 33 \u00d7 51.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nFirst, multiply 33 by 1: 33.\n\nThen, multiply 33 by 50 (since 51 is 50 + 1). So, 33 \u00d7 50. 33 \u00d7 5 is 165, so 165 \u00d7 10 is 1650.\n\nAdding those two results: 1650 + 33 = 1683. So that's the same answer as before. Okay, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the FOIL method for multiplying two-digit numbers. Wait, but 51 and 33 are both two-digit numbers. Let's see:\n\n(50 + 1)(30 + 3) = 50\u00d730 + 50\u00d73 + 1\u00d730 + 1\u00d73. Calculating each term:\n\n50\u00d730 = 1500\n\n50\u00d73 = 150\n\n1\u00d730 = 30\n\n1\u00d73 = 3\n\nAdding all those together: 1500 + 150 = 1650; 1650 + 30 = 1680; 1680 + 3 = 1683. Again, same result. So that's consistent.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are leading me to 1683. So I think that's the correct answer. But just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can check with a calculator. Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can recall that 50\u00d730 is 1500, 50\u00d73 is 150, 1\u00d730 is 30, 1\u00d73 is 3. So adding 1500 + 150 + 30 + 3. 1500 + 150 is 1650, 1650 + 30 is 1680, 1680 + 3 is 1683. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another approach. Let's think of 33 as 30 + 3 again. So 51 \u00d7 33 = 51 \u00d7 (30 + 3) = 51\u00d730 + 51\u00d73. 51\u00d730 is 1530, and 51\u00d73 is 153. Adding those together: 1530 + 153. 1530 + 100 is 1630, 1630 + 53 is 1683. Again, same answer.\n\nOkay, so all different methods of breaking down the multiplication give me 1683. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. Each step checks out when I verify it another way. So I think the product of 51 and 33 is 1683.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 51 and 33 is \\boxed{1683}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 51 and 33, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n51 \\times 33 = (50 + 1) \\times 33 = 50 \\times 33 + 1 \\times 33\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(50 \\times 33\\):\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 33 = 1650\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(1 \\times 33\\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\times 33 = 33\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\n\\[\n1650 + 33 = 1683\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by breaking down the multiplication directly:\n\n\\[\n(50 + 1)(30 + 3) = 50 \\times 30 + 50 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 30 + 1 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 30 = 1500\n\\]\n\\[\n50 \\times 3 = 150\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 30 = 30\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\n\\[\n1500 + 150 + 30 + 3 = 1683\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 51 and 33 is \\(\\boxed{1683}\\).",
