add 67 + 2000 first, which is 2067. Then add the remaining 10: 2067 +10 = 2077. \n\nOkay, same result. So that's good. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method. Let's see. 31 is 30 +1, so 67*30 +67*1. 67*30 is 2010, and 67*1 is 67. Then 2010 +67 is 2077. Yep, same answer. \n\nWait, maybe check with another approach. For example, using the fact that 67*31 is the same as 31*67. Let's see, maybe use another multiplication trick. \n\nAlternatively, think of 31 as 30 +1 and 67 as 70 -9. Wait, 70 -9 is 61, which isn't 67. Maybe that's not helpful. Alternatively, 67 is 70 -3. Then 70*31 is 2170, and subtract 3*31 which is 93. So 2170 -93. Let's compute that. 2170 -90 is 2080, then subtract 3 more: 2077. Oh, that works too! So that's another way. \n\nSo, 67*31 = (70 -3)*31 = 70*31 -3*31 = 2170 -93 = 2077. \n\nSo, all methods lead to 2077. Therefore, the product of 67 and 31 is 2077. \n\nI think that's solid. Let me just check with a calculator to be absolutely sure. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe approximate. 67 is roughly 70, 31 is roughly 30. 70*30 is 2100. So 2100 minus 3*30 (since 70 is 30 more than 67) which is 90. So 2100 -90 is 2010. Then, since 67 is 3 less than 70, and 31 is 1, maybe adjust. Wait, that might not be the right way. Alternatively, 67 is 70 -3, 31 is 30 +1. So, (70 -3)(30 +1) = 70*30 +70*1 -3*30 -3*1 = 2100 +70 -90 -3 = 2100 -23 = 2077. Yep, same answer. \n\nSo, all different methods confirm that the product is 2077. Therefore, I can be confident that the answer is 2077.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 31 is \\boxed{2077}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 31, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 31 = (60 + 7) \\times (30 + 1)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down step by step:\n\n1. Calculate \\(60 \\times 30\\):\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 30 = 1800\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(60 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 1 = 60\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(7 \\times 30\\):\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 30 = 210\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate \\(7 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 1 = 7\n   \\]\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\\[\n1800 + 60 + 210 + 7 = 2077\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by considering the standard multiplication approach:\n\n1. Multiply 67 by 1 (units digit of 31):\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 1 = 67\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 67 by 3 (tens digit of 31):\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 3 = 201\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results from steps 1 and 2, considering the place values:\n   \\[\n   2010 + 67 = 2077\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 67 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{2077}\\).",
