 is 110. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning. Let me just check with a calculator in my mind. 10 times 10 is 100, 10 times 1 is 10, so 100 + 10 = 110. Yep, that's right. So the answer is 110.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 11 and 10 is found by multiplying them together. Breaking it down:\n\n11 \u00d7 10 = (10 + 1) \u00d7 10  \n= 10 \u00d7 10 + 1 \u00d7 10  \n= 100 + 10  \n= 110\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{110}",
