      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 59 and 47. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down, maybe using the distributive property. Let me think. \n\nFirst, 59 is close to 60, right? So if I take 60 times 47 and then subtract 1 times 47, that should work because 59 is 60 minus 1. Let me check that. \n\nSo, 60 times 47. Well, 60 times 40 is 2400, and 60 times 7 is 420. Adding those together gives 2400 + 420 = 2820. Then subtract 47 from that. Wait, is that right? Because 59 is 60 minus 1, so 60*47 - 1*47. Yeah, that makes sense. So 2820 minus 47. Let me do that subtraction. \n\n2820 minus 40 is 2780, and then minus 7 more would be 2773. So is the product 2773? Let me verify that another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. \n\nMultiply 59 by 47. \n\nFirst, multiply 59 by 7. 59*7. Let's compute that. 7*50 is 350, and 7*9 is 63. So 350 + 63 = 413. \n\nThen, multiply 59 by 40. Because 47 is 40 + 7. So 59*40. 59*4 is 236, so times 10 is 2360. \n\nNow add those two results together: 413 + 2360. Let's see. 413 + 2000 is 2413, then plus 360 is 2413 + 360. 2413 + 300 is 2713, then +60 is 2773. \n\nOkay, so both methods give me 2773. That seems consistent. Let me check with another approach just to be thorough. \n\nAnother way is to use the fact that (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd. So if I let 59 = 60 - 1 and 47 = 40 + 7, then:\n\n(60 - 1)(40 + 7) = 60*40 + 60*7 - 1*40 - 1*7. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n60*40 = 2400\n\n60*7 = 420\n\n-1*40 = -40\n\n-1*7 = -7\n\nAdding those together: 2400 + 420 = 2820; 2820 - 40 = 2780; 2780 - 7 = 2773. \n\nSame result. Hmm, seems like 2773 is the correct answer. \n\nWait, maybe I can check using a calculator? Well, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can remember that 60*47 is 2820, subtract 47 gives 2773. Alternatively, maybe I can add 59 + 47 first? Wait, no, addition and multiplication are separate. But just to see, 59 + 47 is 106, but that's addition, not helpful here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe break down 47 into 50 - 3. Let's try that. \n\nSo 59*47 = 59*(50 - 3) = 59*50 - 59*3. \n\n59*50 is 2950. 59*3 is 177. Then subtract 177 from 2950. \n\n2950 - 100 is 2850, then subtract 77 more: 2850 - 77 = 2773. \n\nAgain, same answer. Okay, so all different methods are leading me to 2773. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. \n\nJust to recap, the steps I took were:\n\n1. Recognize that 59 is 60 - 1, so use distributive property to break it down.\n2. Calculated 60*47 = 2820, then subtracted 47 to get 2773.\n3. Verified using standard multiplication: 59*7=413 and 59*40=2360, added them to get 2773.\n4. Tried another decomposition: 47 as 50 - 3, leading to 59*50 - 59*3 = 2950 - 177 = 2773.\n5. All methods converged on 2773, so I'm pretty sure that's the right answer.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Each step checks out. So the product of 59 and 47 is 2773.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 59 and 47 can be calculated using the distributive property. Here's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. Express 59 as \\(60 - 1\\) and 47 as \\(40 + 7\\).\n2. Apply the distributive property:  \n   \\[\n   (60 - 1)(40 + 7) = 60 \\cdot 40 + 60 \\cdot 7 - 1 \\cdot 40 - 1 \\cdot 7\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term:  \n   - \\(60 \\cdot 40 = 2400\\)  \n   - \\(60 \\cdot 7 = 420\\)  \n   - \\(1 \\cdot 40 = 40\\)  \n   - \\(1 \\cdot 7 = 7\\)  \n4. Combine the results:  \n   \\[\n   2400 + 420 - 40 - 7 = 2820 - 47 = 2773\n   \\]\n\nVerification using standard multiplication:  \n- \\(59 \\times 7 = 413\\)  \n- \\(59 \\times 40 = 2360\\)  \n- Add the results: \\(413 + 2360 = 2773\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{2773}\\).",
