  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 22 and 85. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers means adding the smaller number multiple times. But 22 and 85 are both two-digit numbers, so maybe there's a way to break them down to make it easier. Let me think.\n\nFirst, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. 22 times 85. I can write it down like 22  \n\u00d7 85  \nThen, multiply 22 by 5 first because 85 is 80 plus 5. Wait, actually, maybe breaking it into 20 and 5 would be better. Let me try that.\n\nSo, 22 \u00d7 85 can be split into 20 \u00d7 85 + 2 \u00d7 85. Let's compute each part separately.\n\n20 \u00d7 85: 20 times 80 is 1600, and 20 times 5 is 100. So adding those together, 1600 + 100 = 1700.\n\nThen, 2 \u00d7 85: 2 times 80 is 160, and 2 times 5 is 10. So 160 + 10 = 170.\n\nNow, add those two results together: 1700 + 170. Let's see, 1700 + 100 is 1800, and then +70 more would be 1870. So, 22 \u00d7 85 = 1870. Wait, is that right?\n\nLet me check another way to verify. Maybe using the distributive property differently. 85 is 80 + 5, so 22 \u00d7 80 + 22 \u00d7 5. Let's compute that.\n\n22 \u00d7 80: 20 \u00d7 80 is 1600, and 2 \u00d7 80 is 160. So 1600 + 160 = 1760.\n\n22 \u00d7 5: 20 \u00d7 5 = 100, and 2 \u00d7 5 = 10. So 100 + 10 = 110.\n\nAdding those together: 1760 + 110. 1760 + 100 is 1860, plus 10 is 1870. Same result. Okay, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 22 is 11 \u00d7 2, so 11 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 85. Let's see. 11 \u00d7 85 first. 11 \u00d7 80 = 880, and 11 \u00d7 5 = 55. So 880 + 55 = 935. Then multiply by 2: 935 \u00d7 2. 900 \u00d7 2 = 1800, 35 \u00d7 2 = 70. So 1800 + 70 = 1870. Again, same answer. Good, so three different methods all leading to 1870. That gives me confidence.\n\nWait, maybe another way. Let's do it the standard way, multiplying 22 by 85. \n\nMultiplying 22 by 85:\n\nFirst, ignore the two-digit numbers and multiply 22 by 85 as if they were both single-digit numbers. So 22 \u00d7 85. Let's do 22 \u00d7 80 and 22 \u00d7 5.\n\n22 \u00d7 80: 20 \u00d7 80 = 1600, 2 \u00d7 80 = 160. So 1600 + 160 = 1760.\n\n22 \u00d7 5: 20 \u00d7 5 = 100, 2 \u00d7 5 = 10. So 100 + 10 = 110.\n\nAdding 1760 + 110 = 1870. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the vertical multiplication method.\n\n   22\n\u00d7 85\n-----\n110  (22 \u00d7 5)\n1760  (22 \u00d7 80, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n1870\n\nAdding the two partial products: 110 + 1760 = 1870. Perfect.\n\nAnother check: 22 \u00d7 85. Let's see, 22 \u00d7 85. If I know that 20 \u00d7 85 = 1700, and 2 \u00d7 85 = 170, so 1700 + 170 = 1870. All methods converge here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using decimal multiplication. 22.0 \u00d7 85.0. Let's do that.\n\n22.0\n\u00d785.0\n--------\n00.0 (22 \u00d7 0.0)\n110.0 (22 \u00d7 5.0, shifted one position)\n1760.0 (22 \u00d7 80.0, shifted two positions)\n--------\n1870.0\n\nAdding them up: 0.0 + 110.0 = 110.0; 110.0 + 1760.0 = 1870.0. Same result.\n\nSo, all different methods of multiplication\u2014breaking down the numbers, distributive property, standard algorithm, vertical multiplication, decimal multiplication\u2014result in 1870. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 22 and 85 is 1870.\n\nJust to be absolutely sure, maybe I can use a calculator. Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but I can estimate. 22 is roughly 20, and 85 is 85. 20 \u00d7 85 = 1700. Since 22 is 2 more than 20, and 22 is 110% of 20. So 1700 \u00d7 1.1 = 1700 + 170 = 1870. That works too. Alternatively, 85 is 85/100 of 100. 22 \u00d7 85 = 22 \u00d7 (85/100) \u00d7 100 = 22 \u00d7 0.85 \u00d7 100. 22 \u00d7 0.85 is 1870 / 100 = 18.7. So 18.7 \u00d7 100 = 1870. That's another way to think about it.\n\nAlternatively, using fractions. 22 \u00d7 85 = (22/1) \u00d7 (85/1) = (22 \u00d7 85)/1. But that's just the original problem. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, prime factorization. 22 is 2 \u00d7 11, and 85 is 5 \u00d7 17. So multiplying them together: 2 \u00d7 11 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 17. Let's compute that step by step. 2 \u00d7 11 = 22. 22 \u00d7 5 = 110. 110 \u00d7 17. Let's compute 110 \u00d7 17. 100 \u00d7 17 = 1700, 10 \u00d7 17 = 170. So 1700 + 170 = 1870. Again, same result.\n\nWow, so even breaking it down into prime factors and multiplying gives the same answer. That's thorough.\n\nSo, after checking multiple methods\u2014breaking into smaller numbers, distributive property, standard multiplication, decimal multiplication, even prime factorization\u2014each time the result is 1870. Therefore, I can be certain that the product of 22 and 85 is indeed 1870.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 22 and 85 is \\boxed{1870}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 22 and 85, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers**:\n   - \\(22 \\times 85\\) can be split into \\(20 \\times 85 + 2 \\times 85\\).\n   - \\(20 \\times 85 = 1700\\) and \\(2 \\times 85 = 170\\).\n   - Adding these results: \\(1700 + 170 = 1870\\).\n\n2. **Distributive property**:\n   - \\(22 \\times 85 = (22 \\times 80) + (22 \\times 5)\\).\n   - \\(22 \\times 80 = 1760\\) and \\(22 \\times 5 = 110\\).\n   - Adding these results: \\(1760 + 110 = 1870\\).\n\n3. **Standard multiplication**:\n   - Multiplying 22 by 85 as per the standard algorithm:\n     - \\(22 \\times 80 = 1760\\)\n     - \\(22 \\times 5 = 110\\)\n     - Adding these results: \\(1760 + 110 = 1870\\).\n\n4. **Vertical multiplication**:\n   - \n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{r}\n      \\phantom{00}22 \\\\\n     \\times \\phantom{00}85 \\\\\n     \\hline\n      \\phantom{00}110 \\quad (\\text{22} \\times 5) \\\\\n     \\hline\n     \\phantom{00}1760 \\quad (\\text{22} \\times 80) \\\\\n     \\hline\n     \\phantom{00}1870 \\quad (\\text{sum of the partial products}) \\\\\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Prime factorization**:\n   - \\(22 = 2 \\times 11\\) and \\(85 = 5 \\times 17\\).\n   - Multiplying these factors: \\(2 \\times 11 \\times 5 \\times 17 = 1870\\).\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 22 and 85 is \\(\\boxed{1870}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
