      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 60 and 31. Hmm, let's see. Product means multiplication, right? So I have to multiply 60 by 31. Alright, let me think about how to do this step by step.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break it down. 60 times 30 is a common multiplication fact. Let me check that. 60 times 30... Well, 60 times 3 is 180, so 60 times 30 should be 1800. Yeah, that makes sense. Then there's one more 60 left because 31 is 30 plus 1. So I need to add 60 to 1800. Let me do that. 1800 plus 60 is 1860. Wait, is that right? Let me verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's line them up:\n\n  60\nx 31\n----\n\nStarting with the ones place. 1 times 60 is 60. Then move to the tens place. 3 times 60 is 180. But since it's the tens place, I have to add a zero. So 1800. Then add the two results together: 60 plus 1800. That's 1860. Okay, same answer as before. Good.\n\nWait, another way to check might be to use distributive property. 60 times 31 is the same as 60 times (30 + 1). Which is 60*30 + 60*1. We already know 60*30 is 1800, and 60*1 is 60. Adding those gives 1800 + 60 = 1860. Yep, same result.\n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can do it the standard multiplication way step by step. Let's write it out:\n\n     60\n   x 31\n   ----\n     60  (60 x 1)\n   180   (60 x 3, shifted one position to the left)\n   ----\n    1860\n\nSo adding 60 and 1800 gives 1860. That's consistent. I think that's solid. Let me check with another method. Maybe using addition multiple times. 60 added 31 times. But that's tedious. Alternatively, 31 times 60. Let's see, 31 times 60 is the same as 31 times 6 times 10. 31 x 6 is 186, then times 10 is 1860. Oh, that's another way. So 31 x 6 = 186, then times 10 is 1860. That works too.\n\nSo all methods point to the same answer. I think 1860 is correct. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 60 x 30 is 1800, 60 x 1 is 60, so 1800 + 60 = 1860. Yep, that's right. No mistakes here. I think that's the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 60 and 31 can be calculated by breaking down the multiplication into simpler parts. \n\nFirst, multiply 60 by 30:  \n\\[\n60 \\times 30 = 1800\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply 60 by 1:  \n\\[\n60 \\times 1 = 60\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:  \n\\[\n1800 + 60 = 1860\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 60 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{1860}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
