ernatively, maybe I can use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, I need to rely on my math. But all the methods I used give me 8800, so I think that's solid. So the product of 100 and 88 is 8800. I can't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. So I think that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 100 and 88 is \\boxed{8800}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 100 and 88, we can break down the multiplication step by step:\n\n1. Recognize that multiplying by 100 is the same as multiplying by 100.\n2. Alternatively, break down 100 into 10 \u00d7 10 and multiply 88 by 10 first, then by 10 again:\n   - \\( 88 \\times 10 = 880 \\)\n   - \\( 880 \\times 10 = 8800 \\)\n3. Use the distributive property to verify:\n   - \\( 100 \\times 88 = 100 \\times (80 + 8) \\)\n   - \\( 100 \\times 80 = 8000 \\)\n   - \\( 100 \\times 8 = 800 \\)\n   - Adding these results: \\( 8000 + 800 = 8800 \\)\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 100 and 88 is \\(\\boxed{8800}\\).",
