's tens place), which is 32, plus the carried over 1 makes 33. So, the partial products are 6 and 33. So, writing them down, it's 33 and 6, but since we're dealing with two-digit numbers, we need to shift the second one by one place to the left. So, 33 and 60. Then, adding 33 + 60 gives 93. Wait, that's different from before. Wait, no, maybe I confused the places.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by two digits, you first multiply by the units digit, then multiply by the tens digit, and add the results. So, 18 times 4 (units digit) is 72, and 18 times 40 (tens digit) is 720. Then, adding 72 + 720 gives 792. Oh! So earlier, when I did the standard multiplication, I must have made a mistake in the steps. Let me try again.\n\nStandard multiplication:\n\n     18\n   x 44\n   ----\n     72  (4 x 44)\n   720   (18 x 40, shifted one position to the left)\n   ----\n   792\n\nYes, that's correct. So the product is 792. So my first calculation was right, and the second time I messed up the standard multiplication steps. So the correct answer is 792.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using addition. 44 added 18 times. Let's see, 44 + 44 is 88. 88 + 44 is 132. 132 + 44 is 176. 176 + 44 is 220. 220 + 44 is 264. 264 + 44 is 308. 308 + 44 is 352. 352 + 44 is 396. 396 + 44 is 440. Wait, that's 10 times. But 18 times 44 is 792, not 440. Hmm, so adding 44 ten times gives 440, but that's only 10 times. So 18 times would be 440 + 44 + 44 = 440 + 88 = 528. Wait, that's not right either. Maybe this method isn't the best.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use distributive property differently. 44 is 40 + 4, so 40*18 + 4*18. As I did before, which gives 720 + 72 = 792. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method. Let's think of 18 as 20 - 2. So, 44*(20 - 2) = 44*20 - 44*2. 44*20 is 880, and 44*2 is 88. So, 880 - 88 = 792. That works too. So, that's another way to get the same answer.\n\nSo, all methods point to 792. Therefore, the product of 44 and 18 is 792. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just verify with a calculator to be sure. 44 multiplied by 18. Let's compute 44*10=440, 44*8=352. 440+352=792. Yes, that's correct. So, the answer is 792.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 44 and 18 is \\boxed{792}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 44 and 18, we can break down 44 into 40 and 4, then multiply each by 18 and add the results.\n\n1. Calculate \\( 40 \\times 18 \\):\n   \\[\n   40 \\times 18 = 720\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\( 4 \\times 18 \\):\n   \\[\n   4 \\times 18 = 72\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   720 + 72 = 792\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the distributive property:\n\\[\n44 \\times 18 = (40 + 4) \\times 18 = 40 \\times 18 + 4 \\times 18 = 720 + 72 = 792\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  18 \\\\\n\\times 44 \\\\\n\\hline\n  72 \\quad \\text{(4 \\times 44)} \\\\\n720 \\quad \\text{(18 \\times 40, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n  792 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 44 and 18 is \\(\\boxed{792}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
