4 (units place of 44) = 32\n1 (units place of 81) x 4 (tens place of 44) = 4\n1 (units place of 81) x 4 (units place of 44) = 4\n\nThen, sum the products diagonally:\n\n32\n32\n4\n4\nAdding them up: 32 + 32 = 64, 64 + 4 = 68, 68 + 4 = 72. Wait, that's 72. But that's not matching. Wait, maybe I did the lattice method wrong. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the lattice method for multiplying two numbers is to create a grid where each row represents a digit of the first number and each column represents a digit of the second number. Then, multiply each pair of digits and sum them up. So for 81 x 44:\n\nFirst number: 8 (tens) and 1 (units)\nSecond number: 4 (tens) and 4 (units)\n\nSo the grid would be:\n\n    8   1\n  x 4   4\n  ------\n    32  32 (8x4 and 1x4)\n  32   32 (8x4 and 1x4 shifted left)\n  ------\n  3564\n\nWait, no. Wait, the lattice method is to multiply each digit of the first number by each digit of the second number and then add all the products. So:\n\n8 (from 81) x 4 (from 44) = 32\n8 x 4 = 32\n1 x 4 = 4\n1 x 4 = 4\n\nThen, sum all these products: 32 + 32 + 4 + 4 = 72. Wait, that's 72. But that's not 3564. That can't be right. So maybe I messed up the lattice method. Wait, no, the lattice method is supposed to be for multiplying two numbers and then taking the area of the rectangle. Wait, maybe I need to visualize it differently.\n\nWait, perhaps the lattice method for two-digit numbers is:\n\nFor 81 x 44:\n\nFirst, split 81 into 8 and 1, 44 into 4 and 4.\n\nThen, create a grid where the rows are 8 and 1, columns are 4 and 4.\n\nMultiply the rows and columns:\n\n8*4 = 32\n\n8*4 = 32\n\n1*4 = 4\n\n1*4 = 4\n\nThen, sum the products: 32 + 32 + 4 + 4 = 72. But that's not 3564. So clearly, I'm applying the lattice method incorrectly. Maybe the lattice method is for something else. Maybe for multiplying a two-digit number by a two-digit number, you need to do it as two separate multiplications: 80*44 and 1*44, then add them. Which is what I did earlier. So perh